{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-09-17T09:53:36.624Z","sessionId":"55398c64-7c65-40db-9b68-886111a86d7f","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>a6c1b1fd82bc11b97</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>toolu_01YFbdHmuZD4DfqH5tfgA1NR</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>/private/tmp/claude-502/-Users-c-sikawats-Documents-GitHub-Personal-faucet-stream/2e42fef2-d7da-4f6e-97b1-064e451b2570/tasks/a6c1b1fd82bc11b97.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Dedupe reserved names + CDC spec\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>The disk is completely full — I can no longer run any shell command (even `df` fails to write its output file). Stopping here as instructed rather than deleting anything.\n\n## Report\n\nAll three findings are implemented; changes are in the working tree (they appear **staged** — something in the environment ran `git add` on them, not me).\n\n### 1. #654 M12 — `__faucet_ovw` staging suffix (done)\n- **`crates/core/src/idempotency.rs`**: added two `pub const`s next to `COMMIT_TOKEN_TABLE`, same doc style — `OVERWRITE_STAGING_SUFFIX = \"__faucet_ovw\"` (doc calls out that the lifecycle *drops* whatever carries it, so a real table named `&lt;x&gt;__faucet_ovw` is destroyed) and `OVERWRITE_STAGING_OLD_SUFFIX = \"__faucet_ovw_old\"` (the MySQL mid-swap rename target; documented as extending the base so a prefix-matching sweep catches both).\n- **Not re-exported from `crates/core/src/lib.rs`** — the sibling `COMMIT_TOKEN_*` / `ICEBERG_*` consts aren't either; every consumer uses `faucet_core::idempotency::…`. This also kept me out of `lib.rs`, which another agent is editing concurrently.\n- Consumed in all 5 sinks (`postgres:staging_table_name`, `sqlite:staging_table`, `mysql:staging_table_name` + `old_table_name`, `mssql:new()` staging-quoted + `staging_literal`, `mongodb:staging_collection`). Produced strings are byte-identical (`format!(\"{}{}\", name, SUFFIX)`); the existing integration tests still pin the literals (`users__faucet_ovw`, `t__faucet_ovw_old`, …), which is the byte-identity guard. bigquery untouched.\n- Test added: `idempotency::tests::overwrite_staging_suffixes_are_pinned` — exact values, the two differ, and OLD starts with the base.\n\n### 2. #654 M17 — `CdcUnwrapSpec` / `\"__op\"` triplication (done)\n- **`crates/core/src/stage.rs`**: new **ungated** `pub const CDC_DEFAULT_MARKER_FIELD: &amp;str = \"__op\"` (ungated deliberately — `faucet-conformance` depends on `faucet-core` *without* `transform-cdc-unwrap`, and the feature-isolation CI builds it alone). `cdc_default_marker_field()` now returns it.\n- **`cli/src/transforms.rs`**: deleted `CdcUnwrapConfig` and its 7 default fns (~55 lines); the registry entry now does `schema::&lt;faucet_core::CdcUnwrapSpec&gt;()` and `decode::&lt;faucet_core::CdcUnwrapSpec&gt;()` straight into `TransformStage::CdcUnwrap`. Core's derives are sufficient (`Deserialize + JsonSchema`, no `deny_unknown_fields` on either copy, field-for-field identical), so nothing was lost.\n- **`crates/conformance/src/doubles.rs`**: `DELETE_MARKER_FIELD` now aliases `faucet_core::stage::CDC_DEFAULT_MARKER_FIELD`.\n- **Drifted doc fixed by correcting the doc, not the code.** `delete_ops`'s doc claimed \"covers all three CDC vocabularies\" while the default is `[\"d\", \"delete\"]`. I verified the emitters: `postgres-cdc` emits `\"delete\"`; `mysql-cdc`, `mongodb-cdc`, and `mssql-cdc` all emit `\"d\"` — so the 2-element default already covers **all four** built-in CDC sources and is correct. Changing the *set* would alter delete-vs-insert classification for live pipelines, which is exactly the silent-corruption class this finding is about; the field is user-overridable, so the doc was the defect.\n- Tests added: `stage::tests::cdc_default_marker_field_is_pinned`, `cdc_unwrap_spec_default_marker_uses_the_constant` (both `Default` and the serde path), `cdc_unwrap_default_delete_ops_match_the_doc`; `transforms::tests::cdc_unwrap_defaults_come_from_core` (partial config → CLI takes core's defaults for every unset field); `doubles::tests::delete_marker_tracks_the_cdc_unwrap_default`.\n- **`schemas/faucet.schema.json` is unaffected** — I checked: it contains no transform config schemas (`CdcUnwrapConfig` appears nowhere in it). The per-transform schema is generated on demand by `faucet schema transform cdc_unwrap`, and its `title`/descriptions **will** change (`CdcUnwrapConfig` → `CdcUnwrapSpec`, core's field doc wording). No test asserts on that text. Nothing to regenerate.\n\n### 3. #654 L27 — `__unchanged_toast__` (done)\n- **`crates/source/postgres-cdc/src/stream.rs`**: new `pub const UNCHANGED_TOAST_FIELD: &amp;str = \"__unchanged_toast__\"` with a doc that states it is *part of the emitted record* (an ordinary key an `auto_map` sink sees as a column, a drift policy as an addition), referenced at the insertion site.\n- **Placed in the postgres-cdc crate, not core** — TOAST is a Postgres storage concept; core knows nothing about it, and per PRINCIPLES §1 a vendor-specific key doesn't belong in the generic engine. It's publicly reachable as `faucet_source_postgres_cdc::stream::UNCHANGED_TOAST_FIELD` (the module is `pub`), so I did **not** touch `crates/source/postgres-cdc/src/lib.rs` (outside my file list).\n- **`crates/source/postgres-cdc/README.md`** — expanded the Unchanged-TOAST bullet: what the column means, that it is a reserved key inside the record (not out-of-band metadata), that it survives `cdc_unwrap` into the flat row, how to drop it (`drop` transform with `fields: [\"__unchanged_toast__\"]`), and the exported const name. *This file is outside the list you gave me, but finding 3 explicitly required the README; it's in the same crate, so no cross-agent overlap.*\n- Test added: `stream::tests::unchanged_toast_field_name_is_pinned`.\n\n### Verification status\n- ✅ `cargo test -p faucet-core --all-features` — pass (1170 lib tests, incl. all 4 new ones), plus integration + doctests.\n- ✅ `cargo test -p faucet-conformance --all-features` — pass (53 + 2 doctests, incl. the new one).\n- ✅ `cargo test -p faucet-sink-{postgres,sqlite,mysql,mssql,mongodb} --all-features --lib` — all compile and pass.\n- ✅ `cargo test -p faucet-cli --features transforms,transform-cdc-unwrap --lib cdc_unwrap` — 4/4 pass incl. the new test.\n- ✅ `cargo fmt` on all 9 crates (it reflowed one `assert_eq!` in my new CLI test; already applied).\n- ❌ **`cargo clippy` not completed** — two independent blockers, neither mine:\n  1. `faucet-core` clippy fails on **another agent's** code: `crates/core/src/write_mode.rs:637` → `error: manual implementation of .is_multiple_of()` (in the new `sql_literal`). Whoever owns `write_mode.rs` needs `…count().is_multiple_of(2)`.\n  2. Then the **disk filled** (460Gi volume at 100%, 912Mi free, `target/` = 66G). Builds now fail with `failed to create directory target/debug/.fingerprint/…` and shell commands themselves fail with ENOSPC. Not deleting anything per your instruction.\n- ❌ **`faucet-source-postgres-cdc` tests not run.** `crates/source/postgres-cdc/src/replication.rs` is mid-edit by a parallel agent and does not compile (`pg_err` signature mismatch: `PgWireError` vs `sqlx::Error` at `replication.rs:552`; later `cannot find function slot_in_use`), plus 3 failing `replication::tests::*` around slot-active message classification — all in their file, not mine. My `stream.rs` change is a const + two references + one test; it needs a re-run once their edit lands and disk is freed.\n\n### Deliberately not done\n- No re-export of the new consts from `crates/core/src/lib.rs` or `crates/source/postgres-cdc/src/lib.rs` (rationale above; also avoids clobbering a file another agent is editing — I briefly added one there and reverted it, `git diff` on `lib.rs` now shows only the other agent's `sql_literal` line).\n- Did not touch `crates/sink/bigquery/**` (yours), nor the bigquery/sqlite/mongodb/mysql/postgres **integration tests** or the sink READMEs that still spell the suffix literally — those literals are the intentional byte-identity pins, and the READMEs are user-facing docs describing the on-the-wire name.\n- Did not change the `delete_ops` default set (justified above).</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>235926</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>99</tool_uses><duration_ms>2301318</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
