{"parentUuid":"231046ed-a8ef-48bb-bf4e-f6aa5af1576d","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011Cf99feGjw7Uuvn2Mp3Qy2","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Slow going (instrumented rebuilds). Let me update the PR metadata while they run:"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":652,"cache_read_input_tokens":297075,"output_tokens":3215,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":652,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":3215,"cache_read_input_tokens":297075,"cache_creation_input_tokens":652,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":652},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011Cf99fdsRvCH1u3u2Pguxx","type":"assistant","uuid":"a699380d-1d09-4f3b-a320-98fe8dc186ba","timestamp":"2026-09-17T14:13:51.381Z","effort":"high","perTurnEffort":null,"session_id":"55398c64-7c65-40db-9b68-886111a86d7f","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/c-sikawats/Documents/GitHub/Personal/faucet-stream","sessionId":"55398c64-7c65-40db-9b68-886111a86d7f","version":"2.1.267","gitBranch":"fix/audit-correctness-paths","slug":"linear-noodling-leaf"}
{"parentUuid":"a699380d-1d09-4f3b-a320-98fe8dc186ba","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011Cf99feGjw7Uuvn2Mp3Qy2","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01BAg9TBT1fBBMQ84Kt4Dghg","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /Users/c-sikawats/Documents/GitHub/Personal/faucet-stream\nS=/private/tmp/claude-502/-Users-c-sikawats-Documents-GitHub-Personal-faucet-stream/55398c64-7c65-40db-9b68-886111a86d7f/scratchpad\ngh pr view 656 --json body --jq .body > $S/pr_body.md\ncat >> $S/pr_body.md <<'BODY'\n\n---\n\n# Phase 2 — the rest of the non-breaking audit findings\n\nEverything above was the set I was certain of on first pass. This second phase closes every remaining #654 finding that does **not** change a user's YAML or break an API. What stays behind in #654 is only the deliberately-deferred set (H9, H10, M20–M24) — those all break something and need a decision, not a unilateral fix.\n\n## Classification by type, not by text (PRINCIPLES §6)\n\nFour more places decided control flow by matching a rendered string. Each is now keyed off a variant or a code, classified at the one place the typed error still exists.\n\n| | Was | Now |\n|---|---|---|\n| **scheduler** (#654 M2) | Circuit-open detection matched the literal prefix `\"Circuit open after\"` against the *flattened* per-invocation error string. Rewording `CircuitOpen`'s `#[error(...)]` — or localizing it — would silently stop the cooldown from ever applying, so the next cron tick re-fires at a destination that just tripped its breaker. | `InvocationOutcome` carries a typed `InvocationErrorKind`, set by one pure `executor::classify_error`. `classify` reads the variant. Tested from both sides: a reworded message still earns the cooldown; a *lookalike* message in another variant does not. |\n| **run history** (#654 M1) | Every one of ~60 `sqlx` call sites did `HistoryError::Backend(e.to_string())`, discarding the typed error at the boundary. Nothing downstream could tell a pool timeout from a syntax error, so the startup connect **degraded straight to the in-memory store** on any blip — stranding a cluster instance off the shared DB (#235). | One `sql::classify_backend_error` decides transience from the `sqlx::Error` variant, then its SQLSTATE (`57P03`, `53300`, `40001`, `40P01`, class `08`) or SQLite *extended* result code (masked to its low byte, so `SQLITE_BUSY_SNAPSHOT` = 517 classifies like `BUSY` = 5 — matching the text `database is locked` missed every extended form). `connect_with_retry` now retries a transient failure before degrading. Unknown stays retriable, deliberately: a few bounded seconds beats stranding an instance. |\n| **graphql** (#654 M3) | `batch_size = 0` + five prose probes (`\"non-null\"`, `\"must not be null\"`, `\"required\"`, …) flipped a server error into `FaucetError::Config`. A reworded validator turned a config error into a server error and vice-versa. | The variant comes from the typed `extensions.code` alone (`BAD_REQUEST`, `BAD_USER_INPUT`, `GRAPHQL_PARSE_FAILED`, `GRAPHQL_VALIDATION_FAILED` — spec-standard and populated by every major server). The page-size diagnostic survives as an appended *hint*, so a miss degrades the message instead of changing how the error is handled. |\n| **parquet** (#654 L28) | `msg.contains(\"type\")` decided whether an arrow decoder error was type drift. That substring matches nearly every arrow message, so an allocation failure was reported to the user as schema drift. | `matches!` on `JsonError \\| SchemaError \\| CastError \\| ParseError`. A `MemoryError` now keeps its own message. |\n\n## One jitter implementation, five call sites (#654 M13)\n\n`retry::apply_jitter` became `pub` in phase 1 *because* connectors had hand-rolled copies. Now they consume it:\n\n- **kinesis source** — the old per-shard jitter was a **fixed** 0–25% fraction hashed from the shard id. That does not break a herd: after one shared throttle every shard still retried inside the same narrow window, in the same relative order, on every attempt.\n- **kinesis / sqs sinks** — a partially-failed `PutRecords` can leave 500 entries to retry; unjittered they all re-fire in the same instant and re-create the throttle.\n- **grpc reconnect** — also drops a mutable `backoff` variable in favour of deriving the exponential from the attempt counter, so there is no second piece of state to keep in step.\n- **websocket reconnect** (#654 M6) — this one was a **bug, not just duplication**: `reconnect_backoff` was a *fixed* wait, and `max_reconnect_attempts` defaults to unlimited with no ceiling field anywhere in the config. A dead endpoint was therefore redialled once per `reconnect_backoff`, forever. Now exponential-with-jitter off that base, capped. Documented in the README and the field doc — the key's meaning changed from \"the wait\" to \"the wait before the *first* retry\".\n\nEach site asserts the delay lands in core's documented `[0.5, 1.5)` band, never collapses to zero, and — the point of the finding — that 32 identical calls do not all return the same delay.\n\n## Reserved names get exactly one definition (PRINCIPLES §5)\n\nMagic strings that two or more places had to agree on, where a rename in one reached none of the others:\n\n- `CDC_DEFAULT_MARKER_FIELD` (`\"__op\"`, #654 M17) — the CLI kept its **own** mirror of `CdcUnwrapSpec` with its own defaults. A drift between the two would have stamped a marker the sink's `delete_marker` no longer looks for, turning every CDC delete into an insert. The CLI now decodes straight into core's spec.\n- `OVERWRITE_STAGING_SUFFIX` / `_OLD_SUFFIX` (#654 M12) — the overwrite lifecycle **drops** whatever carries the suffix, so the reserved set needs to be enumerable by a collision check or an orphan sweep.\n- `UNCHANGED_TOAST_FIELD` (#654 L27) — part of the emitted record, so downstream configs are written against the literal.\n\n## `faucet_core::sql_literal` is public, with its contract stated (#654 M14)\n\nConnectors had invented a third escaping convention each, so a fix to one reached none. It is now public with the ANSI rule spelled out — and the doc states the condition under which a dialect **must not** use it. `faucet_common_clickhouse::sql_literal` is that case, and its doc now explains why: ClickHouse accepts C-style escapes, so the ANSI \"double the quotes\" rule leaves a trailing-backslash breakout (`'foo\\'` never closes) and mis-reads a pre-escaped `\\'`. There is a property test asserting every backslash in an escaped body starts a two-character pair, so the closing quote can never be consumed.\n\n## No panics on network input (#654 L25)\n\nThe compact XML decoder (in both `source-xml` and `source-rest`) `.expect()`ed its frame stack four times. `quick_xml`'s `check_end_names` happens to reject an unbalanced `</x>` first — but that is a dependency default, not an invariant this code owns. Now a typed `FaucetError::Source` naming the malformed document.\n\n## `deny_unknown_fields` where it was still missing (#654 M18 tail)\n\nkafka, kinesis, pubsub, sqs and databricks config blocks accepted and ignored unknown keys. Also a substantial pass on the kafka sink's field docs, which were the thinnest in the repo — including honest documentation of `headers_path`, which is parsed and extracted but **never attached to the produced message**. That is a real behavioural bug rather than a principles violation, so it is filed as #657 rather than fixed here.\n\n## Overruled: the S3/GCS/Azure `verify` unification\n\nOne workstream unified the three object-store sources' `verify_length` / `verify_checksum` onto a shared `#[serde(flatten)]` spec. That **removes two public fields** from each config — a major break, which the API-evolution policy says is never mine to take unilaterally. Reverted to plain fields, and the shared type deleted rather than left exported-but-unused. The actual benefit is kept: all three now carry identical canonical docs for both switches (including the two skip conditions and why Azure rejects `verify_checksum` outright), and all three validate at config load. `cargo semver-checks` reports \"no semver update required\".\n\n## Filed rather than folded in\n\n- **#657** — kafka `headers_path` never reaches the broker.\n- **#658** — `NodeReport.error` is an `Option<String>`, so the M2 typed fix stops at the matrix path; a topology node that trips the breaker still gets no cooldown.\n\nAddresses #654 (partially — the deferred set stays open).\nBODY\ngh pr edit 656 \\\n  --title \"fix: replace every message-grep classification with a typed one, and close the core retry gate\" \\\n  --body-file $S/pr_body.md 2>&1 | tail -2","description":"Update the PR title and body"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":652,"cache_read_input_tokens":297075,"output_tokens":3215,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":652,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":3215,"cache_read_input_tokens":297075,"cache_creation_input_tokens":652,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":652},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01BAg9TBT1fBBMQ84Kt4Dghg":{"command":"cd /Users/c-sikawats/Documents/GitHub/Personal/faucet-stream\nS=/private/tmp/claude-502/-Users-c-sikawats-Documents-GitHub-Personal-faucet-stream/55398c64-7c65-40db-9b68-886111a86d7f/scratchpad\ngh pr view 656 --json body --jq .body > $S/pr_body.md\ncat >> $S/pr_body.md <<'BODY'\n\n---\n\n# Phase 2 — the rest of the non-breaking audit findings\n\nEverything above was the set I was certain of on first pass. This second phase closes every remaining #654 finding that does **not** change a user's YAML or break an API. What stays behind in #654 is only the deliberately-deferred set (H9, H10, M20–M24) — those all break something and need a decision, not a unilateral fix.\n\n## Classification by type, not by text (PRINCIPLES §6)\n\nFour more places decided control flow by matching a rendered string. Each is now keyed off a variant or a code, classified at the one place the typed error still exists.\n\n| | Was | Now |\n|---|---|---|\n| **scheduler** (#654 M2) | Circuit-open detection matched the literal prefix `\"Circuit open after\"` against the *flattened* per-invocation error string. Rewording `CircuitOpen`'s `#[error(...)]` — or localizing it — would silently stop the cooldown from ever applying, so the next cron tick re-fires at a destination that just tripped its breaker. | `InvocationOutcome` carries a typed `InvocationErrorKind`, set by one pure `executor::classify_error`. `classify` reads the variant. Tested from both sides: a reworded message still earns the cooldown; a *lookalike* message in another variant does not. |\n| **run history** (#654 M1) | Every one of ~60 `sqlx` call sites did `HistoryError::Backend(e.to_string())`, discarding the typed error at the boundary. Nothing downstream could tell a pool timeout from a syntax error, so the startup connect **degraded straight to the in-memory store** on any blip — stranding a cluster instance off the shared DB (#235). | One `sql::classify_backend_error` decides transience from the `sqlx::Error` variant, then its SQLSTATE (`57P03`, `53300`, `40001`, `40P01`, class `08`) or SQLite *extended* result code (masked to its low byte, so `SQLITE_BUSY_SNAPSHOT` = 517 classifies like `BUSY` = 5 — matching the text `database is locked` missed every extended form). `connect_with_retry` now retries a transient failure before degrading. Unknown stays retriable, deliberately: a few bounded seconds beats stranding an instance. |\n| **graphql** (#654 M3) | `batch_size = 0` + five prose probes (`\"non-null\"`, `\"must not be null\"`, `\"required\"`, …) flipped a server error into `FaucetError::Config`. A reworded validator turned a config error into a server error and vice-versa. | The variant comes from the typed `extensions.code` alone (`BAD_REQUEST`, `BAD_USER_INPUT`, `GRAPHQL_PARSE_FAILED`, `GRAPHQL_VALIDATION_FAILED` — spec-standard and populated by every major server). The page-size diagnostic survives as an appended *hint*, so a miss degrades the message instead of changing how the error is handled. |\n| **parquet** (#654 L28) | `msg.contains(\"type\")` decided whether an arrow decoder error was type drift. That substring matches nearly every arrow message, so an allocation failure was reported to the user as schema drift. | `matches!` on `JsonError \\| SchemaError \\| CastError \\| ParseError`. A `MemoryError` now keeps its own message. |\n\n## One jitter implementation, five call sites (#654 M13)\n\n`retry::apply_jitter` became `pub` in phase 1 *because* connectors had hand-rolled copies. Now they consume it:\n\n- **kinesis source** — the old per-shard jitter was a **fixed** 0–25% fraction hashed from the shard id. That does not break a herd: after one shared throttle every shard still retried inside the same narrow window, in the same relative order, on every attempt.\n- **kinesis / sqs sinks** — a partially-failed `PutRecords` can leave 500 entries to retry; unjittered they all re-fire in the same instant and re-create the throttle.\n- **grpc reconnect** — also drops a mutable `backoff` variable in favour of deriving the exponential from the attempt counter, so there is no second piece of state to keep in step.\n- **websocket reconnect** (#654 M6) — this one was a **bug, not just duplication**: `reconnect_backoff` was a *fixed* wait, and `max_reconnect_attempts` defaults to unlimited with no ceiling field anywhere in the config. A dead endpoint was therefore redialled once per `reconnect_backoff`, forever. Now exponential-with-jitter off that base, capped. Documented in the README and the field doc — the key's meaning changed from \"the wait\" to \"the wait before the *first* retry\".\n\nEach site asserts the delay lands in core's documented `[0.5, 1.5)` band, never collapses to zero, and — the point of the finding — that 32 identical calls do not all return the same delay.\n\n## Reserved names get exactly one definition (PRINCIPLES §5)\n\nMagic strings that two or more places had to agree on, where a rename in one reached none of the others:\n\n- `CDC_DEFAULT_MARKER_FIELD` (`\"__op\"`, #654 M17) — the CLI kept its **own** mirror of `CdcUnwrapSpec` with its own defaults. A drift between the two would have stamped a marker the sink's `delete_marker` no longer looks for, turning every CDC delete into an insert. The CLI now decodes straight into core's spec.\n- `OVERWRITE_STAGING_SUFFIX` / `_OLD_SUFFIX` (#654 M12) — the overwrite lifecycle **drops** whatever carries the suffix, so the reserved set needs to be enumerable by a collision check or an orphan sweep.\n- `UNCHANGED_TOAST_FIELD` (#654 L27) — part of the emitted record, so downstream configs are written against the literal.\n\n## `faucet_core::sql_literal` is public, with its contract stated (#654 M14)\n\nConnectors had invented a third escaping convention each, so a fix to one reached none. It is now public with the ANSI rule spelled out — and the doc states the condition under which a dialect **must not** use it. `faucet_common_clickhouse::sql_literal` is that case, and its doc now explains why: ClickHouse accepts C-style escapes, so the ANSI \"double the quotes\" rule leaves a trailing-backslash breakout (`'foo\\'` never closes) and mis-reads a pre-escaped `\\'`. There is a property test asserting every backslash in an escaped body starts a two-character pair, so the closing quote can never be consumed.\n\n## No panics on network input (#654 L25)\n\nThe compact XML decoder (in both `source-xml` and `source-rest`) `.expect()`ed its frame stack four times. `quick_xml`'s `check_end_names` happens to reject an unbalanced `</x>` first — but that is a dependency default, not an invariant this code owns. Now a typed `FaucetError::Source` naming the malformed document.\n\n## `deny_unknown_fields` where it was still missing (#654 M18 tail)\n\nkafka, kinesis, pubsub, sqs and databricks config blocks accepted and ignored unknown keys. Also a substantial pass on the kafka sink's field docs, which were the thinnest in the repo — including honest documentation of `headers_path`, which is parsed and extracted but **never attached to the produced message**. That is a real behavioural bug rather than a principles violation, so it is filed as #657 rather than fixed here.\n\n## Overruled: the S3/GCS/Azure `verify` unification\n\nOne workstream unified the three object-store sources' `verify_length` / `verify_checksum` onto a shared `#[serde(flatten)]` spec. That **removes two public fields** from each config — a major break, which the API-evolution policy says is never mine to take unilaterally. Reverted to plain fields, and the shared type deleted rather than left exported-but-unused. The actual benefit is kept: all three now carry identical canonical docs for both switches (including the two skip conditions and why Azure rejects `verify_checksum` outright), and all three validate at config load. `cargo semver-checks` reports \"no semver update required\".\n\n## Filed rather than folded in\n\n- **#657** — kafka `headers_path` never reaches the broker.\n- **#658** — `NodeReport.error` is an `Option<String>`, so the M2 typed fix stops at the matrix path; a topology node that trips the breaker still gets no cooldown.\n\nAddresses #654 (partially — the deferred set stays open).\nBODY\ngh pr edit 656 \\\n  --title \"fix: replace every message-grep classification with a typed one, and close the core retry gate\" \\\n  --body-file $S/pr_body.md 2>&1 | tail -2","description":"Update the PR title and body"}},