{"parentUuid":"21352362-666b-4114-93e8-2170d0df316a","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"7f18b2ae-41bd-4c93-ada1-530037b42da9","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_015rH4xZDUgHqSpkE46HxgnT","type":"tool_result","content":"{\"labels\":[\"enhancement\",\"tier-1\"],\"n\":651,\"state\":\"OPEN\",\"title\":\"Integration-test program: categorized end-to-end coverage of every shipped guarantee\"}\n================ BODY ================\n## Summary\n\nfaucet's value proposition is *\"the data can be trusted\"* — but today that promise is verified mostly by **unit tests of pure logic plus a handful of per-connector wiremock/testcontainers suites**. There is no systematic integration-test program that exercises the promises we actually make (no loss, no duplication, no silent corruption, atomic full refresh, resumable incremental, exactly-once) **end-to-end, across connectors, under failure**. This issue defines that program: a categorized, prioritized integration-test matrix such that anything we ship is backed by tests that run the real engine against real (containerized) or simulated systems.\n\n## Motivation\n\nTies directly to the Primary Goal (reliability is priority #1) and PRINCIPLES.md §2 (\"correctness lives once — in the engine\") and §7. The engine centralizes correctness — which means **a single engine bug corrupts every pipeline**, and unit tests on pure functions cannot catch integration-order bugs (bookmark persisted before sink confirm, flush skipped on cancel, staging table swapped on a failed run). The recent D365/Salesforce parity work found exactly these classes of issue live (BigQuery overwrite not replacing schema; nullable temporal columns losing their type) — both were **integration** gaps, invisible to unit tests.\n\n## Current state (what exists — do not duplicate)\n\n- Per-connector wiremock suites (`crates/source/rest/tests/*`), testcontainers suites for DBs/Kafka (Docker-gated), `faucet-conformance` harness per connector.\n- CLI: `cli/tests/cli_end_to_end.rs` (examples validate + limited runs), `serve_openapi.rs` (route↔spec sync), `serve_history_sqlite.rs`, `history_e2e.rs`, `schema_config.rs` (committed-schema gate), `plugin_registry.rs`, `wasm_transform.rs`.\n- Coverage job runs the instrumented full suite; Kafka/DB container suites run in CI but are not required checks.\n\n## The categories\n\n### Category A — Correctness-mechanism suites (tier-1, the crux)\nEnd-to-end tests of the engine guarantees, each built as: real `Pipeline::run`/`run_stream` with a real or scripted source/sink, **failure injected at the exact boundary the guarantee protects**.\n\n1. **Bookmark ordering / crash-safety**: sink that fails after N batches → assert the persisted bookmark never advances past the last *confirmed* write; resume re-reads from the correct position; no rows lost, duplicates only within the at-least-once window.\n2. **Exactly-once, both mechanisms**: (a) atomic-watermark: kill the pipeline between sink-commit and state-put; restart; assert the commit-token path skips/re-anchors with zero duplicates (SQL sinks via sqlite/postgres container; Kafka transactional via redpanda container). (b) keyed-upsert: replay a full window and assert idempotence.\n3. **Overwrite atomicity**: kill mid-load (`write_mode: overwrite`) on postgres/sqlite/mysql/mongo/bigquery-emulator → destination table untouched; successful run swaps completely (schema replaced too — regression for the BigQuery WRITE_TRUNCATE bug found 2026-09).\n4. **Scoped cleanup**: run with `complete_for` where the source stops returning rows → destination rows deleted; cancelled/partial run → **no deletion**; `max_keys` overflow → run fails, nothing deleted.\n5. **DLQ routing**: per-row failures land in the DLQ with correct envelopes across `write_batch_partial` sinks; `on_batch_error: dlq_all` vs `propagate`; DLQ replay (`faucet dlq replay`) re-runs quarantined rows through the full transform/quality path.\n6. **Schema drift policies**: each of warn/evolve/ignore/quarantine/fail against a live postgres + sqlite container with an actually-drifting source; `evolve` produces the expected DDL; quarantine requires DLQ.\n7. **Quality/contract/masking ordering**: masking applied before quality/contract/DLQ (PII never leaks into DLQ envelopes); contract `fail` writes nothing from the breaching page.\n8. **Cooperative cancel = flush**: cancel mid-run with a buffered sink (parquet/S3-localstack) → file finalized (footer written / multipart completed), bookmark reflects flushed data only.\n\n### Category B — CDC end-to-end (tier-1)\nReal replication against containers, because CDC is where ordering bugs silently corrupt.\n1. **postgres-cdc**: pgoutput against a real PG container — transactional writes; assert partial transactions never emitted; kill mid-stream → resume from durable bookmark with no WAL loss (`confirmed_flush_lsn` only advances after persistence); slot lifecycle (permanent/temporary/drop).\n2. **mysql-cdc / mongodb-cdc**: same shape (binlog file/pos bookmarks; resumeToken).\n3. **`faucet replicate` snapshot→CDC handoff**: write rows *during* the snapshot → assert zero gap, zero dup after handoff (position captured pre-snapshot, overlap idempotent via upsert); interrupt each phase and resume via the durable phase marker.\n\n### Category C — Protocol-simulation suites (tier-1→2, wiremock)\nThe REST/OData/Bulk surface, scripted against mock servers with hostile behaviors:\n1. Every pagination style × termination edge (malformed Link header, repeating cursor, 204 mid-stream, zero-record page, `total` lies).\n2. Auth: token expiry mid-run → single-flight refresh (concurrent partitions must not stampede); 401-invalidate-retry; shared `auth:` catalog reuse across matrix rows.\n3. `async_job` (Bulk) lifecycle: submit→poll→locator pages; job failure states; locator loss mid-fetch; incremental bookmark capture semantics (#630).\n4. OData: `$metadata` typed fan-out; `@odata.nextLink` preserving `$filter`; key-range partition ranges collectively exhaustive/non-overlapping (mock server asserts every requested range and row union); `@odata.*` control-field stripping.\n5. `discovery:` recipe: list→describe→emit against a mock provider; template rendering (`${name_snake}` etc.); describe failures degrade per-dataset, not per-run.\n6. Native byte-passthrough (#633): CSV bytes reach the sink un-materialized (recording sink asserts no Value-path writes — extend the existing pipeline unit tests to a full CLI run).\n\n### Category D — Control-plane & runtime lifecycle (tier-2)\nSpawn the real `faucet serve` binary (existing spawn-harness pattern; keep the `CARGO_LLVM_COV` skip-guard):\n1. Run lifecycle: submit→running→completed/failed/cancelled; timeout enforcement flushes; graceful-shutdown drain; `RUN_FLUSH_GRACE` honored.\n2. Idempotency keys: replay vs 409 on fingerprint mismatch; claim takeover after expiry.\n3. Cluster mode A: two instances, shared PG history — pending claim, cross-instance cancel, lease-expiry orphan recovery (kill -9 one instance), no double-execution. Mode B: sharded source across instances; shard reclaim on instance death.\n4. `schedule`: tick fires, overlap policies (skip/queue/forbid), `max_consecutive_failures` exit, timezone/DST boundary tick (use a fixed tz fixture date).\n5. Triggers: webhook debounce/dedupe-header; object-arrival cursor no-reprocessing; queue-depth edge-trigger semantics.\n6. Templates: register→launch→trigger→rollback→deprecate; clustered secret-param refusal.\n7. `backfill`: unit tiling, resume from marker, live bookmark untouched.\n8. RBAC/audit: viewer/operator/admin per-route matrix; every denial audited.\n\n### Category E — Fault injection (tier-2, toxiproxy or equivalent)\n1. Connection drop mid-page (source) and mid-upload (sink) → resilience policy retries within class; run either completes or fails *cleanly* (bookmark consistent) — never hangs (regression for the observed indefinite-retry hang, 2026-09).\n2. 429/5xx storms → backoff honors `Retry-After`; circuit breaker opens after N failed pages and surfaces `CircuitOpen`.\n3. Poison row: one bad row with `poison.action: dlq` → run continues; with `fail` → clean abort.\n4. Slow sink (latency injection) → backpressure keeps memory O(batch) (assert peak RSS via the #631 reporting).\n\n### Category F — Fidelity round-trip matrix (tier-2→3)\nFor each source↔sink family, a golden round-trip: seed typed data (ints incl. i64 edges, floats, decimals, timestamps/tz, dates, bools, nulls, unicode, nested JSON, empty strings) → pipeline → assert **byte/type-exact** landing. Priority pairs first: postgres→bigquery(emulator), mysql→postgres, csv/parquet→sqlite, kafka→jsonl, mongo→postgres. Shared fixture + assertion helper in `faucet-conformance` so each pair is ~20 lines.\n\n### Category G — Compatibility gates (tier-2)\n1. **State-format compatibility**: bookmarks/EO envelopes/phase markers written by release N must load under N+1 (commit golden state fixtures; test reads them).\n2. **Config compatibility**: every `cli/examples/*.yaml` validates AND `--dry-run`s per release; committed JSON-schema gate (exists) stays.\n3. **Serve API**: openapi sync test (exists) + response-shape snapshots for `/v1/runs`, `/v1/catalog/*`.\n\n## CI strategy\n\n- **Per-PR (required)**: Categories A, C, and G — wiremock + sqlite/postgres containers only (fast, no flaky cloud deps). Target < 15 min added.\n- **Per-PR (non-required but reported)**: B, D on Linux runners (Docker).\n- **Nightly**: E (toxiproxy), F full matrix, cluster suites — with an auto-filed issue on failure.\n- All container suites keep the existing Docker-gating pattern; LocalStack readiness-poll per the known gotcha; spawned-binary tests carry the `CARGO_LLVM_COV` skip-guard.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] Each category has a tracked checklist (this issue's task list) and lands as one or more PRs; every guarantee listed in Category A/B has at least one failing-first test (demonstrate it fails when the guarantee is deliberately broken).\n- [ ] A `docs/book` \"reliability testing\" page documents what is covered and how to run each tier locally.\n- [ ] CI wiring per the strategy above; per-PR added time < 15 min.\n- [ ] The conformance harness gains the shared fixture/assertion helpers (Category F) so new connectors inherit round-trip tests by default.\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- Performance regression thresholds (separate concern; benchmarks exist under `scripts/`).\n- Cloud-vendor live-account tests (BigQuery/Snowflake real endpoints) — emulators/mocks only in CI.\n\n## References\n\n- PRINCIPLES.md §2/§7/§9 · `.claude/rules/connectors.md` (exactly-once, DLQ, cleanup, drift semantics) · existing suites listed above · #630/#633 (async_job, byte-passthrough) · the 2026-09 BigQuery overwrite-schema and nullable-temporal findings (both integration-class bugs).","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"5d29ee04-6797-4ee8-bb0b-a1106594f232","timestamp":"2026-09-17T23:19:10.290Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"{\"labels\":[\"enhancement\",\"tier-1\"],\"n\":651,\"state\":\"OPEN\",\"title\":\"Integration-test program: categorized end-to-end coverage of every shipped guarantee\"}\n================ BODY ================\n## Summary\n\nfaucet's value proposition is *\"the data can be trusted\"* — but today that promise is verified mostly by **unit tests of pure logic plus a handful of per-connector wiremock/testcontainers suites**. There is no systematic integration-test program that exercises the promises we actually make (no loss, no duplication, no silent corruption, atomic full refresh, resumable incremental, exactly-once) **end-to-end, across connectors, under failure**. This issue defines that program: a categorized, prioritized integration-test matrix such that anything we ship is backed by tests that run the real engine against real (containerized) or simulated systems.\n\n## Motivation\n\nTies directly to the Primary Goal (reliability is priority #1) and PRINCIPLES.md §2 (\"correctness lives once — in the engine\") and §7. The engine centralizes correctness — which means **a single engine bug corrupts every pipeline**, and unit tests on pure functions cannot catch integration-order bugs (bookmark persisted before sink confirm, flush skipped on cancel, staging table swapped on a failed run). The recent D365/Salesforce parity work found exactly these classes of issue live (BigQuery overwrite not replacing schema; nullable temporal columns losing their type) — both were **integration** gaps, invisible to unit tests.\n\n## Current state (what exists — do not duplicate)\n\n- Per-connector wiremock suites (`crates/source/rest/tests/*`), testcontainers suites for DBs/Kafka (Docker-gated), `faucet-conformance` harness per connector.\n- CLI: `cli/tests/cli_end_to_end.rs` (examples validate + limited runs), `serve_openapi.rs` (route↔spec sync), `serve_history_sqlite.rs`, `history_e2e.rs`, `schema_config.rs` (committed-schema gate), `plugin_registry.rs`, `wasm_transform.rs`.\n- Coverage job runs the instrumented full suite; Kafka/DB container suites run in CI but are not required checks.\n\n## The categories\n\n### Category A — Correctness-mechanism suites (tier-1, the crux)\nEnd-to-end tests of the engine guarantees, each built as: real `Pipeline::run`/`run_stream` with a real or scripted source/sink, **failure injected at the exact boundary the guarantee protects**.\n\n1. **Bookmark ordering / crash-safety**: sink that fails after N batches → assert the persisted bookmark never advances past the last *confirmed* write; resume re-reads from the correct position; no rows lost, duplicates only within the at-least-once window.\n2. **Exactly-once, both mechanisms**: (a) atomic-watermark: kill the pipeline between sink-commit and state-put; restart; assert the commit-token path skips/re-anchors with zero duplicates (SQL sinks via sqlite/postgres container; Kafka transactional via redpanda container). (b) keyed-upsert: replay a full window and assert idempotence.\n3. **Overwrite atomicity**: kill mid-load (`write_mode: overwrite`) on postgres/sqlite/mysql/mongo/bigquery-emulator → destination table untouched; successful run swaps completely (schema replaced too — regression for the BigQuery WRITE_TRUNCATE bug found 2026-09).\n4. **Scoped cleanup**: run with `complete_for` where the source stops returning rows → destination rows deleted; cancelled/partial run → **no deletion**; `max_keys` overflow → run fails, nothing deleted.\n5. **DLQ routing**: per-row failures land in the DLQ with correct envelopes across `write_batch_partial` sinks; `on_batch_error: dlq_all` vs `propagate`; DLQ replay (`faucet dlq replay`) re-runs quarantined rows through the full transform/quality path.\n6. **Schema drift policies**: each of warn/evolve/ignore/quarantine/fail against a live postgres + sqlite container with an actually-drifting source; `evolve` produces the expected DDL; quarantine requires DLQ.\n7. **Quality/contract/masking ordering**: masking applied before quality/contract/DLQ (PII never leaks into DLQ envelopes); contract `fail` writes nothing from the breaching page.\n8. **Cooperative cancel = flush**: cancel mid-run with a buffered sink (parquet/S3-localstack) → file finalized (footer written / multipart completed), bookmark reflects flushed data only.\n\n### Category B — CDC end-to-end (tier-1)\nReal replication against containers, because CDC is where ordering bugs silently corrupt.\n1. **postgres-cdc**: pgoutput against a real PG container — transactional writes; assert partial transactions never emitted; kill mid-stream → resume from durable bookmark with no WAL loss (`confirmed_flush_lsn` only advances after persistence); slot lifecycle (permanent/temporary/drop).\n2. **mysql-cdc / mongodb-cdc**: same shape (binlog file/pos bookmarks; resumeToken).\n3. **`faucet replicate` snapshot→CDC handoff**: write rows *during* the snapshot → assert zero gap, zero dup after handoff (position captured pre-snapshot, overlap idempotent via upsert); interrupt each phase and resume via the durable phase marker.\n\n### Category C — Protocol-simulation suites (tier-1→2, wiremock)\nThe REST/OData/Bulk surface, scripted against mock servers with hostile behaviors:\n1. Every pagination style × termination edge (malformed Link header, repeating cursor, 204 mid-stream, zero-record page, `total` lies).\n2. Auth: token expiry mid-run → single-flight refresh (concurrent partitions must not stampede); 401-invalidate-retry; shared `auth:` catalog reuse across matrix rows.\n3. `async_job` (Bulk) lifecycle: submit→poll→locator pages; job failure states; locator loss mid-fetch; incremental bookmark capture semantics (#630).\n4. OData: `$metadata` typed fan-out; `@odata.nextLink` preserving `$filter`; key-range partition ranges collectively exhaustive/non-overlapping (mock server asserts every requested range and row union); `@odata.*` control-field stripping.\n5. `discovery:` recipe: list→describe→emit against a mock provider; template rendering (`${name_snake}` etc.); describe failures degrade per-dataset, not per-run.\n6. Native byte-passthrough (#633): CSV bytes reach the sink un-materialized (recording sink asserts no Value-path writes — extend the existing pipeline unit tests to a full CLI run).\n\n### Category D — Control-plane & runtime lifecycle (tier-2)\nSpawn the real `faucet serve` binary (existing spawn-harness pattern; keep the `CARGO_LLVM_COV` skip-guard):\n1. Run lifecycle: submit→running→completed/failed/cancelled; timeout enforcement flushes; graceful-shutdown drain; `RUN_FLUSH_GRACE` honored.\n2. Idempotency keys: replay vs 409 on fingerprint mismatch; claim takeover after expiry.\n3. Cluster mode A: two instances, shared PG history — pending claim, cross-instance cancel, lease-expiry orphan recovery (kill -9 one instance), no double-execution. Mode B: sharded source across instances; shard reclaim on instance death.\n4. `schedule`: tick fires, overlap policies (skip/queue/forbid), `max_consecutive_failures` exit, timezone/DST boundary tick (use a fixed tz fixture date).\n5. Triggers: webhook debounce/dedupe-header; object-arrival cursor no-reprocessing; queue-depth edge-trigger semantics.\n6. Templates: register→launch→trigger→rollback→deprecate; clustered secret-param refusal.\n7. `backfill`: unit tiling, resume from marker, live bookmark untouched.\n8. RBAC/audit: viewer/operator/admin per-route matrix; every denial audited.\n\n### Category E — Fault injection (tier-2, toxiproxy or equivalent)\n1. Connection drop mid-page (source) and mid-upload (sink) → resilience policy retries within class; run either completes or fails *cleanly* (bookmark consistent) — never hangs (regression for the observed indefinite-retry hang, 2026-09).\n2. 429/5xx storms → backoff honors `Retry-After`; circuit breaker opens after N failed pages and surfaces `CircuitOpen`.\n3. Poison row: one bad row with `poison.action: dlq` → run continues; with `fail` → clean abort.\n4. Slow sink (latency injection) → backpressure keeps memory O(batch) (assert peak RSS via the #631 reporting).\n\n### Category F — Fidelity round-trip matrix (tier-2→3)\nFor each source↔sink family, a golden round-trip: seed typed data (ints incl. i64 edges, floats, decimals, timestamps/tz, dates, bools, nulls, unicode, nested JSON, empty strings) → pipeline → assert **byte/type-exact** landing. Priority pairs first: postgres→bigquery(emulator), mysql→postgres, csv/parquet→sqlite, kafka→jsonl, mongo→postgres. Shared fixture + assertion helper in `faucet-conformance` so each pair is ~20 lines.\n\n### Category G — Compatibility gates (tier-2)\n1. **State-format compatibility**: bookmarks/EO envelopes/phase markers written by release N must load under N+1 (commit golden state fixtures; test reads them).\n2. **Config compatibility**: every `cli/examples/*.yaml` validates AND `--dry-run`s per release; committed JSON-schema gate (exists) stays.\n3. **Serve API**: openapi sync test (exists) + response-shape snapshots for `/v1/runs`, `/v1/catalog/*`.\n\n## CI strategy\n\n- **Per-PR (required)**: Categories A, C, and G — wiremock + sqlite/postgres containers only (fast, no flaky cloud deps). Target < 15 min added.\n- **Per-PR (non-required but reported)**: B, D on Linux runners (Docker).\n- **Nightly**: E (toxiproxy), F full matrix, cluster suites — with an auto-filed issue on failure.\n- All container suites keep the existing Docker-gating pattern; LocalStack readiness-poll per the known gotcha; spawned-binary tests carry the `CARGO_LLVM_COV` skip-guard.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] Each category has a tracked checklist (this issue's task list) and lands as one or more PRs; every guarantee listed in Category A/B has at least one failing-first test (demonstrate it fails when the guarantee is deliberately broken).\n- [ ] A `docs/book` \"reliability testing\" page documents what is covered and how to run each tier locally.\n- [ ] CI wiring per the strategy above; per-PR added time < 15 min.\n- [ ] The conformance harness gains the shared fixture/assertion helpers (Category F) so new connectors inherit round-trip tests by default.\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- Performance regression thresholds (separate concern; benchmarks exist under `scripts/`).\n- Cloud-vendor live-account tests (BigQuery/Snowflake real endpoints) — emulators/mocks only in CI.\n\n## References\n\n- PRINCIPLES.md §2/§7/§9 · `.claude/rules/connectors.md` (exactly-once, DLQ, cleanup, drift semantics) · existing suites listed above · #630/#633 (async_job, byte-passthrough) · the 2026-09 BigQuery overwrite-schema and nullable-temporal findings (both integration-class bugs).","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"21352362-666b-4114-93e8-2170d0df316a","session_id":"55398c64-7c65-40db-9b68-886111a86d7f","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/c-sikawats/Documents/GitHub/Personal/faucet-stream","sessionId":"55398c64-7c65-