{"parentUuid":"49a14a76-19c4-47ed-b59b-e662251b579e","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"edited_text_file","filename":"/Users/c-sikawats/Documents/GitHub/Personal/faucet-stream/crates/conformance/src/fidelity.rs","snippet":"1\t//! Shared round-trip fidelity corpus (#651 Category F).\n2\t//!\n3\t//! The promise faucet makes is not \"the rows arrive\" but \"the rows arrive\n4\t//! *unchanged*\". Those are different claims, and the second is the one that\n5\t//! breaks quietly: an `i64` past 2^53 that went through a JSON number, a\n6\t//! timestamp that lost its offset, a `\"\"` that became `NULL`, a `0.0` that came\n7\t//! back `-0.0`. None of that fails a run — it lands, and the report is green.\n8\t//!\n9\t//! So every source↔sink pair wants the same test: seed a corpus that contains\n10\t//! exactly the values known to break, move it through a real pipeline, and\n11\t//! assert the landed rows are *equal* to what went in. Writing that per pair\n12\t//! invites each one to quietly pick an easier corpus. This module owns the\n13\t//! corpus and the comparison instead, so a pair's test is the pipeline wiring\n14\t//! and nothing else.\n15\t//!\n16\t//! ```no_run\n17\t//! # use faucet_conformance::fidelity;\n18\t//! # async fn example(records: Vec<faucet_core::Value>) {\n19\t//! // Landed rows come back from the destination however that sink reads back.\n20\t//! let landed: Vec<faucet_core::Value> = read_back_somehow().await;\n21\t//! fidelity::assert_round_trip(&fidelity::corpus(), &landed, fidelity::Tolerance::exact());\n22\t//! # }\n23\t//! # async fn read_back_somehow() -> Vec<faucet_core::Value> { vec![] }\n24\t//! ```\n25\t\n26\tuse std::collections::BTreeSet;\n27\t\n28\tuse faucet_core::{Value, json};\n29\t\n30\t/// Key every corpus record carries, so a destination that reorders rows can\n31\t/// still be compared. Named with the reserved `__` prefix so it cannot collide\n32\t/// with a realistic column.\n33\tpub const ROW_KEY: &str = \"__fidelity_id\";\n34\t\n35\t/// What a destination is *allowed* to change, because its type system genuinely\n36\t/// cannot represent the input.\n37\t///\n38\t/// Every allowance is a real, documented limitation of some destination — not a\n39\t/// convenience. A pair that needs one is saying \"this column cannot survive\n40\t/// here\", which is worth stating explicitly in that pair's test rather than\n41\t/// hiding inside a loose comparison.\n42\t#[derive(Debug, Clone, Default)]\n43\tpub struct Tolerance {\n44\t    /// Columns to skip entirely (the destination has no representation at all).\n45\t    skip: BTreeSet<String>,\n46\t    /// Compare floats within this relative epsilon instead of bit-exactly.\n47\t    /// `None` means bit-exact.\n48\t    float_epsilon: Option<f64>,\n49\t    /// Treat a landed `null` as equal to an input empty string — for the\n50\t    /// destinations (Oracle-family, some warehouse loaders) that cannot store a\n51\t    /// zero-length string distinctly from NULL.\n52\t    empty_string_is_null: bool,\n53\t    /// Accept a landed number where a string was sent, and vice versa, as long\n54\t    /// as the *rendered* values agree — for schemaless destinations that coerce\n55\t    /// on read.\n56\t    lenient_scalar_kind: bool,\n57\t}\n58\t\n59\timpl Tolerance {\n60\t    /// No allowances: every value must land byte- and type-identical. Start\n61\t    /// here, and widen only with a comment naming the destination limitation.\n62\t    pub fn exact() -> Self {\n63\t        Self::default()\n64\t    }\n65\t\n66\t    /// Skip a column this destination cannot represent.\n67\t    pub fn skipping(mut self, column: &str) -> Self {\n68\t        self.skip.insert(column.to_string());\n69\t        self\n70\t    }\n71\t\n72\t    /// Compare floats within a relative epsilon.\n73\t    pub fn float_epsilon(mut self, eps: f64) -> Self {\n74\t        self.float_epsilon = Some(eps);\n75\t        self\n76\t    }\n77\t\n78\t    /// Accept `null` where an empty string was sent.\n79\t    pub fn empty_string_becomes_null(mut self) -> Self {\n80\t        self.empty_string_is_null = true;\n81\t        self\n82\t    }\n83\t\n84\t    /// Accept a scalar whose JSON kind changed but whose rendering did not.\n85\t    pub fn lenient_scalar_kind(mut self) -> Self {\n86\t        self.lenient_scalar_kind = true;\n87\t        self\n88\t    }\n89\t}\n90\t\n91\t/// The corpus: one record per hazard class, each keyed by [`ROW_KEY`].\n92\t///\n93\t/// Every field here exists because it has broken a real pipeline somewhere.\n94\t/// Add to it when a fidelity bug is found — that is what stops the same class\n95\t/// of bug reappearing in the next connector.\n96\tpub fn corpus() -> Vec<Value> {\n97\t    vec![\n98\t        json!({\n99\t            ROW_KEY: \"integers\",\n100\t            // Past 2^53, so anything that round-trips through an f64 (a JS\n101\t            // number, an unguarded JSON parse) corrupts these silently.\n102\t            \"i64_max\": i64::MAX,\n103\t            \"i64_min\": i64::MIN,\n104\t            \"beyond_f64_exact\": 9_007_199_254_740_993i64,\n105\t            \"zero\": 0,\n106\t            \"negative\": -1,\n107\t        }),\n108\t        json!({\n109\t            ROW_KEY: \"floats\",\n110\t            \"simple\": 1.5,\n111\t            // Not representable in binary: a destination that re-renders via a\n112\t            // short decimal form changes it.\n113\t            \"repeating\": 0.1,\n114\t            \"very_small\": 1e-300,\n115\t            \"very_large\": 1e300,\n116\t            // Distinct from 0.0 by sign bit; many round-trips lose it.\n117\t            \"negative_zero\": -0.0,\n118\t        }),\n119\t        json!({\n120\t            ROW_KEY: \"strings\",\n121\t            \"empty\": \"\",\n122\t            \"unicode\": \"héllo wörld — 日本語 🚰\",\n123\t            // The three characters that break naive SQL literal escaping.\n124\t            \"quote\": \"it's\",\n125\t            \"backslash\": \"back\\\\slash\",\n126\t            \"double_quote\": \"say \\\"hi\\\"\",\n127\t            \"newline\": \"line1\\nline2\",\n128\t            \"tab\": \"a\\tb\",\n129\t            // Leading/trailing whitespace a CHAR column would pad or trim.\n130\t            \"padded\": \"  spaced  \",\n131\t        }),\n132\t        json!({\n133\t            ROW_KEY: \"temporal\",\n134\t            \"date\": \"2026-02-29\",\n135\t            \"timestamp_utc\": \"2026-09-18T12:34:56Z\",\n136\t            \"timestamp_offset\": \"2026-09-18T12:34:56+05:30\",\n137\t            \"timestamp_micros\": \"2026-09-18T12:34:56.123456Z\",\n138\t            // Pre-epoch, which unsigned or unix-seconds encodings mangle.\n139\t            \"before_epoch\": \"1969-07-20T20:17:40Z\",\n140\t        }),\n141\t        json!({\n142\t            ROW_KEY: \"booleans_and_null\",\n143\t            \"true_val\": true,\n144\t            \"false_val\": false,\n145\t            \"null_val\": Value::Null,\n146\t        }),\n147\t        json!({\n148\t            ROW_KEY: \"nested\",\n149\t            \"object\": { \"a\": 1, \"b\": { \"c\": [1, 2, 3] } },\n150\t            \"array\": [1, \"two\", null, true],\n151\t            \"empty_object\": {},\n152\t            \"empty_array\": [],\n153\t        }),\n154\t    ]\n155\t}\n156\t\n157\t/// The corpus as a flat single record, for destinations that take one wide row\n158\t/// rather than one row per hazard class. Field names are prefixed with the\n159\t/// class so they stay unique.\n160\tpub fn flat_corpus() -> Value {\n161\t    let mut out = serde_json::Map::new();\n162\t    out.insert(ROW_KEY.to_string(), json!(\"flat\"));\n163\t    for rec in corpus() {\n164\t        let Some(obj) = rec.as_object() else { continue };\n165\t        let Some(class) = obj.get(ROW_KEY).and_then(|v| v.as_str()) else {\n166\t            continue;\n167\t        };\n168\t        for (k, v) in obj {\n169\t            if k == ROW_KEY {\n170\t                continue;\n171\t            }\n172\t            out.insert(format!(\"{class}_{k}\"), v.clone());\n173\t        }\n174\t    }\n175\t    Value::Object(out)\n176\t}\n177\t\n178\t/// One field that did not survive the trip.\n179\t#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq)]\n180\tpub struct Mismatch {\n181\t    /// [`ROW_KEY`] of the record.\n182\t    pub row: String,\n183\t    /// Field name, or `\"<row>\"` when the whole record is missing or extra.\n184\t    pub field: String,\n185\t    /// What was sent.\n186\t    pub sent: Value,\n187\t    /// What landed (`Null` when absent).\n188\t    pub landed: Value,\n189\t}\n190\t\n191\timpl std::fmt::Display for Mismatch {\n192\t    fn fmt(&self, f: &mut std::fmt::Formatter<'_>) -> std::fmt::Result {\n193\t        write!(\n194\t            f,\n195\t            \"row {:?} field {:?}: sent {} but landed {}\",\n196\t            self.row, self.field, self.sent, self.landed\n197\t        )\n198\t    }\n199\t}\n200\t\n\n... [283 lines truncated] ..."},"type":"attachment","uuid":"b0c11264-e44c-42e1-a0a5-c47240869a57","timestamp":"2026-09-17T23:24:44.931Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\nNote: /Users/c-sikawats/Documents/GitHub/Personal/faucet-stream/crates/conformance/src/fidelity.rs changed on disk since you last read it. That's usually deliberate, so take it as the current state rather than reverting it; if the change looks wrong, say so rather than undoing it yourself — otherwise no need to call it out. Here are the relevant changes (shown with line numbers):\n1\t//! Shared round-trip fidelity corpus (#651 Category F).\n2\t//!\n3\t//! The promise faucet makes is not \"the rows arrive\" but \"the rows arrive\n4\t//! *unchanged*\". Those are different claims, and the second is the one that\n5\t//! breaks quietly: an `i64` past 2^53 that went through a JSON number, a\n6\t//! timestamp that lost its offset, a `\"\"` that became `NULL`, a `0.0` that came\n7\t//! back `-0.0`. None of that fails a run — it lands, and the report is green.\n8\t//!\n9\t//! So every source↔sink pair w