{"parentUuid":"27e8975f-1976-4146-9b98-cbfeaa577e74","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"hook_success","hookName":"PostToolUse:Bash","toolUseID":"toolu_01AuqaeCXFMJTGcvkQT5XTbV","hookEvent":"PostToolUse","content":"","stdout":"{\"terminalSequence\":\"\\u001b]777;notify;warp://cli-agent;{\\\"v\\\":1,\\\"agent\\\":\\\"claude\\\",\\\"event\\\":\\\"tool_complete\\\",\\\"session_id\\\":\\\"55398c64-7c65-40db-9b68-886111a86d7f\\\",\\\"cwd\\\":\\\"/Users/c-sikawats/Documents/GitHub/Personal/faucet-stream\\\",\\\"project\\\":\\\"faucet-stream\\\",\\\"tool_name\\\":\\\"Bash\\\"}\\u0007\"}\n","stderr":"/Users/c-sikawats/.claude/plugins/cache/claude-code-warp/warp/2.2.0/scripts/emit-terminal-sequence.sh: line 82: /dev/tty: Device not configured\n","exitCode":0,"command":"${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/scripts/on-post-tool-use.sh","durationMs":308},"type":"attachment","uuid":"f5756515-bf67-4ce2-a73f-0f06bd2a5257","timestamp":"2026-09-17T23:39:21.777Z","session_id":"55398c64-7c65-40db-9b68-886111a86d7f","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/c-sikawats/Documents/GitHub/Personal/faucet-stream","sessionId":"55398c64-7c65-40db-9b68-886111a86d7f","version":"2.1.267","gitBranch":"test/reliability-program","slug":"linear-noodling-leaf"}
{"parentUuid":"f5756515-bf67-4ce2-a73f-0f06bd2a5257","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"edited_text_file","filename":"/Users/c-sikawats/Documents/GitHub/Personal/faucet-stream/crates/conformance/tests/compat_state_format.rs","snippet":"1\t//! #651 Category G1 — state-format compatibility.\n2\t//!\n3\t//! A resumable pipeline's bookmark outlives the process that wrote it, so every\n4\t//! upgrade crosses the state-format boundary. If release N+1 cannot read what N\n5\t//! wrote, the pipeline resumes from the wrong place — re-delivering everything\n6\t//! or losing everything — and **neither failure is loud**. The run is green\n7\t//! either way.\n8\t//!\n9\t//! A round-trip test cannot catch this, because it serializes and deserializes\n10\t//! with the same code: both halves move together and the test stays green\n11\t//! through a breaking change. So these tests read **frozen files** written by a\n12\t//! released version (`tests/fixtures/state/`) and assert the current code still\n13\t//! understands them.\n14\t//!\n15\t//! If one of these fails, the fixture is not what is wrong.\n16\t\n17\tuse faucet_core::Value;\n18\tuse faucet_core::idempotency::{\n19\t    format_token, format_token_with_bookmark, parse_token, parse_token_parts, unwrap_state,\n20\t    wrap_state,\n21\t};\n22\t\n23\t/// Load a frozen fixture. A missing file is a failure, not a skip — a silently\n24\t/// skipped compatibility gate is the same as not having one.\n25\tfn fixture(name: &str) -> Value {\n26\t    let path = std::path::Path::new(env!(\"CARGO_MANIFEST_DIR\"))\n27\t        .join(\"tests/fixtures/state\")\n28\t        .join(name);\n29\t    let text = std::fs::read_to_string(&path).unwrap_or_else(|e| {\n30\t        panic!(\n31\t            \"golden fixture {} is missing or unreadable: {e}\",\n32\t            path.display()\n33\t        )\n34\t    });\n35\t    serde_json::from_str(&text)\n36\t        .unwrap_or_else(|e| panic!(\"golden fixture {} is not valid JSON: {e}\", path.display()))\n37\t}\n38\t\n39\t#[test]\n40\tfn an_exactly_once_envelope_written_by_a_release_still_unwraps() {\n41\t    let stored = fixture(\"eo-envelope-v1.json\");\n42\t    let (bookmark, seq) = unwrap_state(&stored);\n43\t\n44\t    assert_eq!(\n45\t        seq, 42,\n46\t        \"the committed sequence must survive the upgrade — it is what decides which \\\n47\t         pages are already durable\"\n48\t    );\n49\t    let bookmark = bookmark.expect(\"the envelope carries a bookmark\");\n50\t    assert_eq!(bookmark[\"page\"], 7);\n51\t    assert_eq!(bookmark[\"updated_at\"], \"2026-05-01T00:00:00Z\");\n52\t}\n53\t\n54\t#[test]\n55\tfn an_envelope_with_no_bookmark_yet_unwraps_to_none_not_to_a_null_bookmark() {\n56\t    // The distinction matters: `Some(Value::Null)` would be handed to\n57\t    // `apply_start_bookmark` as if it were a real position.\n58\t    let (bookmark, seq) = unwrap_state(&fixture(\"eo-envelope-null-bookmark-v1.json\"));\n59\t    assert_eq!(seq, 0);\n60\t    assert!(\n61\t        bookmark.is_none(),\n62\t        \"a null bookmark must unwrap to None, got {bookmark:?}\"\n63\t    );\n64\t}\n65\t\n66\t#[test]\n67\tfn a_bare_at_least_once_bookmark_is_still_read_as_a_bookmark() {\n68\t    // Every pipeline that predates exactly-once wrote a bare bookmark. Reading\n69\t    // it as an envelope (and so as `None`) would restart it from scratch.\n70\t    let stored = fixture(\"bare-bookmark-v1.json\");\n71\t    let (bookmark, seq) = unwrap_state(&stored);\n72\t    assert_eq!(seq, 0, \"a bare bookmark carries no sequence\");\n73\t    let bookmark = bookmark.expect(\"a bare bookmark must be returned as the bookmark\");\n74\t    assert_eq!(bookmark[\"updated_at\"], \"2026-05-01T00:00:00Z\");\n75\t    assert_eq!(\n76\t        bookmark[\"id\"].as_i64(),\n77\t        Some(9_007_199_254_740_993),\n78\t        \"an id past 2^53 must survive exactly — losing a digit silently re-reads or \\\n79\t         skips rows\"\n80\t    );\n81\t}\n82\t\n83\t#[test]\n84\tfn a_scalar_bookmark_survives_unchanged() {\n85\t    let stored = fixture(\"bare-bookmark-scalar-v1.json\");\n86\t    let (bookmark, seq) = unwrap_state(&stored);\n87\t    assert_eq!(seq, 0);\n88\t    assert_eq!(bookmark, Some(Value::String(\"2026-05-01T00:00:00Z\".into())));\n89\t}\n90\t\n91\t#[test]\n92\tfn commit_tokens_written_by_a_release_still_parse() {\n93\t    let tokens = fixture(\"commit-tokens-v1.json\");\n94\t\n95\t    assert_eq!(\n96\t        parse_token(tokens[\"bare\"].as_str().expect(\"string\")),\n97\t        Some(42),\n98\t        \"a stored bare token must still yield its sequence\"\n99\t    );\n100\t    assert_eq!(\n101\t        parse_token(tokens[\"zero\"].as_str().expect(\"string\")),\n102\t        Some(0)\n103\t    );\n104\t    assert_eq!(\n105\t        parse_token(tokens[\"max\"].as_str().expect(\"string\")),\n106\t        Some(u64::MAX),\n107\t        \"the widest sequence a u64 can hold must round-trip\"\n108\t    );\n109\t\n110\t    let (seq, bookmark) = parse_token_parts(tokens[\"with_bookmark\"].as_str().expect(\"string\"))\n111\t        .expect(\"a bookmark-carrying token must parse\");\n112\t    assert_eq!(seq, 42);\n113\t    assert_eq!(\n114\t        bookmark.expect(\"the token carries a bookmark\")[\"page\"],\n115\t        7,\n116\t        \"the embedded bookmark is what re-anchors the source on resume\"\n117\t    );\n118\t}\n119\t\n120\t#[test]\n121\tfn the_token_encoding_is_still_fixed_width_and_lexicographically_ordered() {\n122\t    // Sinks compare tokens as strings (a SQL `MAX(token)`, a compacted Kafka\n123\t    // key), so the encoding must sort the same way the numbers do. A change to\n124\t    // the width would silently invert that ordering for existing data.\n125\t    let stored = fixture(\"commit-tokens-v1.json\");\n126\t    let width = stored[\"bare\"].as_str().expect(\"string\").len();\n127\t    assert_eq!(\n128\t        format_token(42).len(),\n129\t        width,\n130\t        \"the token width changed — every stored token now sorts against the new ones \\\n131\t         incorrectly\"\n132\t    );\n133\t    assert_eq!(format_token(42), stored[\"bare\"].as_str().expect(\"string\"));\n134\t    assert_eq!(format_token(0), stored[\"zero\"].as_str().expect(\"string\"));\n135\t    assert_eq!(\n136\t        format_token(u64::MAX),\n137\t        stored[\"max\"].as_str().expect(\"string\")\n138\t    );\n139\t\n140\t    // The ordering property itself.\n141\t    let mut rendered: Vec<String> = [0u64, 1, 9, 10, 99, 100, u64::MAX]\n142\t        .iter()\n143\t        .map(|n| format_token(*n))\n144\t        .collect();\n145\t    let numeric_order = rendered.clone();\n146\t    rendered.sort();\n147\t    assert_eq!(\n148\t        rendered, numeric_order,\n149\t        \"fixed-width tokens must sort lexicographically in numeric order\"\n150\t    );\n151\t}\n152\t\n153\t#[test]\n154\tfn the_bookmark_carrying_token_separator_is_unchanged() {\n155\t    let stored = fixture(\"commit-tokens-v1.json\");\n156\t    let expected = stored[\"with_bookmark\"].as_str().expect(\"string\");\n157\t    let rendered = format_token_with_bookmark(42, Some(&serde_json::json!({ \"page\": 7 })));\n158\t    assert_eq!(\n159\t        rendered, expected,\n160\t        \"the separator or the embedded-bookmark rendering changed, so tokens written by \\\n161\t         a release can no longer be parsed back into a resume position\"\n162\t    );\n163\t}\n164\t\n165\t#[test]\n166\tfn garbage_is_rejected_rather_than_silently_read_as_zero() {\n167\t    // Reading an unparseable token as sequence 0 would make the engine believe\n168\t    // nothing had committed and replay the entire stream.\n169\t    for bad in [\n170\t        \"\",\n171\t        \"abc\",\n172\t        \"#\",\n173\t        \"#{}\",\n174\t        \"not-a-number#{\\\"page\\\":1}\",\n175\t        \"4 2\",\n176\t        \"42x\",\n177\t    ] {\n178\t        assert_eq!(\n179\t            parse_token(bad),\n180\t            None,\n181\t            \"{bad:?} must not parse as a valid sequence\"\n182\t        );\n183\t    }\n184\t}\n185\t\n186\t#[test]\n187\tfn a_whitespace_padded_token_still_parses_because_char_columns_pad() {\n188\t    // Deliberate, and load-bearing: the watermark table's token column may be\n189\t    // a fixed-width `CHAR(20)`, which pads on read. Rejecting the padded form\n190\t    // would make every stored watermark on such a column unreadable — the\n191\t    // engine would see \"nothing committed\" and replay the whole stream.\n192\t    //\n193\t    // Pinned here so the leniency is understood as a compatibility requirement\n194\t    // rather than mistaken for sloppiness and \"tidied up\" later.\n195\t    assert_eq!(parse_token(\"  00000000000000000042  \"), Some(42));\n196\t    assert_eq!(parse_token(\" 42\"), Some(42));\n197\t\n198\t    let (seq, bookmark) = parse_token_parts(\" 00000000000000000042 #{\\\"page\\\":7}\")\n199\t        .expect(\"a padded bookmark-carrying token must parse too\");\n\n... [52 lines truncated] ..."},"type":"attachment","uuid":"eb7fe8c1-32e4-439d-83a8-28ad02e29296","timestamp":"2026-09-17T23:39:21.798Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\nNote: /Users/c-sikawats/Documents/GitHub/Personal/faucet-stream/crates/conformance/tests/compat_state_format.rs changed on disk since you last read it. That's usually deliberate, so take it as the current state rather than reverting it; if the change looks wrong, say so rather than undoing it yourself — otherwise no need to call it out. Here are the relevant changes (shown with line numbers):\n1\t//! #651 Category G1 — state-format compatibility.\n2\t//!\n3\t//! A resumable pipeline's bookmark outlives the process that wrote it, so every\n4\t//! upgrade crosses the state-format boundary. If release N+1 cannot read what N\n5\t//! wrote, the pipeline resumes from the wrong place — re-delivering everything\n6\t//! or losing everything — and **neither failure is loud**. The run is green\n7\t//! either way.\n8\t//!\n9\t//! A round-trip test cann