{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-09-17T23:50:35.586Z","sessionId":"55398c64-7c65-40db-9b68-886111a86d7f","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>bmexm1vw4</task-id>\n<summary>Monitor event: \"CI checks landing on PR 660 (reliability program)\"</summary>\n<event>Format: pass</event>\nIf this event is something the user would act on now, send a PushNotification. Routine or benign output doesn't need one.\n</task-notification>"}
{"parentUuid":"d26db4e3-7422-4478-8e6e-558ad9688754","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011Cf9ueURFjUbovrbeiBEZJ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"**[#660](https://github.com/faucet-hq/faucet-stream/pull/660) is up** with the reliability program, auto-merge armed with an explicit message. CI is running and the new job is reporting.\n\n**The first thing #651 needed was an honest audit, and it changed the plan.** The issue's premise — \"no systematic integration-test program\" — is partly stale. The repo already has **292 integration-test files**, including `serve_cluster.rs`, `serve_lifecycle.rs`, `schedule.rs`, `templates_end_to_end.rs`, `replication.rs`. Building the matrix as specified would have produced a large pile of duplicates. So I audited all ~40 sub-items against the live tree first and [posted it as the issue's tracked checklist](https://github.com/faucet-hq/faucet-stream/issues/651#issuecomment-5722787854).\n\nTwo results worth your attention:\n\n- **A5 (DLQ routing) and A7 (masking ordering) are already covered** — `faucet-core` has the full `dlq_all`-vs-`propagate` matrix, the per-page and total budget cases, `dlq_bookmark_advances_only_after_both_flushes`, and both `masking_runs_before_the_sink` and `masking_applies_before_the_dlq_envelope`. I did not duplicate them; the new required job runs them instead.\n- **E1 and F had literally zero files.** Those were the real gaps, along with G1.\n\n**What the program was actually missing was a foundation.** A guarantee like \"the bookmark is persisted only after the sink confirms\" is a property of the *order* in which the engine calls two collaborators — no pure unit test can observe an order, and no connector test can, since it sees one side. Every such test previously hand-rolled a `FailingSink` that failed on *every* call, so the interesting case (fail on the third write, succeed either side) went untested. `scripted` now provides one shared ordered `EventLog` plus doubles that fail at exactly one named boundary.\n\n**51 new tests.** Two design points I'd flag as load-bearing rather than incidental:\n\n- The exactly-once suite runs a **control arm**: the identical injected failure under at-least-once, asserting it *does* duplicate. Without that, the exactly-once assertions wouldn't be distinguishing the two modes and could pass for the wrong reason.\n- Cleanup's cancelled case needs its own test because **a cancel returns `Ok` by design**. The obvious check \"did the run succeed?\" would let that delete through — and it's precisely the case where the key set is partial and every unwritten row looks stale.\n\n**The new tests found two real things:**\n\n1. **SQLite's auto-mapped path is not type-preserving** — no boolean type, so `true`/`false` land as `1`/`0`; negative zero loses its sign. Now named via `Tolerance::skipping` with the reason, *and* the observed values pinned so a change that starts dropping those columns can't hide behind the tolerance. The JSON-document path is exactly lossless with no tolerance at all.\n2. **A whitespace-padded commit token parses — deliberately.** A `CHAR(20)` watermark column pads on read; rejecting the padded form would make every such stored watermark unreadable, so the engine would see \"nothing committed\" and replay the whole stream. My first test asserted the opposite. The code was right and my test was wrong, so I pinned the leniency with its reason rather than \"fixing\" working code.\n\nOne thing I deliberately did **not** do: mark the new job as a required check in branch protection. Per CLAUDE.md a context must be confirmed to actually report first — I'll add it once it has posted on a commit.\n\nI've suggested keeping #651 open for four remaining container/nightly tiers (the other fidelity pairs, the toxiproxy version of E1, live `evolve` DDL, and that branch-protection step), each now ~20 lines thanks to the harness. Say the word if you'd rather those become separate tier-2 issues so #651 closes with #660."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":837,"cache_read_input_tokens":555089,"output_tokens":1326,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":837,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":1326,"cache_read_input_tokens":555089,"cache_creation_input_tokens":837,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":837},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011Cf9ueThb7DFTf9DTNeEDP","type":"assistant","uuid":"d76f1e46-ff2d-49f5-92cf-3d0450203e58","timestamp":"2026-09-17T23:50:51.264Z","effort":"high","perTurnEffort":null,"session_id":"55398c64-7c65-40db-9b68-886111a86d7f","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/c-sikawats/Documents/GitHub/Personal/faucet-stream","sessionId":"55398c64-7c65-40db-9b68-886111a86d7f","version":"2.1.267","gitBranch":"test/reliability-program","slug":"linear-noodling-leaf"}
