{"parentUuid":"eb910fcb-9585-4ddb-8f6f-0622003fa333","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"hook_success","hookName":"PostToolUse:Bash","toolUseID":"toolu_0181BVEVCX4QvZUS2rF5R7af","hookEvent":"PostToolUse","content":"","stdout":"{\"terminalSequence\":\"\\u001b]777;notify;warp://cli-agent;{\\\"v\\\":1,\\\"agent\\\":\\\"claude\\\",\\\"event\\\":\\\"tool_complete\\\",\\\"session_id\\\":\\\"55398c64-7c65-40db-9b68-886111a86d7f\\\",\\\"cwd\\\":\\\"/Users/c-sikawats/Documents/GitHub/Personal/faucet-stream\\\",\\\"project\\\":\\\"faucet-stream\\\",\\\"tool_name\\\":\\\"Bash\\\"}\\u0007\"}\n","stderr":"/Users/c-sikawats/.claude/plugins/cache/claude-code-warp/warp/2.2.0/scripts/emit-terminal-sequence.sh: line 82: /dev/tty: Device not configured\n","exitCode":0,"command":"${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/scripts/on-post-tool-use.sh","durationMs":240},"type":"attachment","uuid":"7f133d23-533c-4629-84a3-b0ca6817e37f","timestamp":"2026-09-17T23:54:52.492Z","session_id":"55398c64-7c65-40db-9b68-886111a86d7f","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/c-sikawats/Documents/GitHub/Personal/faucet-stream","sessionId":"55398c64-7c65-40db-9b68-886111a86d7f","version":"2.1.267","gitBranch":"test/reliability-program","slug":"linear-noodling-leaf"}
{"parentUuid":"7f133d23-533c-4629-84a3-b0ca6817e37f","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"edited_text_file","filename":"/Users/c-sikawats/Documents/GitHub/Personal/faucet-stream/docs/book/src/operations/reliability-testing.md","snippet":"1\t# Reliability testing\n2\t\n3\tfaucet's claim is not that rows arrive — it is that **the data can be trusted**:\n4\tnothing is lost, nothing is duplicated, nothing is silently corrupted, a full\n5\trefresh is atomic, an incremental run resumes exactly where it stopped. Those\n6\tare promises about behaviour *under failure*, so they are verified by a\n7\tdedicated test program rather than by the connector suites.\n8\t\n9\tThis page describes what that program covers, how to run each tier locally, and\n10\thow to add to it.\n11\t\n12\t## Why these tests exist separately\n13\t\n14\tA guarantee like *\"the bookmark is persisted only after the sink confirms the\n15\tdata it covers\"* is not a property of any single function — it is a property of\n16\tthe **order** in which the engine calls two collaborators. A unit test over pure\n17\tlogic cannot observe an order. A connector test cannot either: it sees its own\n18\tside of the boundary.\n19\t\n20\tSo the reliability suites run the real `Pipeline::run` against doubles that fail\n21\tat one *named boundary* and record everything that happened, in order. The\n22\tassertions are then written against the sequence — which is what the guarantee\n23\tactually says — rather than against a final row count, which is what a weaker\n24\ttest checks and which stays green through a real regression.\n25\t\n26\t## The tiers\n27\t\n28\t| Tier | What it covers | Docker? | Runs |\n29\t|---|---|---|---|\n30\t| **Engine guarantees** | Bookmark ordering, exactly-once (both mechanisms), overwrite atomicity, cleanup safety, cancel-still-flushes | No | Every PR, **required** |\n31\t| **State compatibility** | Bookmarks / exactly-once envelopes / commit tokens written by a release still load | No | Every PR, **required** |\n32\t| **Connector conformance** | Per-connector battery (capabilities truthful, bounded memory, idempotent replay, …) | Per connector | Every PR |\n33\t| **Containerized integration** | Real databases, Kafka, object stores; CDC replication | Yes | Every PR, reported |\n34\t| **Fidelity round-trip** | Type-exact landing per source↔sink pair | Per pair | Every PR, reported |\n35\t\n36\tThe first two tiers are deliberately Docker-free and run in seconds. They are\n37\tthe required gate because a regression in them corrupts data *silently* — the\n38\trun still reports success — so they must never be skippable or flaky.\n39\t\n40\t## Running them locally\n41\t\n42\t```bash\n43\t# The required gate: engine guarantees + state-format compatibility.\n44\tcargo test -p faucet-conformance --all-features\n45\t\n46\t# The engine's own unit suites, which cover the DLQ routing/budget matrix\n47\t# and the masking-before-DLQ ordering.\n48\tcargo test -p faucet-core --all-features\n49\t\n50\t# One guarantee at a time.\n51\tcargo test -p faucet-conformance --test reliability_bookmark_ordering\n52\tcargo test -p faucet-conformance --test reliability_exactly_once\n53\tcargo test -p faucet-conformance --test reliability_lifecycle\n54\tcargo test -p faucet-conformance --test reliability_cleanup_safety\n55\tcargo test -p faucet-conformance --test compat_state_format\n56\t```\n57\t\n58\tContainer-backed suites need a Docker socket. On macOS with\n59\t[colima](https://github.com/abiosoft/colima):\n60\t\n61\t```bash\n62\tcolima start\n63\texport DOCKER_HOST=\"unix://$HOME/.colima/default/docker.sock\"\n64\tcargo test --workspace --all-features\n65\t```\n66\t\n67\t> The SQL Server image is x86-only, so the MSSQL suites cannot run on an\n68\t> ARM Mac — CI covers them.\n69\t\n70\t## What each guarantee suite asserts\n71\t\n72\t### Bookmark ordering (`reliability_bookmark_ordering`)\n73\t\n74\tEvery persisted bookmark is backed by writes the sink already confirmed. A\n75\tfailed write, a failed flush, and a failed state-store `put` each leave the\n76\tdurable bookmark *behind* the failure, so a resumed run re-reads rather than\n77\tskips. Resume starts at the page after the last durable bookmark — no gap, and\n78\tat most one page of duplicates.\n79\t\n80\t### Exactly-once (`reliability_exactly_once`)\n81\t\n82\tBoth mechanisms, separately:\n83\t\n84\t- **Atomic watermark** — writes route through the idempotent path with\n85\t  monotonic, distinct commit tokens; a crash in the window between the sink's\n86\t  commit and the state-store `put` replays **nothing**; re-running a completed\n87\t  pipeline writes nothing; a failed write leaves no token behind to skip on.\n88\t- **Keyed upsert** — convergence with no watermark at all.\n89\t\n90\tThe suite also runs a **control arm**: the identical injected failure under the\n91\tdefault at-least-once mode, asserting that it *does* duplicate. Without that,\n92\tthe exactly-once assertions would not be distinguishing the two modes and could\n93\tpass for the wrong reason.\n94\t\n95\t### Overwrite atomicity and cancellation (`reliability_lifecycle`)\n96\t\n97\t`begin_overwrite` precedes the first write and `commit_overwrite` follows the\n98\tlast. A failed write, a failed flush, and a cancel each abort **without ever\n99\tswapping** — a mid-run truncate-then-fail is the most destructive thing a data\n100\ttool can do. A failing commit surfaces rather than reporting success.\n101\t\n102\tSeparately, a cancelled run *flushes*: that is the difference between a\n103\tcooperative cancel and a dropped future, and it is what makes a buffered\n104\tParquet footer or an S3 multipart commit instead of orphaning.\n105\t\n106\t### Cleanup safety (`reliability_cleanup_safety`)\n107\t\n108\tScoped cleanup is the only feature that **deletes destination rows**, so its\n109\ttests are about when it must *not* fire: a failed run, a failed flush, a\n110\tcancelled run (which returns `Ok` — the trap), and an overflowed key set all\n111\tdelete nothing. A complete run that legitimately read zero rows still cleans the\n112\tscope, because that is a complete answer.\n113\t\n114\t### State-format compatibility (`compat_state_format`)\n115\t\n116\tFrozen files under `crates/conformance/tests/fixtures/state/` hold state written\n117\tby a released version. The tests read them and assert the current code still\n118\tunderstands them.\n119\t\n120\t**A round-trip test cannot catch this class of bug**, because it serializes and\n121\tdeserializes with the same code — both halves move together and the test stays\n122\tgreen through a breaking change. If one of these fails, the fixture is not what\n123\tis wrong: either the reader regressed, or the change needs an explicit\n124\tmigration.\n125\t\n126\t## Adding to the program\n127\t\n128\t### A new guarantee\n129\t\n130\t1. Add a `Boundary` variant to `faucet_conformance::scripted` if the failure\n131\t   point is new, and log a matching `Event`.\n132\t2. Write the test against `Pipeline::run` and assert on the **event sequence**,\n133\t   not a final count.\n134\t3. Prove the assertion can fail. Where the check is reusable, put it in\n135\t   `scripted` and add a `#[should_panic]` test that feeds it a deliberately\n136\t   wrong event log — the existing durability assertion does exactly this. A\n137\t   check that cannot fail is worthless.\n138\t\n139\t### A new source↔sink fidelity pair\n140\t\n141\tTwo pairs exist as references:\n142\t\n143\t- **`crates/sink/jsonl/tests/fidelity.rs`** — the strictest, and the control for\n144\t  the whole category. A JSONL file has no schema, no column types and no\n145\t  affinity rules, so nothing can be excused as a destination limitation: the\n146\t  assertion carries **no tolerance at all**. If a value survives here but not in\n147\t  a typed destination, the loss belongs to that destination; if it fails here,\n148\t  the loss is faucet's.\n149\t- **`crates/sink/sqlite/tests/fidelity.rs`** — a typed destination. Its\n150\t  JSON-document mode is exactly lossless; its auto-mapped mode is not, and the\n151\t  test names each reason (SQLite has no boolean type, so `true`/`false` become\n152\t  `1`/`0`; negative zero loses its sign through a TEXT-affinity column) and pins\n153\t  the observed values so a future change that drops those columns entirely\n154\t  cannot hide behind the tolerance.\n155\t\n156\tUse the shared corpus so the pair cannot quietly pick easier data:\n157\t\n158\t```rust\n159\tuse faucet_conformance::fidelity::{self, Tolerance};\n160\t\n161\tlet landed = /* read the destination back */;\n162\tfidelity::assert_round_trip(&fidelity::corpus(), &landed, Tolerance::exact());\n163\t```\n164\t\n\n... [17 lines truncated] ..."},"type":"attachment","uuid":"8bfd93b0-a0fb-49d4-abaa-3fa637cd989a","timestamp":"2026-09-17T23:54:52.511Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\nNote: /Users/c-sikawats/Documents/GitHub/Personal/faucet-stream/docs/book/src/operations/reliability-testing.md changed on disk since you last read it. That's usually deliberate, so take it as the current state rather than reverting it; if the change looks wrong, say so rather than undoing it yourself — otherwise no need to call it out. Here are the relevant changes (shown with line numbers):\n1\t# Reliability testing\n2\t\n3\tfaucet's claim is not that rows arrive — it is that **the data can be trusted**:\n4\tnothing is lost, nothing is duplicated, nothing is silently corrupted, a full\n5\trefresh is atomic, an incremental run resumes exactly where it stopped. Those\n6\tare promises about behaviour *under failure*, so they are verified by a\n7\tdedicated test program rather than by the connector suites.\n8\t\n9\tThis page describes what that program covers, how to run each tier locally, and\n10\thow to add to it.\n11\t\n12\t## Why these tests exist separately\n13\t\n14\tA guarantee like *\"the bookmark is persisted only after the sink confirms the\n15\tdata it covers\"* is not a property of any single function — it is a property of\n16\tthe **order** in which the engine calls two collaborators. A unit test over pure\n17\tlogic cannot observe an order. A connector test cannot either: it sees its own\n18\tside of the boundary.\n19\t\n20\tSo the reliability suites run the real `Pipeline::run` against doubles that fail\n21\tat one *named boundary* and record everything that happened, in order. The\n22\tassertions are then written against the sequence — which is what the guarantee\n23\tactually says — rather than against a final row count, which is what a weaker\n24\ttest checks and which stays green through a real regression.\n25\t\n26\t## The tiers\n27\t\n28\t| Tier | What it covers | Docker? | Runs |\n29\t|---|---|---|---|\n30\t| **Engine guarantees** | Bookmark ordering, exactly-once (both mechanisms), overwrite atomicity, cleanup safety, cancel-still-flushes | No | Every PR, **required** |\n31\t| **State compatibility** | Bookmarks / exactly-once envelopes / commit tokens written by a release still load | No | Every PR, **required** |\n32\t| **Connector conformance** | Per-connector battery (capabilities truthful, bounded memory, idempotent replay, …) | Per connector | Every PR |\n33\t| **Containerized integration** | Real databases, Kafka, object stores; CDC replication | Yes | Every PR, reported |\n34\t| **Fidelity round-trip** | Type-exact landing per source↔sink pair | Per pair | Every PR, reported |\n35\t\n36\tThe first two tiers are deliberately Docker-free and run in seconds. They are\n37\tthe required gate because a regression in them corrupts data *silently* — the\n38\trun still reports success — so they must never be skippable or flaky.\n39\t\n40\t## Running them locally\n41\t\n42\t```bash\n43\t# The required gate: engine guarantees + state-format compatibility.\n44\tcargo test -p faucet-conformance --all-features\n45\t\n46\t# The engine's own unit suites, which cover the DLQ routing/budget matrix\n47\t# and the masking-before-DLQ ordering.\n48\tcargo test -p faucet-core --all-features\n49\t\n50\t# One guarantee at a time.\n51\tcargo test -p faucet-conformance --test reliability_bookmark_ordering\n52\tcargo test -p faucet-conformance --test reliability_exactly_once\n53\tcargo test -p faucet-conformance --test reliability_lifecycle\n54\tcargo test -p faucet-conformance --test reliability_cleanup_safety\n55\tcargo test -p faucet-conformance --test compat_state_format\n56\t```\n57\t\n58\tContainer-backed suites need a Docker socket. On macOS with\n59\t[colima](https://github.com/abiosoft/colima):\n60\t\n61\t```bash\n62\tcolima start\n63\texport DOCKER_HOST=\"unix://$HOME/.colima/default/docker.sock\"\n64\tcargo test --workspace --all-features\n65\t```\n66\t\n67\t> The SQL Server image is x86-only, so the MSSQL suites cannot run on an\n68\t> ARM Mac — CI covers them.\n69\t\n70\t## What each guarantee suite asserts\n71\t\n72\t### Bookmark ordering (`reliability_bookmark_ordering`)\n73\t\n74\tEvery persisted bookmark is backed by writes the sink already confirmed. A\n75\tfailed write, a failed flush, and a failed state-store `put` each leave the\n76\tdurable bookmark *behind* the failure, so a resumed run re-reads rather than\n77\tskips. Resume starts at the page after the last durable bookmark — no gap, and\n78\tat most one page of duplicates.\n79\t\n80\t### Exactly-once (`reliability_exactly_once`)\n81\t\n82\tBoth mechanisms, separately:\n83\t\n84\t- **Atomic watermark** — writes route through the idempotent path with\n85\t  monotonic, distinct commit tokens; a crash in the window between the sink's\n86\t  commit and the state-store `put` replays **nothing**; re-running a completed\n87\t  pipeline writes nothing; a failed write leaves no token behind to skip on.\n88\t- **Keyed upsert** — convergence with no watermark at all.\n89\t\n90\tThe suite also runs a **control arm**: the identical injected failure under the\n91\tdefault at-least-once mode, asserting that it *does* duplicate. Without that,\n92\tthe exactly-once assertions would not be distinguishing the two modes and could\n93\tpass for the wrong reason.\n94\t\n95\t### Overwrite atomicity and cancellation (`reliability_lifecycle`)\n96\t\n97\t`begin_overwrite` precedes the first write and `commit_overwrite` follows the\n98\tlast. A failed write, a failed flush, and a cancel each abort **without ever\n99\tswapping** — a mid-run truncate-then-fail is the most destructive thing a data\n100\ttool can do. A failing commit surfaces rather than reporting success.\n101\t\n102\tSeparately, a cancelled run *flushes*: that is the difference between a\n103\tcooperative cancel and a dropped future, and it is what makes a buffered\n104\tParquet footer or an S3 multipart commit instead of orphaning.\n105\t\n106\t### Cleanup safety (`reliability_cleanup_safety`)\n107\t\n108\tScoped cleanup is the only feature that **deletes destination rows**, so its\n109\ttests are about when it must *not* fire: a failed run, a failed flush, a\n110\tcancelled run (which returns `Ok` — the trap), and an overflowed key set all\n111\tdelete nothing. A complete run that legitimately read zero rows still cleans the\n112\tscope, because that is a complete answer.\n113\t\n114\t### State-format compatibility (`compat_state_format`)\n115\t\n116\tFrozen files under `crates/conformance/tests/fixtures/state/` hold state written\n117\tby a released version. The tests read them and assert the current code still\n118\tunderstands them.\n119\t\n120\t**A round-trip test cannot catch this class of bug**, because it serializes and\n121\tdeserializes with the same code — both halves move together and the test stays\n122\tgreen through a breaking change. If one of these fails, the fixture is not what\n123\tis wrong: either the reader regressed, or the change needs an explicit\n124\tmigration.\n125\t\n126\t## Adding to the program\n127\t\n128\t### A new guarantee\n129\t\n130\t1. Add a `Boundary` variant to `faucet_conformance::scripted` if the failure\n131\t   point is new, and log a matching `Event`.\n132\t2. Write the test against `Pipeline::run` and assert on the **event sequence**,\n133\t   not a final count.\n134\t3. Prove the assertion can fail. Where the check is reusable, put it in\n135\t   `scripted` and add a `#[should_panic]` test that feeds it a deliberately\n136\t   wrong event log — the existing durability assertion does exactly this. A\n137\t   check that cannot fail is worthless.\n138\t\n139\t### A new source↔sink fidelity pair\n140\t\n141\tTwo pairs exist as references:\n142\t\n143\t- **`crates/sink/jsonl/tests/fidelity.rs`** — the strictest, and the control for\n144\t  the whole category. A JSONL file has no schema, no column types and no\n145\t  affinity rules, so nothing can be excused as a destination limitation: the\n146\t  assertion carries **no tolerance at all**. If a value survives here but not in\n147\t  a typed destination, the loss belongs to that destination; if it fails here,\n148\t  the loss is faucet's.\n149\t- **`crates/sink/sqlite/tests/fidelity.rs`** — a typed destination. Its\n150\t  JSON-document mode is exactly lossless; its auto-mapped mode is not, and the\n151\t  test names each reason (SQLite has no boolean type, so `true`/`false` become\n152\t  `1`/`0`; negative zero loses its sign through a TEXT-affinity column) and pins\n153\t  the observed values so a future change that drops those columns entirely\n154\t  cannot hide behind the tolerance.\n155\t\n156\tUse the shared corpus so the pair cannot quietly pick easier data:\n157\t\n158\t```rust\n159\tuse faucet_conformance::fidelity::{self, Tolerance};\n160\t\n161\tlet landed = /* read the destination back */;\n162\tfidelity::assert_round_trip(&fidelity::corpus(), &landed, Tolerance::exact());\n163\t```\n164\t\n\n... [17 lines truncated] ...\n</system-reminder>"}],"session_id":"55398c64-7c65-40db-9b68-886111a86d7f","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/c-sikawats/Documents/GitHub/Personal/faucet-stream","sessionId":"55398c64-7c65-40db-9b68-886111a86d7f","version":"2.1.267","gitBranch":"test/reliability-program","slug":"linear-noodling-leaf"}
