{"parentUuid":"b0833c74-825a-4457-ab55-24adccfc393a","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CfAb5RZbcv3cTYhEe1Y2M","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_016LJZCtYQ5b6TEkdLYV3pZi","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /Users/c-sikawats/Documents/GitHub/Personal/faucet-stream\nS=/private/tmp/claude-502/-Users-c-sikawats-Documents-GitHub-Personal-faucet-stream/55398c64-7c65-40db-9b68-886111a86d7f/scratchpad\ncat > $S/pr664.md <<'BODY'\nThree bug fixes for the reliability release, each verified against the live code first.\n\nBatched into one PR because CI takes ~1h per cycle and these are one coherent unit of work (the open bugs blocking the release), but each is a self-contained commit with its own `Closes`.\n\n## 1. A silent data-loss path — `Closes #652`\n\n`run_stream` checkpointed each bookmark-carrying page **before** `Pipeline::run` decided the overwrite outcome. So a run whose `commit_overwrite` then failed left the destination untouched — correct — while the state store claimed delivery. The next incremental run resumed past the gap, so the \"refreshed\" destination was **permanently missing rows**, silently, with every run green thereafter.\n\n`run_stream` now holds bookmarks under `is_overwrite()` and returns the last one; `Pipeline::run` persists it only after the swap commits.\n\n**I verified the tests catch it.** Reverting the fix fails four of the five new tests, and the event log shows the bug directly:\n\n```\n[BeginOverwrite, Write(3), Flush, StatePut{page:0}, Write(3), Flush, StatePut{page:1}, …]\n                                  ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^ no CommitOverwrite, yet page 3 is durable\n```\n\nThe fifth test is the control: ADR 0002's per-page ordering stays intact for non-overwrite runs. Deferring there would trade this bug for a worse one — a long run that failed would restart from the beginning.\n\nErring toward *not* persisting is the safe asymmetry, and the code says so: a held bookmark costs a redundant re-read (at-least-once, absorbed by the refresh itself); an early one costs rows never read again. Same reasoning covers the `suppress_overwrite_lifecycle` fan-out case, where the orchestrator owns the commit and therefore the persist.\n\n## 2. A config key accepted and ignored — `Closes #657`\n\n`headers_path` was accepted, extracted by a tested helper, and documented — then dropped on the floor. Neither produce path attached it, so a config asking for headers produced messages with none: no error, no warning, and a consumer routing on a header saw nothing.\n\nWired on both paths. The commit-token record deliberately does **not** carry them — it is faucet's own watermark on its own side-topic, not user data.\n\nA JSON `null` now becomes a **valueless** header rather than the string `\"null\"`. Kafka's model permits no value, and `\"null\"` is indistinguishable to a consumer from the four-character string. No existing test asserted the old behaviour, so nothing was rewritten to fit.\n\nFive integration tests **consume the messages back and read their headers**, so the assertions are about what reached the broker, not what the extractor returned. Two things that cost me three wrong hypotheses, now documented in the code:\n\n- `rdkafka`'s `StreamConsumer` polls on a background task, so a current-thread runtime never receives anything;\n- the first `recv()` also pays for the consumer group's JoinGroup/SyncGroup rebalance, so a short timeout gives up mid-rebalance and reports an empty topic — indistinguishable from \"the sink produced nothing\".\n\nI only found these by baselining the *existing* Kafka test in the same environment, which passed and made it clear the broker was fine and my test was wrong.\n\nThe three per-record routing values now travel as a `RecordRouting` struct. Clippy's arity limit was the prompt, but eight positional parameters — three of them `Option` — was the real problem.\n\n## 3. A runtime failure wearing a config label — `Closes #662`\n\nQuery sources typed **runtime** query failures as `FaucetError::Config`:\n\n```\nConfig(\"PostgreSQL query failed: … terminating connection due to administrator command\")\n```\n\n`Config` means \"your YAML is wrong\", so this sends an on-call engineer to their pipeline config during a database incident. And `is_retriable()` is false for both variants today, so nothing retries differently *yet* — but a dropped connection should eventually be retriable, and `Config` is the one variant that must never be. Leaving it mistyped would silently exclude every SQL source from that change.\n\n**Scope correction:** the issue (which I filed) claimed nine sources. Verifying each, only three had query-time sites — duckdb's are genuine config/open failures, spanner's three are a non-scalar parameter, an INT64 overflow and a NUMERIC cursor column (all correctly `Config`), and bigquery/redshift had none. So: 6 sites across 3 crates. I've corrected the issue.\n\nConnect-time sites stay `Config` and now say why inline: they run in `new()` before any data moves, which is what lets `validate`/`doctor` report an unreachable destination as a config problem.\n\nSQLSTATE preservation and the retriability change that depends on it are deliberately **not** here — they need their own failure-injection tests, and #656 has the pattern to reuse.\n\n## Gates\n\n`cargo fmt --all`; `clippy --all-targets --all-features -D warnings` on every touched crate; `faucet-core` 1164 lib tests; `faucet-conformance` 139; the full Kafka suite against a real broker (60 unit + 3 conformance + 9 coverage + 1 exactly-once + 5 new); postgres/mysql/sqlite source libs; `cargo semver-checks` → no semver update required for `faucet-core` and `faucet-sink-kafka`.\nBODY\ngh pr create --repo faucet-hq/faucet-stream --base main --head fix/overwrite-bookmark-ordering \\\n  --title \"fix: three reliability bugs — overwrite bookmark ordering, kafka headers, source error typing\" \\\n  --body-file $S/pr664.md 2>&1 | tail -2","description":"Open the reliability fixes PR"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":398,"cache_read_input_tokens":805958,"output_tokens":2124,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":398,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":2124,"cache_read_input_tokens":805958,"cache_creation_input_tokens":398,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":398},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_016LJZCtYQ5b6TEkdLYV3pZi":{"command":"cd /Users/c-sikawats/Documents/GitHub/Personal/faucet-stream\nS=/private/tmp/claude-502/-Users-c-sikawats-Documents-GitHub-Personal-faucet-stream/55398c64-7c65-40db-9b68-886111a86d7f/scratchpad\ncat > $S/pr664.md <<'BODY'\nThree bug fixes for the reliability release, each verified against the live code first.\n\nBatched into one PR because CI takes ~1h per cycle and these are one coherent unit of work (the open bugs blocking the release), but each is a self-contained commit with its own `Closes`.\n\n## 1. A silent data-loss path — `Closes #652`\n\n`run_stream` checkpointed each bookmark-carrying page **before** `Pipeline::run` decided the overwrite outcome. So a run whose `commit_overwrite` then failed left the destination untouched — correct — while the state store claimed delivery. The next incremental run resumed past the gap, so the \"refreshed\" destination was **permanently missing rows**, silently, with every run green thereafter.\n\n`run_stream` now holds bookmarks under `is_overwrite()` and returns the last one; `Pipeline::run` persists it only after the swap commits.\n\n**I verified the tests catch it.** Reverting the fix fails four of the five new tests, and the event log shows the bug directly:\n\n```\n[BeginOverwrite, Write(3), Flush, StatePut{page:0}, Write(3), Flush, StatePut{page:1}, …]\n                                  ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^ no CommitOverwrite, yet page 3 is durable\n```\n\nThe fifth test is the control: ADR 0002's per-page ordering stays intact for non-overwrite runs. Deferring there would trade this bug for a worse one — a long run that failed would restart from the beginning.\n\nErring toward *not* persisting is the safe asymmetry, and the code says so: a held bookmark costs a redundant re-read (at-least-once, absorbed by the refresh itself); an early one costs rows never read again. Same reasoning covers the `suppress_overwrite_lifecycle` fan-out case, where the orchestrator owns the commit and therefore the persist.\n\n## 2. A config key accepted and ignored — `Closes #657`\n\n`headers_path` was accepted, extracted by a tested helper, and documented — then dropped on the floor. Neither produce path attached it, so a config asking for headers produced messages with none: no error, no warning, and a consumer routing on a header saw nothing.\n\nWired on both paths. The commit-token record deliberately does **not** carry them — it is faucet's own watermark on its own side-topic, not user data.\n\nA JSON `null` now becomes a **valueless** header rather than the string `\"null\"`. Kafka's model permits no value, and `\"null\"` is indistinguishable to a consumer from the four-character string. No existing test asserted the old behaviour, so nothing was rewritten to fit.\n\nFive integration tests **consume the messages back and read their headers**, so the assertions are about what reached the broker, not what the extractor returned. Two things that cost me three wrong hypotheses, now documented in the code:\n\n- `rdkafka`'s `StreamConsumer` polls on a background task, so a current-thread runtime never receives anything;\n- the first `recv()` also pays for the consumer group's JoinGroup/SyncGroup rebalance, so a short timeout gives up mid-rebalance and reports an empty topic — indistinguishable from \"the sink produced nothing\".\n\nI only found these by baselining the *existing* Kafka test in the same environment, which passed and made it clear the broker was fine and my test was wrong.\n\nThe three per-record routing values now travel as a `RecordRouting` struct. Clippy's arity limit was the prompt, but eight positional parameters — three of them `Option` — was the real problem.\n\n## 3. A runtime failure wearing a config label — `Closes #662`\n\nQuery sources typed **runtime** query failures as `FaucetError::Config`:\n\n```\nConfig(\"PostgreSQL query failed: … terminating connection due to administrator command\")\n```\n\n`Config` means \"your YAML is wrong\", so this sends an on-call engineer to their pipeline config during a database incident. And `is_retriable()` is false for both variants today, so nothing retries differently *yet* — but a dropped connection should eventually be retriable, and `Config` is the one variant that must never be. Leaving it mistyped would silently exclude every SQL source from that change.\n\n**Scope correction:** the issue (which I filed) claimed nine sources. Verifying each, only three had query-time sites — duckdb's are genuine config/open failures, spanner's three are a non-scalar parameter, an INT64 overflow and a NUMERIC cursor column (all correctly `Config`), and bigquery/redshift had none. So: 6 sites across 3 crates. I've corrected the issue.\n\nConnect-time sites stay `Config` and now say why inline: they run in `new()` before any data moves, which is what lets `validate`/`doctor` report an unreachable destination as a config problem.\n\nSQLSTATE preservation and the retriability change that depends on it are deliberately **not** here — they need their own failure-injection tests, and #656 has the pattern to reuse.\n\n## Gates\n\n`cargo fmt --all`; `clippy --all-targets --all-features -D warnings` on every touched crate; `faucet-core` 1164 lib tests; `faucet-conformance` 139; the full Kafka suite against a real broker (60 unit + 3 conformance + 9 coverage + 1 exactly-once + 5 new); postgres/mysql/sqlite source libs; `cargo semver-checks` → no semver update required for `faucet-core` and `faucet-sink-kafka`.\nBODY\ngh pr create --repo faucet-hq/faucet-stream --base main --head fix/overwrite-bookmark-ordering \\\n  --title \"fix: three reliability bugs — overwrite bookmark ordering, kafka headers, source error typing\" \\\n  --body-file $S/pr664.md 2>&1 | tail -2","description":"Open the reliability fixes PR"}},"apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011CfAb5QrBQ2unhKv6dR8vj","type":"assistant","uuid":"c89ca528-6fe2-4ada-8a41-c132bcb16bbc","timestamp":"2026-09-18T08:28:03.917Z","effort":"high","perTurnEffort":null,"session_id":"55398c64-7c65-40db-9b68-886111a86d7f","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/c-sikawats/Documents/GitHub/Personal/faucet-stream","sessionId":"55398c64-7c65-40db-9b68-886111a86d7f","version":"2.1.267","gitBranch":"fix/overwrite-bookmark-ordering","slug":"linear-noodling-leaf"}
