{"parentUuid":"690d7b72-0134-4d33-b5f1-e6ae08e6edeb","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"0b270ed8-79ad-458f-b783-66e7a379786e","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01XSfwFFY5AA4336RrtHr3T5","type":"tool_result","content":"- [No employer/use-case keywords in issues](feedback_no_employer_or_usecase_keywords_in_issues.md) — never \"Drivetrain\"/tenant ids/customer orgs/internal repos in public repo artifacts; generic industry framing; keep this rule OUT of tracked files.\n- [Stop asking, just execute](feedback_stop_asking_just_execute.md) — when a task lists N items, fix all N and report once; don't ask \"shall I continue?\" mid-list.\n════════ #619\nObject-store sources: `concurrency` is a no-op on the streaming path (serial object reads in real runs) + whole-object Parquet buffering\n\n## Summary\n\nFor the object-store sources (`s3`, `gcs`, `azure-blob`), the documented **`concurrency` config field is a no-op on the streaming path** — `stream_pages`/`stream_batches` (what `Pipeline::run` actually drives) read objects **strictly serially**; `concurrency` (`buffer_unordered`) is applied only in the deprecated eager `fetch_with_context`. So a many-object prefix/glob scan is single-flighted in every real run, despite the knob. Secondary: several formats buffer whole objects, and no source uses parallel byte-range downloads.\n\n## Motivation\n\n[Primary Goal — performance + correctness of the public API.] A documented performance knob that silently does nothing is both a perf loss and an API-correctness bug. Object-store reads of many objects are exactly where parallelism matters.\n\n## Findings\n\n- **s3 source — MED (bug).** `stream_pages` reads objects in a serial `for key in &keys` loop (`crates/source/s3/src/stream.rs:405`); columnar `stream_batches` serial (`:616`). `concurrency` (default 10) is used only in `fetch_with_context` (`:332-341`). JSONL streams line-by-line (good); `JsonArray`/`RawText` buffer whole object; **Parquet-on-S3 buffers the whole object** into `Bytes` + sync `ParquetRecordBatchReaderBuilder` (`:140-149,772-795`) instead of the async `ParquetObjectReader`.\n- **gcs source — MED (bug).** Identical: serial `stream_pages` (`crates/source/gcs/src/stream.rs:404`); concurrency only in eager path (`:345-363`); whole-object Parquet buffering.\n- **azure-blob source — MED (bug).** Serial `stream_pages` (`crates/source/azure-blob/src/stream.rs:261`); concurrency only in eager path (`:212-220`). Holds an `object_store` handle exposing `get_ranges` but doesn't use it.\n- **sftp source — MED.** Always serial (`crates/source/sftp/src/stream.rs:136`), **no `concurrency` field at all**; `raw_text`/`json_array` buffer whole file.\n- **parquet source — LOW (reference).** True `ParquetObjectReader` byte-range/row-group async streaming with projection pushdown; only minor serial multi-file streaming + serial up-front footer probes.\n\n## Proposed design\n\n1. **Make `concurrency` real on the streaming path:** run per-object readers through `buffer_unordered(concurrency)` in `stream_pages`/`stream_batches` (with a documented ordering caveat, or an `ordered` option). Add a `concurrency` field to the sftp source.\n2. **Stop buffering whole objects for Parquet-on-S3/GCS:** use the async `ParquetObjectReader` (as the parquet source does) for row-group streaming.\n3. **Optional (large single objects):** parallel byte-range download via `object_store::get_ranges` (azure/parquet already on `object_store`; s3/gcs would need a manual range reader over the vendor SDK).\n\n## Affected crates\n`crates/source/s3/`, `crates/source/gcs/`, `crates/source/azure-blob/`, `crates/source/sftp/`; reference `crates/source/parquet/src/stream.rs`.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n- `concurrency` demonstrably parallelizes object reads in `stream_pages` (a multi-object scan is faster with concurrency > 1); test asserts overlap.\n- Parquet-on-S3/GCS streams row groups (bounded memory), not whole-object.\n- sftp gains a working `concurrency` knob.\n- ≥95% patch coverage.\n\n## References\nSurfaced by the connector audit. Related: object-store sink issue (companion). Roadmap epic #38.\n\n════════ #620\nLakehouse sinks: delta `target_file_size` dead knob + O(whole-table) bulk memory; coalesce CDC commits (delta/iceberg)\n\n## Summary\n\nTwo lakehouse-sink issues surfaced by the audit. (1) **delta**: the `target_file_size` config knob is validated but **never wired** into the writer, and a **bulk load buffers the entire dataset in memory** (it only commits at end-of-run because bulk sources emit no bookmarks) → O(whole-table) peak memory + reliance on delta-rs's default file sizing. (2) **Both delta and iceberg**: a **CDC/streaming source** (one bookmark per transaction) forces **one commit + data file set per transaction** → many tiny snapshots/files → the small-files problem that bloats table metadata and degrades all downstream reads.\n\nNote: neither sink commits per *page* (both accumulate across pages and commit on bookmark-carrying pages, `crates/core/src/pipeline.rs:1715/1768/1849`), so **bulk/query pipelines are fine** (iceberg is already the good post-BigQuery-fix shape: 256 MB rolling files, one snapshot/run). The exposure is CDC feeds + delta's memory.\n\n## Motivation\n\n[Primary Goal — reliability/performance.] A bulk delta load can OOM on a large table (whole dataset buffered). CDC→lakehouse is a core streaming use case, and per-transaction snapshots are a well-known table-format anti-pattern (metadata bloat, slow query planning).\n\n## Findings\n\n- **delta — HIGH.** `RecordBatchWriter::for_table(table)` built with no `WriterConfig`/target size (`crates/sink/delta/src/sink.rs:119`); `target_file_size` declared+validated (`config.rs:59,87`) but never referenced → **dead knob**. Writer buffers parquet in memory until `flush_and_commit` (`sink.rs:335-338`); bulk sources never emit a bookmark, so the only flush is end-of-run → **peak memory ≈ O(whole dataset)**. CDC → one commit + file(s) per transaction → small files + `_delta_log` bloat. (`deltalake` 0.32.4.)\n- **iceberg — LOW bulk / HIGH CDC.** `RollingFileWriter` rolls data files to storage at `target_file_size_mb` (default 256, wired at `writer.rs:95-103`) → O(target) memory, one `fast_append` snapshot/run for bulk (good). But CDC (bookmark-per-txn, and the EO-eligible class) → one snapshot + ≥1 file per transaction, each with manifest/manifest-list churn (`sink.rs:424,448`). (`iceberg` 0.10.0 — note stale \"0.9.1\" comments at `sink.rs:20,373` and in `.claude/rules/architecture.md`.)\n\n## Proposed design\n\n1. **delta: wire `target_file_size`** into the delta-rs writer (`WriterConfig`/writer-properties) so files roll at the configured threshold; add a **size/row-based intermediate flush+commit** for bulk so memory is bounded to O(target_file_size) (accept a few commits/run).\n2. **Both: coalesce commits across CDC transactions** — commit every N transactions / accumulated bytes / time window instead of one-commit-per-bookmark, carrying the **highest bookmark/commit-token** for the coalesced group (must reconcile with per-transaction durability + exactly-once token model).\n3. Fix the stale `iceberg-rust 0.9.1` → `0.10.0` version references.\n\n## Affected crates\n`crates/sink/delta/`, `crates/sink/iceberg/`; the flush/bookmark cadence lives in `crates/core/src/pipeline.rs` — a shared \"commit coalescing\" option (every N/bytes/time) is the cleanest home.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n- A bulk delta load of a large table has bounded peak memory (~target_file_size), not O(whole table); `target_file_size` actually controls output file size.\n- A CDC run of N small transactions produces far fewer than N snapshots/commits (coalesced), with correct resume/EO semantics.\n- ≥95% patch coverage; version comments corrected.\n\n## References\nCompanion to the BigQuery/warehouse sink work (#612/#614). Surfaced by the connector audit. Roadmap epic #38.\n\n════════ #609\nTemplate register accepts a wrongly-formatted connector config (only caught at run time)\n\n## Summary\n`faucet template register` (and `faucet validate`) accept a template whose **connector `config` is structurally invalid**. The bad config passes registration, the template **launches**, and the error only surfaces when a run is triggered. We should **detect a wrongly-formatted template at register/validate time** and reject it with a clear, per-node error.\n\n## Repro (real)\nA `test-mssql` template was registered with the connector block nested wrong:\n```yaml\npipeline:\n  sources:\n    default:\n      type: mssql\n      config:\n        connection:                     # WRONG — MssqlConnectionConfig is #[serde(flatten)]\n          connection_string: \"REDACTED;\"\n        query: \"SELECT * FROM ${param.schema}.${param.table}\"\n```\n- `POST /v1/templates` → **201 Created**, and it **launched** fine.\n- First triggered run → **failed**: `Config error: MSSQL config requires either connection_url or connection_string`.\n\nThe correct shape is flattened (`config: { connection_string: … }`). Registration never noticed the mistake.\n\n## Root cause\n`cli/src/templates/store.rs::register` validates via `topology::validate_topology_spec` **or** `expand::expand(&cfg)` + `compile_transforms(node.transforms)` per row. **None of these deserialize the connector `config` into its typed struct** or otherwise check the connector's schema — the `config` stays an opaque `serde_json::Value` until `registry::build_source`/`build_sink` runs at execution time. So any connector-config shape error slips through:\n- wrong nesting / flattening (the mssql case above),\n- unknown field under `deny_unknown_fields`,\n- wrong scalar type, missing required field,\n- typo'd field name.\n\n`faucet validate` has the same blind spot (it also only expands).\n\n## Proposed design\nAdd a **non-connecting config validation** step run at register and in `faucet validate`:\n- New registry hooks `registry::validate_source_config(kind, &Value) -> CliResult<()>` / `validate_sink_config(kind, &Value)` (feature-gated per connector) that **deserialize** the `config` `Value` into the connector's typed config (serde, honoring `deny_unknown_fields`) and run its `validate()` if present — **without** building clients/pools or opening connections.\n- Call them per node in `store::register` (on the placeholder-bound copy already used for structural validation, so `${param.*}` / `${env:}` / `${file:}` don't block deserialize) and in the `validate` command.\n- Connectors **not compiled into this build** can't be checked → report `cannot validate <kind>: feature not compiled` (warn or hard-fail behind a flag), never a silent pass.\n\nDeserialize-only keeps it fast and credential-free; live connectivity stays the job of `faucet doctor`.\n\n## Affected crates / files\n- `cli/src/registry.rs` (new `validate_source_config` / `validate_sink_config` dispatchers, per-connector arms)\n- `cli/src/templates/store.rs` (invoke in `register`)\n- `cli/src/commands/validate.rs` / `cli/src/expand.rs` (invoke in `faucet validate`)\n- connector config structs already derive `Deserialize` + often have `validate()` — reused as-is\n\n## Edge cases & failure modes\n════════ #623\nREST source: Salesforce Bulk / async_job buffers the entire result set into one page (O(whole extract) memory, non-resumable)\n\n## Summary\n\n`faucet-source-rest`'s async-job path (`async_job:` — used for Salesforce **Bulk API**, the `test-salesforce` tap) **buffers the entire result set into memory and yields it as a single `StreamPage`**. `run_async_job` (`crates/source/rest/src/stream.rs:1043-1160`) submits → polls → then loops over the Salesforce result-set locators (`Sforce-Locator`, #557) doing `all_records.extend(records)` across every locator page (`stream.rs:1147`), and `stream_pages_inner` short-circuits with a single `yield StreamPage { records, bookmark: None }` for the whole job (`stream.rs:512-515`). So a large Bulk extract is fully materialized as one giant `Vec<Value>` — **O(whole extract) peak memory**, no per-page flush, and (because `bookmark: None`) no intermediate progress/bookmark, so a crash loses the entire run.\n\nThis is the REST analog of the warehouse/lakehouse buffer-everything issues (#620 etc.) and the opposite of the correctly-streamed paginated/OData REST paths (which are O(one API page)).\n\n## Motivation\n\n[Primary Goal — reliability/efficiency.] Salesforce is one of the targeted production taps, and Bulk API is exactly the path used for *large* objects (that's why Bulk exists). Holding the whole result in RAM caps extract size by memory and makes the run non-resumable.\n\n## Findings\n\n- `stream_pages_inner` async-job short-circuit: single yield for the whole job — `crates/source/rest/src/stream.rs:512-515`.\n- `run_async_job` accumulator: `all_records.extend(records)` over all locator pages, returns one `Vec` — `stream.rs:1129-1158` (extend at `:1147`).\n- Each locator page is itself whole-body (`resp.bytes()` → `parse_fetch_page`, `stream.rs:1023,1166-1204`) — fine per page, but they're concatenated, not streamed.\n- Contrast: the paginated/OData data path streams per page with no accumulator (`stream.rs:624-780`) — the correct shape.\n\n## Proposed design\n\nConvert `run_async_job` from \"accumulate then return `Vec`\" into a **generator that yields each locator/fetch page as its own `StreamPage`** as it is downloaded, mirroring the paginated loop:\n- yield each locator page's parsed records immediately; only advance to the next `Sforce-Locator` after yielding;\n- carry a resumable bookmark where the API allows (the locator/job id + offset), so a crash can resume mid-job (or at least emit progress);\n- keep the submit/poll lifecycle unchanged.\n\nPeak memory then drops to O(one locator page). This is the highest-value source-side change for the 9 REST taps.\n\n## Affected crates / files\n`crates/source/rest/src/stream.rs` (`run_async_job`, the `async_job` branch of `stream_pages_inner`).\n\n## Edge cases\n- Empty result / zero locators (yield nothing, clean finish).\n- Poll timeout / job failure (unchanged error handling).\n- CSV vs JSON vs Excel fetch-page formats (`parse_fetch_page`) — stream each regardless of format.\n- Resume semantics: if the Bulk job id expires, fall back to full re-run.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n- A multi-locator Bulk extract yields multiple `StreamPage`s (one per locator/fetch page), peak memory O(one page), verified by a wiremock test asserting >1 yielded page and bounded accumulation.\n- No behavioral change for single-locator jobs.\n- ≥95% patch coverage.\n\n## References\nSurfaced by the source-side audit of a production deployment-targeted taps. Companion to the warehouse/lakehouse buffering issues #617–#621. Roadmap epic #38.","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"29dc44e1-5b7c-4b15-ab58-3c2a3224a49b","timestamp":"2026-09-18T08:38:09.657Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"- [No employer/use-case keywords in issues](feedback_no_employer_or_usecase_keywords_in_issues.md) — never \"Drivetrain\"/tenant ids/customer orgs/internal repos in public repo artifacts; generic industry framing; keep this rule OUT of tracked files.\n- [Stop asking, just execute](feedback_stop_asking_just_execute.md) — when a task lists N items, fix all N and report once; don't ask \"shall I continue?\" mid-list.\n════════ #619\nObject-store sources: `concurrency` is a no-op on the streaming path (serial object reads in real runs) + whole-object Parquet buffering\n\n## Summary\n\nFor the object-store sources (`s3`, `gcs`, `azure-blob`), the documented **`concurrency` config field is a no-op on the streaming path** — `stream_pages`/`stream_batches` (what `Pipeline::run` actually drives) read objects **strictly serially**; `concurrency` (`buffer_unordered`) is applied only in the deprecated eager `fetch_with_context`. So a many-object prefix/glob scan is single-flighted in every real run, despite the knob. Secondary: several formats buffer whole objects, and no source uses parallel byte-range downloads.\n\n## Motivation\n\n[Primary Goal — performance + correctness of the public API.] A documented performance knob that silently does nothing is both a perf loss and an API-correctness bug. Object-store reads of many objects are exactly where parallelism matters.\n\n## Findings\n\n- **s3 source — MED (bug).** `stream_pages` reads objects in a serial `for key in &keys` loop (`crates/source/s3/src/stream.rs:405`); columnar `stream_batches` serial (`:616`). `concurrency` (default 10) is used only in `fetch_with_context` (`:332-341`). JSONL streams line-by-line (good); `JsonArray`/`RawText` buffer whole object; **Parquet-on-S3 buffers the whole object** into `Bytes` + sync `ParquetRecordBatchReaderBuilder` (`:140-149,772-795`) instead of the async `ParquetObjectReader`.\n- **gcs source — MED (bug).** Identical: serial `stream_pages` (`crates/source/gcs/src/stream.rs:404`); concurrency only in eager path (`:345-363`); whole-object Parquet buffering.\n- **azure-blob source — MED (bug).** Serial `stream_pages` (`crates/source/azure-blob/src/stream.rs:261`); concurrency only in eager path (`:212-220`). Holds an `object_store` handle exposing `get_ranges` but doesn't use it.\n- **sftp source — MED.** Always serial (`crates/source/sftp/src/stream.rs:136`), **no `concurrency` field at all**; `raw_text`/`json_array` buffer whole file.\n- **parquet source — LOW (reference).** True `ParquetObjectReader` byte-range/row-group async streaming with projection pushdown; only minor serial multi-file streaming + serial up-front footer probes.\n\n## Proposed design\n\n1. **Make `concurrency` real on the streaming path:** run per-object readers through `buffer_unordered(concurrency)` in `stream_pages`/`stream_batches` (with a documented ordering caveat, or an `ordered` option). Add a `concurrency` field to the sftp source.\n2. **Stop buffering whole objects for Parquet-on-S3/GCS:** use the async `ParquetObjectReader` (as the parquet source does) for row-group streaming.\n3. **Optional (large single objects):** parallel byte-range download via `object_store::get_ranges` (azure/parquet already on `object_store`; s3/gcs would need a manual range reader over the vendor SDK).\n\n## Affected crates\n`crates/source/s3/`, `crates/source/gcs/`, `crates/source/azure-blob/`, `crates/source/sftp/`; reference `crates/source/parquet/src/stream.rs`.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n- `concurrency` demonstrably parallelizes object reads in `stream_pages` (a multi-object scan is faster with concurrency > 1); test asserts overlap.\n- Parquet-on-S3/GCS streams row groups (bounded memory), not whole-object.\n- sftp gains a working `concurrency` knob.\n- ≥95% patch coverage.\n\n## References\nSurfaced by the connector audit. Related: object-store sink issue (companion). Roadmap epic #38.\n\n════════ #620\nLakehouse sinks: delta `target_file_size` dead knob + O(whole-table) bulk memory; coalesce CDC commits (delta/iceberg)\n\n## Summary\n\nTwo lakehouse-sink issues surfaced by the audit. (1) **delta**: the `target_file_size` config knob is validated but **never wired** into the writer, and a **bulk load buffers the entire dataset in memory** (it only commits at end-of-run because bulk sources emit no bookmarks) → O(whole-table) peak memory + reliance on delta-rs's default file sizing. (2) **Both delta and iceberg**: a **CDC/streaming source** (one bookmark per transaction) forces **one commit + data file set per transaction** → many tiny snapshots/files → the small-files problem that bloats table metadata and degrades all downstream reads.\n\nNote: neither sink commits per *page* (both accumulate across pages and commit on bookmark-carrying pages, `crates/core/src/pipeline.rs:1715/1768/1849`), so **bulk/query pipelines are fine** (iceberg is already the good post-BigQuery-fix shape: 256 MB rolling files, one snapshot/run). The exposure is CDC feeds + delta's memory.\n\n## Motivation\n\n[Primary Goal — reliability/performance.] A bulk delta load can OOM on a large table (whole dataset buffered). CDC→lakehouse is a core streaming use case, and per-transaction snapshots are a well-known table-format anti-pattern (metadata bloat, slow query planning).\n\n## Findings\n\n- **delta — HIGH.** `RecordBatchWriter::for_table(table)` built with no `WriterConfig`/target size (`crates/sink/delta/src/sink.rs:119`); `target_file_size` declared+validated (`config.rs:59,87`) but never referenced → **dead knob**. Writer buffers parquet in memory until `flush_and_commit` (`sink.rs:335-338`); bulk sources never emit a bookmark, so the only flush is end-of-run → **peak memory ≈ O(whole dataset)**. CDC → one commit + file(s) per transaction → small files + `_delta_log` bloat. (`deltalake` 0.32.4.)\n- **iceberg — LOW bulk / HIGH CDC.** `RollingFileWriter` rolls data files to storage at `target_file_size_mb` (default 256, wired at `writer.rs:95-103`) → O(target) memory, one `fast_append` snapshot/run for bulk (good). But CDC (bookmark-per-txn, and the EO-eligible class) → one snapshot + ≥1 file per transaction, each with manifest/manifest-list churn (`sink.rs:424,448`). (`iceberg` 0.10.0 — note stale \"0.9.1\" comments at `sink.rs:20,373` and in `.claude/rules/architecture.md`.)\n\n## Proposed design\n\n1. **delta: wire `target_file_size`** into the delta-rs writer (`WriterConfig`/writer-properties) so files roll at the configured threshold; add a **size/row-based intermediate flush+commit** for bulk so memory is bounded to O(target_file_size) (accept a few commits/run).\n2. **Both: coalesce commits across CDC transactions** — commit every N transactions / accumulated bytes / time window instead of one-commit-per-bookmark, carrying the **highest bookmark/commit-token** for the coalesced group (must reconcile with per-transaction durability + exactly-once token model).\n3. Fix the stale `iceberg-rust 0.9.1` → `0.10.0` version references.\n\n## Affected crates\n`crates/sink/delta/`, `crates/sink/iceberg/`; the flush/bookmark cadence lives in `crates/core/src/pipeline.rs` — a shared \"commit coalescing\" option (every N/bytes/time) is the cleanest home.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n- A bulk delta load of a large table has bounded peak memory (~target_file_size), not O(whole table); `target_file_size` actually controls output file size.\n- A CDC run of N small transactions produces far fewer than N snapshots/commits (coalesced), with correct resume/EO semantics.\n- ≥95% patch coverage; version comments corrected.\n\n## References\nCompanion to the BigQuery/warehouse sink work (#612/#614). Surfaced by the connector audit. Roadmap epic #38.\n\n════════ #609\nTemplate register accepts a wrongly-formatted connector config (only caught at run time)\n\n## Summary\n`faucet template register` (and `faucet validate`) accept a template whose **connector `config` is structurally invalid**. The bad config passes registration, the template **launches**, and the error only surfaces when a run is triggered. We should **detect a wrongly-formatted template at register/validate time** and reject it with a clear, per-node error.\n\n## Repro (real)\nA `test-mssql` template was registered with the connector block nested wrong:\n```yaml\npipeline:\n  sources:\n    default:\n      type: mssql\n      config:\n        connection:                     # WRONG — MssqlConnectionConfig is #[serde(flatten)]\n          connection_string: \"REDACTED;\"\n        query: \"SELECT * FROM ${param.schema}.${param.table}\"\n```\n- `POST /v1/templates` → **201 Created**, and it **launched** fine.\n- First triggered run → **failed**: `Config error: MSSQL config requires either connection_url or connection_string`.\n\nThe correct shape is flattened (`config: { connection_string: … }`). Registration never noticed the mistake.\n\n## Root cause\n`cli/src/templates/store.rs::register` validates via `topology::validate_topology_spec` **or** `expand::expand(&cfg)` + `compile_transforms(node.transforms)` per row. **None of these deserialize the connector `config` into its typed struct** or otherwise check the connector's schema — the `config` stays an opaque `serde_json::Value` until `registry::build_source`/`build_sink` runs at execution time. So any connector-config shape error slips through:\n- wrong nesting / flattening (the mssql case above),\n- unknown field under `deny_unknown_fields`,\n- wrong scalar type, missing required field,\n- typo'd field name.\n\n`faucet validate` has the same blind spot (it also only expands).\n\n## Proposed design\nAdd a **non-connecting config validation** step run at register and in `faucet validate`:\n- New registry hooks `registry::validate_source_config(kind, &Value) -> CliResult<()>` / `validate_sink_config(kind, &Value)` (feature-gated per connector) that **deserialize** the `config` `Value` into the connector's typed config (serde, honoring `deny_unknown_fields`) and run its `validate()` if present — **without** building clients/pools or opening connections.\n- Call them per node in `store::register` (on the placeholder-bound copy already used for structural validation, so `${param.*}` / `${env:}` / `${file:}` don't block deserialize) and in the `validate` command.\n- Connectors **not compiled into this build** can't be checked → report `cannot validate <kind>: feature not compiled` (warn or hard-fail behind a flag), never a silent pass.\n\nDeserialize-only keeps it fast and credential-free; live connectivity stays the job of `faucet doctor`.\n\n## Affected crates / files\n- `cli/src/registry.rs` (new `validate_source_config` / `validate_sink_config` dispatchers, per-connector arms)\n- `cli/src/templates/store.rs` (invoke in `register`)\n- `cli/src/commands/validate.rs` / `cli/src/expand.rs` (invoke in `faucet validate`)\n- connector config structs already derive `Deserialize` + often have `validate()` — reused as-is\n\n## Edge cases & failure modes\n════════ #623\nREST source: Salesforce Bulk / async_job buffers the entire result set into one page (O(whole extract) memory, non-resumable)\n\n## Summary\n\n`faucet-source-rest`'s async-job path (`async_job:` — used for Salesforce **Bulk API**, the `test-salesforce` tap) **buffers the entire result set into memory and yields it as a single `StreamPage`**. `run_async_job` (`crates/source/rest/src/stream.rs:1043-1160`) submits → polls → then loops over the Salesforce result-set locators (`Sforce-Locator`, #557) doing `all_records.extend(records)` across every locator page (`stream.rs:1147`), and `stream_pages_inner` short-circuits with a single `yield StreamPage { records, bookmark: None }` for the whole job (`stream.rs:512-515`). So a large Bulk extract is fully materialized as one giant `Vec<Value>` — **O(whole extract) peak memory**, no per-page flush, and (because `bookmark: None`) no intermediate progress/bookmark, so a crash loses the entire run.\n\nThis is the REST analog of the warehouse/lakehouse buffer-everything issues (#620 etc.) and the opposite of the correctly-streamed paginated/OData REST paths (which are O(one API page)).\n\n## Motivation\n\n[Primary Goal — reliability/efficiency.] Salesforce is one of the targeted production taps, and Bulk API is exactly the path used for *large* objects (that's why Bulk exists). Holding the whole result in RAM caps extract size by memory and makes the run non-resumable.\n\n## Findings\n\n- `stream_pages_inner` async-job short-circuit: single yield for the whole job — `crates/source/rest/src/stream.rs:512-515`.\n- `run_async_job` accumulator: `all_records.extend(records)` over all locator pages, returns one `Vec` — `stream.rs:1129-1158` (extend at `:1147`).\n- Each locator page is itself whole-body (`resp.bytes()` → `parse_fetch_page`, `stream.rs:1023,1166-1204`) — fine per page, but they're concatenated, not streamed.\n- Contrast: the paginated/OData data path streams per page with no accumulator (`stream.rs:624-780`) — the correct shape.\n\n## Proposed design\n\nConvert `run_async_job` from \"accumulate then return `Vec`\" into a **generator that yields each locator/fetch page as its own `StreamPage`** as it is downloaded, mirroring the paginated loop:\n- yield each locator page's parsed records immediately; only advance to the next `Sforce-Locator` after yielding;\n- carry a resumable bookmark where the API allows (the locator/job id + offset), so a crash can resume mid-job (or at least emit progress);\n- keep the submit/poll lifecycle unchanged.\n\nPeak memory then drops to O(one locator page). This is the highest-value source-side change for the 9 REST taps.\n\n## Affected crates / files\n`crates/source/rest/src/stream.rs` (`run_async_job`, the `async_job` branch of `stream_pages_inner`).\n\n## Edge cases\n- Empty result / zero locators (yield nothing, clean finish).\n- Poll timeout / job failure (unchanged error handling).\n- CSV vs JSON vs Excel fetch-page formats (`parse_fetch_page`) — stream each regardless of format.\n- Resume semantics: if the Bulk job id expires, fall back to full re-run.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n- A multi-locator Bulk extract yields multiple `StreamPage`s (one per locator/fetch page), peak memory O(one page), verified by a wiremock test asserting >1 yielded page and bounded accumulation.\n- No behavioral change for single-locator jobs.\n- ≥95% patch coverage.\n\n## References\nSurfaced by the source-side audit of a production deployment-targeted taps. Companion to the warehouse/lakehouse buffering issues #617–#621. Roadmap epic #38.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"690d7b72-0134-4d33-b5f1-e6ae08e6edeb","session_id":"55398c64-7c65-40db-9b68-886111a86d7f","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/c-sikawats/Documents/GitHub/Personal/faucet-stream","sessionId":"55398c64-7c65-40db-9b68-886111a86d7f","version":"2.1.267","gitBranch":"fix/overwrite-bookmark-ordering","slug":"linear-noodling-leaf"}
