{"parentUuid":"383850f9-0d45-406a-99fc-b1a4403c0c62","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"0b270ed8-79ad-458f-b783-66e7a379786e","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01SApuRiGnWfFvjYrHTBCUE3","type":"tool_result","content":"19\n--- README mentions\ncrates/source/s3/README.md:16:- **Parallel object reads** — up to `concurrency` objects fetched at once via `futures::buffer_unordered` (default 10).\ncrates/source/s3/README.md:60:      concurrency: 16\ncrates/source/s3/README.md:89:| `concurrency` | int | `10` | Maximum number of objects fetched concurrently. Clamped to `≥ 1` at runtime. |\ncrates/source/s3/README.md:204:      concurrency: 32\ncrates/source/s3/README.md:246:> **Memory ceiling — `raw_text` / `json_array`.** Both formats hold one whole decoded object in memory at a time (inherent: `raw_text`'s record *is* the file, and a JSON array isn't valid until its closing `]`). Because objects are fetched concurrently, peak memory is roughly **`concurrency` × (largest object's decoded size)**, not `batch_size`. For large `raw_text` / `json_array` objects, lower `concurrency` to cap peak memory, or re-emit the data as `json_lines` upstream so it streams line-by-line.\ncrates/source/s3/README.md:340:        .concurrency(20)\ncrates/source/s3/README.md:356:- **Parallel reads** — listed keys are read through `futures::stream::buffer_unordered(concurrency)`, so up to `concurrency` `GetObject` calls are in flight at once.\ncrates/source/s3/README.md:359:Throughput scales with `concurrency` and is ultimately bounded by S3 / network bandwidth — benchmark with your own object sizes and parallelism.\ncrates/source/s3/README.md:395:| Out-of-memory on large `raw_text` / `json_array` objects | These formats buffer a whole object; peak memory ≈ `concurrency` × largest object. Lower `concurrency`, or re-emit as `json_lines`. |\ncrates/source/gcs/README.md:17:- **Concurrent reads** — objects are fetched in parallel via `buffer_unordered(concurrency)` (default 10), so wall-clock time is bounded by your slowest objects, not their sum.\ncrates/source/gcs/README.md:81:| `concurrency` | int | `10` | Maximum concurrent object reads. Higher = faster on many small objects; lower caps peak memory for large `raw_text` / `json_array` objects. |\ncrates/source/gcs/README.md:151:    concurrency: 20\ncrates/source/gcs/README.md:184:    concurrency: 4       # raw_text holds a whole object in memory — keep concurrency low\ncrates/source/gcs/README.md:211:> **Memory ceiling — `raw_text` / `json_array`.** Both hold one whole decoded object in memory at a time (inherent: a raw-text record *is* the whole file, and a JSON array isn't valid until its closing `]`). Because objects are fetched concurrently, peak memory is bounded by roughly **`concurrency` × (largest object's decoded size)**, not by `batch_size`. For large `raw_text` / `json_array` objects, lower `concurrency` to cap peak memory, or re-emit the data as `json_lines` upstream so it streams at `O(batch_size)`.\ncrates/source/gcs/README.md:310:    .concurrency(20)\ncrates/source/gcs/README.md:324:4. Objects are read concurrently via `buffer_unordered(concurrency)` and parsed per `file_format`.\ncrates/source/gcs/README.md:360:| Out-of-memory on large `raw_text` / `json_array` objects | These formats hold a whole object in memory and peak at ~`concurrency × largest-object size`. Lower `concurrency`, cap with `max_objects`, or re-emit the data as `json_lines`. |\ncrates/source/azure-blob/README.md:33:| `concurrency` | int | `10` | Max concurrent object reads. |","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"49b58fce-62f9-45b4-bcc6-8b071894131a","timestamp":"2026-09-18T10:03:12.693Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"19\n--- README mentions\ncrates/source/s3/README.md:16:- **Parallel object reads** — up to `concurrency` objects fetched at once via `futures::buffer_unordered` (default 10).\ncrates/source/s3/README.md:60:      concurrency: 16\ncrates/source/s3/README.md:89:| `concurrency` | int | `10` | Maximum number of objects fetched concurrently. Clamped to `≥ 1` at runtime. |\ncrates/source/s3/README.md:204:      concurrency: 32\ncrates/source/s3/README.md:246:> **Memory ceiling — `raw_text` / `json_array`.** Both formats hold one whole decoded object in memory at a time (inherent: `raw_text`'s record *is* the file, and a JSON array isn't valid until its closing `]`). Because objects are fetched concurrently, peak memory is roughly **`concurrency` × (largest object's decoded size)**, not `batch_size`. For large `raw_text` / `json_array` objects, lower `concurrency` to cap peak memory, or re-emit the data as `json_lines` upstream so it streams line-by-line.\ncrates/source/s3/README.md:340:        .concurrency(20)\ncrates/source/s3/README.md:356:- **Parallel reads** — listed keys are read through `futures::stream::buffer_unordered(concurrency)`, so up to `concurrency` `GetObject` calls are in flight at once.\ncrates/source/s3/README.md:359:Throughput scales with `concurrency` and is ultimately bounded by S3 / network bandwidth — benchmark with your own object sizes and parallelism.\ncrates/source/s3/README.md:395:| Out-of-memory on large `raw_text` / `json_array` objects | These formats buffer a whole object; peak memory ≈ `concurrency` × largest object. Lower `concurrency`, or re-emit as `json_lines`. |\ncrates/source/gcs/README.md:17:- **Concurrent reads** — objects are fetched in parallel via `buffer_unordered(concurrency)` (default 10), so wall-clock time is bounded by your slowest objects, not their sum.\ncrates/source/gcs/README.md:81:| `concurrency` | int | `10` | Maximum concurrent object reads. Higher = faster on many small objects; lower caps peak memory for large `raw_text` / `json_array` objects. |\ncrates/source/gcs/README.md:151:    concurrency: 20\ncrates/source/gcs/README.md:184:    concurrency: 4       # raw_text holds a whole object in memory — keep concurrency low\ncrates/source/gcs/README.md:211:> **Memory ceiling — `raw_text` / `json_array`.