{"parentUuid":"20ef6795-a0a8-4338-8e21-a736388a847f","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CfAiiRPHDg6RF6oBSe1j4","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_014NFYAz3WHTGPEuVAtJ4w2E","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport pathlib\n\ndef edit(path, pairs):\n    p = pathlib.Path(path); s = p.read_text()\n    for old, new in pairs:\n        assert old in s, (path, old[:70])\n        s = s.replace(old, new, 1)\n    p.write_text(s)\n    print(\"updated\", path)\n\nedit(\"crates/source/s3/README.md\", [\n (\"- **Parallel object reads** — up to `concurrency` objects fetched at once via `futures::buffer_unordered` (default 10).\",\n  \"- **Parallel object reads** — up to `concurrency` objects fetched at once (default 10), on the streaming path as well as the batch one. The streaming prefetch is *ordered*, so records still arrive in listing order and a failing object is still blamed at its own position.\"),\n (\"- **Parallel reads** — listed keys are read through `futures::stream::buffer_unordered(concurrency)`, so up to `concurrency` `GetObject` calls are in flight at once.\",\n  \"\"\"- **Parallel reads** — `stream_pages` fetches listed keys through an ordered `futures::stream::buffered(concurrency)` look-ahead, so up to `concurrency` `GetObject` calls are in flight while the current object is being decoded; the eager batch path uses `buffer_unordered(concurrency)`. `concurrency: 0` is clamped to 1.\n- **What the look-ahead holds** — for `json_lines` it is only the open body readers (opening an object does not download it), so peak memory stays `O(batch_size)`; for `json_array` / `raw_text` / `parquet` it is up to `concurrency` whole object bodies, which is the memory bound the knob advertises.\"\"\"),\n])\n\nedit(\"crates/source/gcs/README.md\", [\n (\"- **Concurrent reads** — objects are fetched in parallel via `buffer_unordered(concurrency)` (default 10), so wall-clock time is bounded by your slowest objects, not their sum.\",\n  \"- **Concurrent reads** — objects are fetched in parallel (default 10) on the streaming path as well as the batch one, so wall-clock time is bounded by your slowest objects, not their sum. The streaming prefetch is *ordered*, so records still arrive in listing order and a failing object is still blamed at its own position.\"),\n (\"4. Objects are read concurrently via `buffer_unordered(concurrency)` and parsed per `file_format`.\",\n  \"\"\"4. Objects are read concurrently — `stream_pages` uses an ordered `buffered(concurrency)` look-ahead (so up to `concurrency` reads overlap the current object's decode), the eager batch path uses `buffer_unordered(concurrency)` — and parsed per `file_format`. `concurrency: 0` is clamped to 1. For `json_lines` the look-ahead holds only open body readers, keeping peak memory `O(batch_size)`; for `json_array` / `raw_text` / `parquet` it holds up to `concurrency` whole bodies.\"\"\"),\n])\n\nedit(\"crates/source/azure-blob/README.md\", [\n (\"| `concurrency` | int | `10` | Max concurrent object reads. |\",\n  \"| `concurrency` | int | `10` | Max concurrent object reads, on the streaming path as well as the batch one. The streaming prefetch is ordered, so records stay in listing order; `0` is clamped to 1. For `json_lines` it overlaps only the request setup (peak memory stays `O(batch_size)`); for `json_array` / `raw_text` up to `concurrency` whole bodies are resident. |\"),\n])\nPY","description":"Update s3/gcs/azure READMEs"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3358,"cache_read_input_tokens":254347,"output_tokens":1317,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3358,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":1317,"cache_read_input_tokens":254347,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3358,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3358},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_014NFYAz3WHTGPEuVAtJ4w2E":{"command":"cd /Users/c-sikawats/Documents/GitHub/Personal/faucet-stream && python3 - <<'PY'\nimport pathlib\n\ndef edit(path, pairs):\n    p = pathlib.Path(path); s = p.read_text()\n    for old, new in pairs:\n        assert old in s, (path, old[:70])\n        s = s.replace(old, new, 1)\n    p.write_text(s)\n    print(\"updated\", path)\n\nedit(\"crates/source/s3/README.md\", [\n (\"- **Parallel object reads** — up to `concurrency` objects fetched at once via `futures::buffer_unordered` (default 10).\",\n  \"- **Parallel object reads** — up to `concurrency` objects fetched at once (default 10), on the streaming path as well as the batch one. The streaming prefetch is *ordered*, so records still arrive in listing order and a failing object is still blamed at its own position.\"),\n (\"- **Parallel reads** — listed keys are read through `futures::stream::buffer_unordered(concurrency)`, so up to `concurrency` `GetObject` calls are in flight at once.\",\n  \"\"\"- **Parallel reads** — `stream_pages` fetches listed keys through an ordered `futures::stream::buffered(concurrency)` look-ahead, so up to `concurrency` `GetObject` calls are in flight while the current object is being decoded; the eager batch path uses `buffer_unordered(concurrency)`. `concurrency: 0` is clamped to 1.\n- **What the look-ahead holds** — for `json_lines` it is only the open body readers (opening an object does not download it), so peak memory stays `O(batch_size)`; for `json_array` / `raw_text` / `parquet` it is up to `concurrency` whole object bodies, which is the memory bound the knob advertises.\"\"\"),\n])\n\nedit(\"crates/source/gcs/README.md\", [\n (\"- **Concurrent reads** — objects are fetched in parallel via `buffer_unordered(concurrency)` (default 10), so wall-clock time is bounded by your slowest objects, not their sum.\",\n  \"- **Concurrent reads** — objects are fetched in parallel (default 10) on the streaming path as well as the batch one, so wall-clock time is bounded by your slowest objects, not their sum. The streaming prefetch is *ordered*, so records still arrive in listing order and a failing object is still blamed at its own position.\"),\n (\"4. Objects are read concurrently via `buffer_unordered(concurrency)` and parsed per `file_format`.\",\n  \"\"\"4. Objects are read concurrently — `stream_pages` uses an ordered `buffered(concurrency)` look-ahead (so up to `concurrency` reads overlap the current object's decode), the eager batch path uses `buffer_unordered(concurrency)` — and parsed per `file_format`. `concurrency: 0` is clamped to 1. For `json_lines` the look-ahead holds only open body readers, keeping peak memory `O(batch_size)`; for `json_array` / `raw_text` / `parquet` it holds up to `concurrency` whole bodies.\"\"\"),\n])\n\nedit(\"crates/source/azure-blob/README.md\", [\n (\"| `concurrency` | int | `10` | Max concurrent object reads. |\",\n  \"| `concurrency` | int | `10` | Max concurrent object reads, on the streaming path as well as the batch one. The streaming prefetch is ordered, so records stay in listing order; `0` is clamped to 1. For `json_lines` it overlaps only the request setup (peak memory stays `O(batch_size)`); for `json_array` / `raw_text` up to `concurrency` whole bodies are resident. |\"),\n])\nPY","description":"Update s3/gcs/azure READMEs"}},"wireIngestContext":{"toolu_014NFYAz3WHTGPEuVAtJ4w2E":{"cwd":"/Users/c-sikawats/Documents/GitHub/Personal/faucet-stream"}},"apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011CfAiiQp3P83Yk22J8Toeu","type":"assistant","uuid":"d746c420-6710-4d70-af93-c3437af792a3","timestamp":"2026-09-18T10:08:01.419Z","effort":"high","perTurnEffort":null,"se