{"parentUuid":"b49f8f03-8719-432f-8578-4cf037764fba","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"0b270ed8-79ad-458f-b783-66e7a379786e","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01VgKPwNnHepZgHcETu5xcuX","type":"tool_result","content":"commit 124aafb32c017d08e8fb55fe52db32cd8d832b0d\nAuthor: PawanSikawat <pawansikawat1998@gmail.com>\nDate:   Fri Sep 18 15:40:03 2026 +0530\n\n    fix(sources): make `concurrency` real on the object-store streaming path\n    \n    `buffer_unordered(concurrency)` only ever existed in the eager\n    `fetch_with_context` batch path. `stream_pages` — what every real run\n    drives — read objects strictly one at a time, so s3 / gcs / azure-blob\n    accepted a documented `concurrency` knob and ignored it; the sftp source\n    had no such field at all.\n    \n    Each source now prefetches objects through an **ordered**\n    `buffered(concurrency)` look-ahead, so:\n    \n    - reads overlap (a 60-object scan is >2x faster at concurrency 15 —\n      asserted against MinIO, warm-up excluded so the comparison measures\n      overlap rather than client setup);\n    - pages still arrive in listing order, and a failing object is still\n      blamed at its own position, so concurrency changes throughput and\n      nothing an operator can observe;\n    - `concurrency: 0` is clamped to 1 rather than producing a `buffered(0)`\n      stream that yields nothing forever.\n    \n    What the look-ahead holds differs by format, and that difference is the\n    memory contract: `json_lines` prefetches only open body readers (opening\n    an object does not download it), so peak memory stays O(batch_size),\n    while `json_array` / `raw_text` / `parquet` hold up to `concurrency`\n    whole bodies — the bound the knob already advertised.\n    \n    sftp gains a `concurrency` field defaulting to 4 rather than 10: every\n    read shares one SSH channel and servers commonly cap concurrent handles.\n    `faucet-common-sftp` re-exports the open-file handle as `SftpFile` so the\n    source can name it without depending on `russh-sftp` directly.\n    \n    Closes #619\n    \n    Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n    Entire-Checkpoint: 01M2SZX8A0CGBT7RBPP5H3S01K\n\ndiff --git a/crates/source/s3/src/stream.rs b/crates/source/s3/src/stream.rs\nindex 399725e0..a3565a89 100644\n--- a/crates/source/s3/src/stream.rs\n+++ b/crates/source/s3/src/stream.rs\n@@ -11,6 +11,22 @@ use std::pin::Pin;\n use std::sync::Mutex;\n use tokio::io::AsyncBufReadExt;\n \n+/// One object's payload, fetched in the shape its `file_format` decodes from.\n+///\n+/// Prefetching these with a bounded look-ahead is what makes `concurrency`\n+/// real on the streaming path (#619). The bound it promises differs by format\n+/// and that difference is the point: `JsonLines` overlaps only the request\n+/// round-trip — `open_object_reader` does not download the body, so the decode\n+/// still streams line-by-line at O(batch) memory — while the whole-object\n+/// formats hold at most `concurrency` bodies at once.\n+enum Fetched {\n+    Lines(Pin<Box<dyn tokio::io::AsyncBufRead + Send + Unpin>>),\n+    RawText(String),\n+    JsonArray(String),\n+    #[cfg(feature = \"arrow\")]\n+    Parquet(bytes::Bytes),\n+}\n+\n /// An S3 source that lists and reads objects from a bucket.\n pub struct S3Source {\n     config: S3SourceConfig,\n@@ -157,7 +173,26 @@ impl S3Source {\n         &self,\n         key: &str,\n     ) -> Result<(arrow::datatypes::SchemaRef, Vec<arrow::array::RecordBatch>), FaucetError> {\n-        let data = self.read_object_bytes(key).await?;\n+        Self::decode_parquet(self.read_object_bytes(key).await?, key).await\n+    }\n+\n+    /// Fetch one object in the shape its configured `file_format` needs.\n+    async fn fetch(&self, key: &str) -> Result<Fetched, FaucetError> {\n+        Ok(match self.config.file_format {\n+            S3FileFormat::JsonLines => Fetched::Lines(self.open_object_reader(key).await?),\n+            S3FileFormat::RawText => Fetched::RawText(self.read_object_text(key).await?),\n+            S3FileFormat::JsonArray => Fetched::JsonArray(self.read_object_text(key).await?),\n+            #[cfg(feature = \"arrow\")]\n+            S3FileFormat::Parquet => Fetched::Parquet(self.read_object_bytes(key).await?),\n+        })\n+    }\n+\n+    /// Decode already-fetched Parquet bytes on a blocking thread.\n+    #[cfg(feature = \"arrow\")]\n+    async fn decode_parquet(\n+        data: bytes::Bytes,\n+        key: &str,\n+    ) -> Result<(arrow::datatypes::SchemaRef, Vec<arrow::array::RecordBatch>), FaucetError> {\n         let key_owned = key.to_string();\n         tokio::task::spawn_blocking(move || decode_parquet_bytes(data, &key_owned))\n             .await\n@@ -402,10 +437,24 @@ impl faucet_core::Source for S3Source {\n             let mut buffer: Vec<Value> = Vec::with_capacity(initial_capacity);\n             let mut total = 0usize;\n \n-            for key in &keys {\n-                match self.config.file_format {\n-                    S3FileFormat::JsonLines => {\n-                        let reader = self.open_object_reader(key).await?;\n+            // Overlap the object reads (#619). `buffered` keeps listing order,\n+            // so an object that fails surfaces at exactly the point it would\n+            // have serially — concurrency changes throughput, not semantics.\n+            let concurrency = self.config.concurrency.max(1);\n+            // The key is moved into each future rather than borrowed: a\n+            // closure returning a borrow-capturing async block is not\n+            // higher-ranked enough for `buffered`.\n+            let mut fetched = stream::iter(keys.iter().cloned())\n+                .map(|key| async move {\n+                    let payload = self.fetch(&key).await;\n+                    (key, payload)\n+                })\n+                .buffered(concurrency);\n+\n+            while let Some((key, payload)) = fetched.next().await {\n+                let key = &key;\n+                match payload? {","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"e0269416-66ec-45cc-aa15-62e4efce8bb6","timestamp":"2026-09-18T10:49:29.971Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"commit 124aafb32c017d08e8fb55fe52db32cd8d832b0d\nAuthor: PawanSikawat <pawansikawat1998@gmail.com>\nDate:   Fri Sep 18 15:40:03 2026 +0530\n\n    fix(sources): make `concurrency` real on the object-store streaming path\n    \n    `buffer_unordered(concurrency)` only ever existed in the eager\n    `fetch_with_context` batch path. `stream_pages` — what every real run\n    drives — read objects strictly one at a time, so s3 / gcs / azure-blob\n    accepted a documented `concurrency` knob and ignored it; the sftp source\n    had no such field at all.\n    \n    Each source now prefetches objects through an **ordered**\n    `buffered(concurrency)` look-ahead, so:\n    \n    - reads overlap (a 60-object scan is >2x faster at concurrency 15 —\n      asserted against MinIO, warm-up excluded so the comparison measures\n      overlap rather than client setup);\n    - pages still arrive in listing order, and a failing object is still\n      blamed at its own position, so concurrency changes throughput and\n      nothing an operator can observe;\n    - `concurrency: 0` is clamped to 1 rather than producing a `buffered(0)`\n      stream that yields nothing forever.\n    \n    What the look-ahead holds differs by format, and that difference is the\n    memory contract: `json_lines` prefetches only open body readers (opening\n    an object does not download it), so peak memory stays O(batch_size),\n    while `json_array` / `raw_text` / `parquet` hold up to `concurrency`\n    whole bodies — the bound the knob already advertised.\n    \n    sftp gains a `concurrency` field defaulting to 4 rather than 10: every\n    read shares one SSH channel and servers commonly cap concurrent handles.\n    `faucet-common-sftp` re-exports the open-file handle as `SftpFile` so the\n    source can name it without depending on `russh-sftp` directly.\n    \n    Closes #619\n    \n    Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n    Entire-Checkpoint: 01M2SZX8A0CGBT7RBPP5H3S01K\n\ndiff --git a/crates/source/s3/src/stream.rs b/crates/source/s3/src/stream.rs\nindex 399725e0..a3565a89 100644\n--- a/crates/source/s3/src/stream.rs\n+++ b/crates/source/s3/src/stream.rs\n@@ -11,6 +11,22 @@ use std::pin::Pin;\n use std::sync::Mutex;\n use tokio::io::AsyncBufReadExt;\n \n+/// One object's payload, fetched in the shape its `file_format` decodes from.\n+///\n+/// Prefetching these with a bounded look-ahead is what makes `concurrency`\n+/// real on the streaming path (#619). The bound it promises differs by format\n+/// and that difference is the point: `JsonLines` overlaps only the request\n+/// round-trip — `open_object_reader` does not download the body, so the decode\n+/// still streams line-by-line at O(batch) memory — while the whole-object\n+/// formats hold at most `concurrency` bodies at once.\n+enum Fetched {\n+    Lines(Pin<Box<dyn tokio::io::AsyncBufRead + Send + Unpin>>),\n+    RawText(String),\n+    JsonArray(String),\n+    #[cfg(feature = \"arrow\")]\n+    Parquet(bytes::Bytes),\n+}\n+\n /// An S3 source that lists and reads objects from a bucket.\n pub struct S3Source {\n     config: S3SourceConfig,\n@@ -157,7 +173,26 @@ impl S3Source {\n         &self,\n         key: &str,\n     ) -> Result<(arrow::datatypes::SchemaRef, Vec<arrow::array::RecordBatch>), FaucetError> {\n-        let data = self.read_object_bytes(key).await?;\n+        Self::decode_parquet(self.read_object_bytes(key).await?, key).await\n+    }\n+\n+    /// Fetch one object in the shape its configured `file_format` needs.\n+    async fn fetch(&self, key: &str) -> Result<Fetched, FaucetError> {\n+        Ok(match self.config.file_format {\n+            S3FileFormat::JsonLines => Fetched::Lines(self.open_object_reader(key).await?),\n+            S3FileFormat::RawText => Fetched::RawText(self.read_object_text(key).await?),\n+            S3FileFormat::JsonArray => Fetched::JsonArray(self.read_object_text(key).await?),\n+            #[cfg(feature = \"arrow\")]\n+            S3FileFormat::Parquet => Fetched::Parquet(self.read_object_bytes(key).await?),\n+        })\n+    }\n+\n+    /// Decode already-fetched Parquet bytes on a blocking thread.\n+    #[cfg(feature = \"arrow\")]\n+    async fn decode_parquet(\n+        data: bytes::Bytes,\n+        key: &str,\n+    ) -> Result<(arrow::datatypes::SchemaRef, Vec<arrow::array::RecordBatch>), FaucetError> {\n         let key_owned = key.to_string();\n         tokio::task::spawn_blocking(move || decode_parquet_bytes(data, &key_owned))\n             .await\n@@ -402,10 +437,24 @@ impl faucet_core::Source for S3Source {\n             let mut buffer: Vec<Value> = Vec::with_capacity(initial_capacity);\n             let mut total = 0usize;\n \n-            for key in &keys {\n-                match self.config.file_format {\n-                    S3FileFormat::JsonLines => {\n-                        let reader = self.open_object_reader(key).await?;\n+            // Overlap the object reads (#619). `buffered` keeps listing order,\n+            // so an object that fails surfaces at exactly the point it would\n+            // have serially — concurrency changes throughput, not semantics.\n+            let concurrency = self.config.concurrency.max(1);\n+            // The key is moved into each future rather than borrowed: a\n+            // closure returning a borrow-capturing async block is not\n+            // higher-ranked enough for `buffered`.\n+            let mut fetched = stream::iter(keys.iter().cloned())\n+                .map(|key| async move {\n+                    let payload = self.fetch(&key).await;\n+                    (key, payload)\n+                })\n+                .buffered(concurrency);\n+\n+            while let Some((key, payload)) = fetched.next().await {\n+                let key = &key;\n+                match payload? {","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"b49f8f03-8719-432f-8578-4cf037764fba","session_id":"55398c64-7c65-40db-9b68-886111a86d7f","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/c-sikawats/Documents/GitHub/Personal/faucet-stream","sessionId":"55398c64-7c65-40db-9b68-886111a86d7f","version":"2.1.267","gitBranch":"fix/inert-config-knobs","slug":"linear-noodling-leaf"}
