{"parentUuid":"d8937043-6dcf-4912-b381-e322f1f0f1e0","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"0b270ed8-79ad-458f-b783-66e7a379786e","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_019gv4z4mcTUSfyiDStEYDJh","type":"tool_result","content":"## Summary\n\nFor the object-store sources (`s3`, `gcs`, `azure-blob`), the documented **`concurrency` config field is a no-op on the streaming path** — `stream_pages`/`stream_batches` (what `Pipeline::run` actually drives) read objects **strictly serially**; `concurrency` (`buffer_unordered`) is applied only in the deprecated eager `fetch_with_context`. So a many-object prefix/glob scan is single-flighted in every real run, despite the knob. Secondary: several formats buffer whole objects, and no source uses parallel byte-range downloads.\n\n## Motivation\n\n[Primary Goal — performance + correctness of the public API.] A documented performance knob that silently does nothing is both a perf loss and an API-correctness bug. Object-store reads of many objects are exactly where parallelism matters.\n\n## Findings\n\n- **s3 source — MED (bug).** `stream_pages` reads objects in a serial `for key in &keys` loop (`crates/source/s3/src/stream.rs:405`); columnar `stream_batches` serial (`:616`). `concurrency` (default 10) is used only in `fetch_with_context` (`:332-341`). JSONL streams line-by-line (good); `JsonArray`/`RawText` buffer whole object; **Parquet-on-S3 buffers the whole object** into `Bytes` + sync `ParquetRecordBatchReaderBuilder` (`:140-149,772-795`) instead of the async `ParquetObjectReader`.\n- **gcs source — MED (bug).** Identical: serial `stream_pages` (`crates/source/gcs/src/stream.rs:404`); concurrency only in eager path (`:345-363`); whole-object Parquet buffering.\n- **azure-blob source — MED (bug).** Serial `stream_pages` (`crates/source/azure-blob/src/stream.rs:261`); concurrency only in eager path (`:212-220`). Holds an `object_store` handle exposing `get_ranges` but doesn't use it.\n- **sftp source — MED.** Always serial (`crates/source/sftp/src/stream.rs:136`), **no `concurrency` field at all**; `raw_text`/`json_array` buffer whole file.\n- **parquet source — LOW (reference).** True `ParquetObjectReader` byte-range/row-group async streaming with projection pushdown; only minor serial multi-file streaming + serial up-front footer probes.\n\n## Proposed design\n\n1. **Make `concurrency` real on the streaming path:** run per-object readers through `buffer_unordered(concurrency)` in `stream_pages`/`stream_batches` (with a documented ordering caveat, or an `ordered` option). Add a `concurrency` field to the sftp source.\n2. **Stop buffering whole objects for Parquet-on-S3/GCS:** use the async `ParquetObjectReader` (as the parquet source does) for row-group streaming.\n3. **Optional (large single objects):** parallel byte-range download via `object_store::get_ranges` (azure/parquet already on `object_store`; s3/gcs would need a manual range reader over the vendor SDK).\n\n## Affected crates\n`crates/source/s3/`, `crates/source/gcs/`, `crates/source/azure-blob/`, `crates/source/sftp/`; reference `crates/source/parquet/src/stream.rs`.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n- `concurrency` demonstrably parallelizes object reads in `stream_pages` (a multi-object scan is faster with concurrency > 1); test asserts overlap.\n- Parquet-on-S3/GCS streams row groups (bounded memory), not whole-object.\n- sftp gains a working `concurrency` knob.\n- ≥95% patch coverage.\n\n## References\nSurfaced by the connector audit. Related: object-store sink issue (companion). Roadmap epic #38.","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"f3db9161-3b6f-40ba-ae0f-fecad00ba84f","timestamp":"2026-09-18T11:00:10.995Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"## Summary\n\nFor the object-store sources (`s3`, `gcs`, `azure-blob`), the documented **`concurrency` config field is a no-op on the streaming path** — `stream_pages`/`stream_batches` (what `Pipeline::run` actually drives) read objects **strictly serially**; `concurrency` (`buffer_unordered`) is applied only in the deprecated eager `fetch_with_context`. So a many-object prefix/glob scan is single-flighted in every real run, despite the knob. Secondary: several formats buffer whole objects, and no source uses parallel byte-range downloads.\n\n## Motivation\n\n[Primary Goal — performance + correctness of the public API.] A documented performance knob that silently does nothing is both a perf loss and an API-correctness bug. Object-store reads of many objects are exactly where parallelism matters.\n\n## Findings\n\n- **s3 source — MED (bug).** `stream_pages` reads objects in a serial `for key in &keys` loop (`crates/source/s3/src/stream.rs:405`); columnar `stream_batches` serial (`:616`). `concurrency` (default 10) is used only in `fetch_with_context` (`:332-341`). JSONL streams line-by-line (good); `JsonArray`/`RawText` buffer whole object; **Parquet-on-S3 buffers the whole object** into `Bytes` + sync `ParquetRecordBatchReaderBuilder` (`:140-149,772-795`) instead of the async `ParquetObjectReader`.\n- **gcs source — MED (bug).** Identical: serial `stream_pages` (`crates/source/gcs/src/stream.rs:404`); concurrency only in eager path (`:345-363`); whole-object Parquet buffering.\n- **azure-blob source — MED (bug).** Serial `stream_pages` (`crates/source/azure-blob/src/stream.rs:261`); concurrency only in eager path (`:212-220`). Holds an `object_store` handle exposing `get_ranges` but doesn't use it.\n- **sftp source — MED.** Always serial (`crates/source/sftp/src/stream.rs:136`), **no `concurrency` field at all**; `raw_text`/`json_array` buffer whole file.\n- **parquet source — LOW (reference).** True `ParquetObjectReader` byte-range/row-group async streaming with projection pushdown; only minor serial multi-file streaming + serial up-front footer probes.\n\n## Proposed design\n\n1. **Make `concurrency` real on the streaming path:** run per-object readers through `buffer_unordered(concurrency)` in `stream_pages`/`stream_batches` (with a documented ordering caveat, or an `ordered` option). Add a `concurrency` field to the sftp source.\n2. **Stop buffering whole objects for Parquet-on-S3/GCS:** use the async `ParquetObjectReader` (as the parquet source does) for row-group streaming.\n3. **Optional (large single objects):** parallel byte-range download via `object_store::get_ranges` (azure/parquet already on `object_store`; s3/gcs would need a manual range reader over the vendor SDK).\n\n## Affected crates\n`crates/source/s3/`, `crates/source/gcs/`, `crates/source/azure-blob/`, `crates/source/sftp/`; reference `crates/source/parquet/src/stream.rs`.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n- `concurrency` demonstrably parallelizes object reads in `stream_pages` (a multi-object scan is faster with concurrency > 1); test asserts overlap.\n- Parquet-on-S3/GCS streams row groups (bounded memory), not whole-object.\n- sftp gains a working `concurrency` knob.\n- ≥95% patch coverage.\n\n## References\nSurfaced by the connector audit. Related: object-store sink issue (companion). Roadmap epic #38.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"d8937043-6dcf-4912-b381-e322f1f0f1e0","session_id":"55398c64-7c65-40db-9b68-886111a86d7f","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/c-sikawats/Documents/GitHub/Personal/faucet-stream","sessionId":"55398c64-7c65-40db-9b68-886111a86d7f","version":"2.1.267","gitBranch":"fix/inert-config-knobs","slug":"linear-noodling-leaf"}
