{"parentUuid":"cdf0ae85-05bb-4327-b546-b8bd7996c33f","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"0b270ed8-79ad-458f-b783-66e7a379786e","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_015w1PudT8bBRBbEdw4PdFiE","type":"tool_result","content":"crates/source/s3/README.md:357:- **What the look-ahead holds** — for `json_lines` it is only the open body readers (opening an object does not download it), so peak memory stays `O(batch_size)`; for `json_array` / `raw_text` / `parquet` it is up to `concurrency` whole object bodies, which is the memory bound the knob advertises.\ncrates/source/s3/README.md:246:> **Memory ceiling — `raw_text` / `json_array`.** Both formats hold one whole decoded object in memory at a time (inherent: `raw_text`'s record *is* the file, and a JSON array isn't valid until its closing `]`). Because objects are fetched concurrently, peak memory is roughly **`concurrency` × (largest object's decoded size)**, not `batch_size`. For large `raw_text` / `json_array` objects, lower `concurrency` to cap peak memory, or re-emit the data as `json_lines` upstream so it streams line-by-line.\ncrates/source/gcs/README.md:211:> **Memory ceiling — `raw_text` / `json_array`.** Both hold one whole decoded object in memory at a time (inherent: a raw-text record *is* the whole file, and a JSON array isn't valid until its closing `]`). Because objects are fetched concurrently, peak memory is bounded by roughly **`concurrency` × (largest object's decoded size)**, not by `batch_size`. For large `raw_text` / `json_array` objects, lower `concurrency` to cap peak memory, or re-emit the data as `json_lines` upstream so it streams at `O(batch_size)`.","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"25d6302b-d492-47e2-a13d-92642935e9e4","timestamp":"2026-09-18T11:09:06.779Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"crates/source/s3/README.md:357:- **What the look-ahead holds** — for `json_lines` it is only the open body readers (opening an object does not download it), so peak memory stays `O(batch_size)`; for `json_array` / `raw_text` / `parquet` it is up to `concurrency` whole object bodies, which is the memory bound the knob advertises.\ncrates/source/s3/README.md:246:> **Memory ceiling — `raw_text` / `json_array`.** Both formats hold one whole decoded object in memory at a time (inherent: `raw_text`'s record *is* the file, and a JSON array isn't valid until its closing `]`). Because objects are fetched concurrently, peak memory is roughly **`concurrency` × (largest object's decoded size)**, not `batch_size`. For large `raw_text` / `json_array` objects, lower `concurrency` to cap peak memory, or re-emit the data as `json_lines` upstream so it streams line-by-line.\ncrates/source/gcs/README.md:211:> **Memory ceiling — `raw_text` / `json_array`.** Both hold one whole decoded object in memory at a time (inherent: a raw-text record *is* the whole file, and a JSON array isn't valid until its closing `]`). Because objects are fetched concurrently, peak memory is bounded by roughly **`concurrency` × (largest object's decoded size)**, not by `batch_size`. For large `raw_text` / `json_array` objects, lower `concurrency` to cap peak memory, or re-emit the data as `json_lines` upstream so it streams at `O(batch_size)`.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"cdf0ae85-05bb-4327-b546-b8bd7996c33f","session_id":"55398c64-7c65-40db-9b68-886111a86d7f","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/c-sikawats/Documents/GitHub/Personal/faucet-stream","sessionId":"55398c64-7c65-40db-9b68-886111a86d7f","version":"2.1.267","gitBranch":"fix/inert-config-knobs","slug":"linear-noodling-leaf"}
