{"parentUuid":"af70b95f-658d-4355-a45c-4b662d9242ba","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CfAqVHcfaggZR8a84X11t","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Now updating PR #665's body to cover the Parquet work I just added:"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":712,"cache_read_input_tokens":425603,"output_tokens":1672,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":712,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":1672,"cache_read_input_tokens":425603,"cache_creation_input_tokens":712,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":712},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011CfAqVFVfQzdBxLqQPAY8p","type":"assistant","uuid":"4f26d59f-a1f0-4a25-adea-2ebb15f2b362","timestamp":"2026-09-18T11:36:39.128Z","effort":"high","perTurnEffort":null,"session_id":"55398c64-7c65-40db-9b68-886111a86d7f","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/c-sikawats/Documents/GitHub/Personal/faucet-stream","sessionId":"55398c64-7c65-40db-9b68-886111a86d7f","version":"2.1.267","gitBranch":"fix/inert-config-knobs","slug":"linear-noodling-leaf"}
{"parentUuid":"4f26d59f-a1f0-4a25-adea-2ebb15f2b362","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CfAqVHcfaggZR8a84X11t","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_017mMG52Gmx9Ri3Q6nyCmeJt","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /Users/c-sikawats/Documents/GitHub/Personal/faucet-stream\npython3 - <<'PY'\nimport pathlib\np = pathlib.Path(\"/tmp/pr619.md\"); s = p.read_text()\nold = \"\"\"sftp gains `concurrency`, defaulting to **4** rather than 10: every read shares\none SSH channel and servers commonly cap concurrent handles.\n`faucet-common-sftp` re-exports the open-file handle as `SftpFile` so the source\ncan name it without depending on `russh-sftp` directly.\n\"\"\"\nnew = \"\"\"sftp gains `concurrency`, defaulting to **4** rather than 10: every read shares\none SSH channel and servers commonly cap concurrent handles.\n`faucet-common-sftp` re-exports the open-file handle as `SftpFile` so the source\ncan name it without depending on `russh-sftp` directly.\n\n### …and Parquet objects are no longer buffered whole\n\nThe issue's other acceptance criterion. Both sources pulled an entire Parquet\nobject into `Bytes` before decoding — on the row path *and* the columnar one —\nso peak memory was the size of the largest object, which for this format is\nroutinely gigabytes.\n\nParquet is random-access by design: its footer locates every row group. Each\nsource gains a `parquet_range` adapter implementing the parquet crate's\n`AsyncFileReader` over ranged reads (`GetObject` with a `Range` header for s3,\n`read_object` with a `ReadRange::segment` for gcs), and both paths now drive a\n`ParquetRecordBatchStream` with the Arrow batch size capped at `batch_size` —\na row-group bound alone is not enough when one row group holds millions of rows.\n\n`object_store`'s `ParquetObjectReader` (which the issue suggests, and the\nparquet source uses) would have done this, but these crates authenticate\nthrough the vendor SDKs — their own credential chains, `endpoint_url`/host\noverrides, checksum options — so adopting `object_store` here would mean a\nsecond, divergent way to authenticate, for one format.\n\nTwo configurations keep the whole-object path, because each is a guarantee\nworth more than the memory saving: `verify_checksum` (the stored checksum\ncovers the whole object, so verifying it means reading all of it) and a\nresolved `compression` codec (a compressed member is not randomly\naddressable, so the footer cannot be located without inflating everything).\n`verify_length` needs no exception — it guards a truncated *transfer*, and the\nranged reader enforces exactly that per fetch: a short range is an error\nnaming the object, never bytes quietly missing from a row group.\n\nTested against MinIO on a multi-row-group object: the ranged reader yields\nbyte-identical rows to the buffered path (an off-by-one offset would produce\nplausible-but-wrong values, the worst outcome available here), the first page\narrives measurably sooner than the whole object could have been downloaded,\nthe columnar path honours `batch_size`, and a divergent later schema is still\nrejected.\n\"\"\"\nassert old in s\ns = s.replace(old, new, 1)\n\nold = \"\"\"- The GCS concurrency test is `#[ignore]`d for the same reason as the rest of\n  that file — `fake-gcs-server` speaks REST while the connector uses the gRPC\n  data plane. The s3 (MinIO) and azure-blob (Azurite) suites run unignored.\"\"\"\nnew = \"\"\"- The GCS tests are `#[ignore]`d for the same reason as the rest of that file —\n  `fake-gcs-server` speaks REST while the connector uses the gRPC data plane —\n  so the gcs half shares the s3 design but is covered by compilation and review\n  rather than by an emulator. The s3 (MinIO) and azure-blob (Azurite) suites run\n  unignored.\n- Both sftp crates gained the workspace's\n  `[package.metadata.cargo-semver-checks.lints]` block; without it the new\n  `concurrency` field reads as a major break, which is exactly the contract the\n  API-evolution policy declares per crate.\n- The committed `schemas/faucet.schema.json` is spliced, not regenerated: a\n  regeneration reorders every key (serde_json's map type flips with feature\n  unification) and would bury 11 real lines in 20k of churn. Verified against a\n  real `--all-features` `faucet schema config` — zero semantic drift.\"\"\"\nassert old in s\ns = s.replace(old, new, 1)\np.write_text(s)\nprint(\"body updated\")\nPY\n/opt/homebrew/bin/gh pr edit 665 --repo faucet-hq/faucet-stream --body-file /tmp/pr619.md 2>&1 | tail -2","description":"Update PR 665 body with the parquet work"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":712,"cache_read_input_tokens":425603,"output_tokens":1672,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":712,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":1672,"cache_read_input_tokens":425603,"cache_creation_input_tokens":712,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":712},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_017mMG52Gmx9Ri3Q6nyCmeJt":{"command":"cd /Users/c-sikawats/Documents/GitHub/Personal/faucet-stream\npython3 - <<'PY'\nimport pathlib\np = pathlib.Path(\"/tmp/pr619.md\"); s = p.read_text()\nold = \"\"\"sftp gains `concurrency`, defaulting to **4** rather than 10: every read shares\none SSH channel and servers commonly cap concurrent handles.\n`faucet-common-sftp` re-exports the open-file handle as `SftpFile` so the source\ncan name it without depending on `russh-sftp` directly.\n\"\"\"\nnew = \"\"\"sftp gains `concurrency`, defaulting to **4** rather than 10: every read shares\none SSH channel and servers commonly cap concurrent handles.\n`faucet-common-sftp` re-exports the open-file handle as `SftpFile` so the source\ncan name it without depending on `russh-sftp` directly.\n\n### …and Parquet objects are no longer buffered whole\n\nThe issue's other acceptance criterion. Both sources pulled an entire Parquet\nobject into `Bytes` before decoding — on the row path *and* the columnar one —\nso peak memory was the size of the largest object, which for this format is\nroutinely gigabytes.\n\nParquet is random-access by design: its footer locates every row group. Each\nsource gains a `parquet_range` adapter implementing the parquet crate's\n`AsyncFileReader` over ranged reads (`GetObject` with a `Range` header for s3,\n`read_object` with a `ReadRange::segment` for gcs), and both paths now drive a\n`ParquetRecordBatchStream` with the Arrow batch size capped at `batch_size` —\na row-group bound alone is not enough when one row group holds millions of rows.\n\n`object_store`'s `ParquetObjectReader` (which the issue suggests, and the\nparquet source uses) would have done this, but these crates authenticate\nthrough the vendor SDKs — their own credential chains, `endpoint_url`/host\noverrides, checksum options — so adopting `object_store` here would mean a\nsecond, divergent way to authenticate, for one format.\n\nTwo configurations keep the whole-object path, because each is a guarantee\nworth more than the memory saving: `verify_checksum` (the stored checksum\ncovers the whole object, so verifying it means reading all of it) and a\nresolved `compression` codec (a compressed member is not randomly\naddressable, so the footer cannot be located without inflating everything).\n`verify_length` needs no exception — it guards a truncated *transfer*, and the\nranged reader enforces exactly that per fetch: a short range is an error\nnaming the object, never bytes quietly missing from a row group.\n\nTested against MinIO on a multi-row-group object: the ranged reader yields\nbyte-identical rows to the buffered path (an off-by-one offset would produce\nplausible-but-wrong values, the worst outcome available here), the first page\narrives measurably sooner than the whole object could have been downloaded,\nthe columnar path honours `batch_size`, and a divergent later schema is still\nrejected.\n\"\"\"\nassert old in s\ns = s.replace(old, new, 1)\n\nold = \"\"\"- The GCS concurrency test is `#[ignore]`d for the same reason as the rest of\n  that file — `fake-gcs-server` speaks REST while the connector uses the gRPC\n  data plane. The s3 (MinIO) and azure-blob (Azurite) suites run unignored.\"\"\"\nnew = \"\"\"- The GCS tests are `#[ignore]`d for the same reason as the rest of that file —\n  `fake-gcs-server` speaks REST while the connector uses the gRPC data plane —\n  so the gcs half shares the s3 design but is covered by compilation and review\n  rather than by an emulator. The s3 (MinIO) and azure-blob (Azurite) suites run\n  unignored.\n- Both sftp crates gained the workspace's\n  `[package.metadata.cargo-semver-checks.lints]` block; without it the new\n  `concurrency` field reads as a major break, which is exactly the contract the\n  API-evolution policy declares per crate.\n- The committed `schemas/faucet.schema.json` is spliced, not regenerated: a\n  regeneration reorders every key (serde_json's map type flips with feature\n  unification) and would bury 11 real lines in 20k of churn. Verified against a\n  real `--all-features` `faucet schema config` — zero semantic drift.\"\"\"\nassert old in s\ns = s.replace(old, new, 1)\np.write_text(s)\nprint(\"body updated\")\nPY\n/opt/homebrew/bin/gh pr edit 665 --repo faucet-hq/faucet-stream --body-file /tmp/pr619.md 2>&1 | tail -2","description":"Update PR 665 body with the parquet work"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CfAqVFVfQzdBxLqQPAY8p","type":"assistant","uuid":"81ddb602-1386-4ec6-ad43-7d437553743c","timestamp":"2026-09-18T11:36:55.633Z","effort