{"parentUuid":"9763e107-3004-488b-9cbe-bbb98aae26bb","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"hook_non_blocking_error","hookName":"PreToolUse:Bash","toolUseID":"toolu_01N4xv7WimQa6ENmfV8iUPoo","hookEvent":"PreToolUse","stderr":"Failed with non-blocking status code: /bin/sh: rtk: command not found","stdout":"","exitCode":127,"command":"rtk hook claude","durationMs":15276},"type":"attachment","uuid":"acaa9e3c-871a-440d-a985-744a0ad3c484","timestamp":"2026-09-19T10:26:44.168Z","session_id":"55398c64-7c65-40db-9b68-886111a86d7f","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/c-sikawats/Documents/GitHub/Personal/faucet-stream","sessionId":"55398c64-7c65-40db-9b68-886111a86d7f","version":"2.1.267","gitBranch":"fix/inert-config-knobs","slug":"linear-noodling-leaf"}
{"parentUuid":"9763e107-3004-488b-9cbe-bbb98aae26bb","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"23ad4779-0c1f-48fc-a10e-1c92c4154f79","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01N4xv7WimQa6ENmfV8iUPoo","type":"tool_result","content":"═══ #580\nfeat(sinks): consistent auto-create-table (schema-inferred) across all table-based sinks\n## Summary\nGeneralize the BigQuery `create_table` behavior (#578) into a **consistent auto-create-table capability across every table-based sink**: when the destination table (and, where applicable, its schema/dataset/database) does not exist, create it from the schema inferred over the first written page — controlled by a uniform `create_table: bool` config field, defaulting to `true` (a first-ever sync can't assume the target exists). Set `false` to require a pre-existing target and fail fast.\n\n## Motivation\nMigrating the 47 Meltano taps surfaced this on BigQuery (#578): a first sync to a fresh destination failed because the sink required the table to pre-exist. Every table-based destination has the same first-sync problem. Today the behavior is **inconsistent** across sinks — some auto-map columns, some have a `create_table` flag with different defaults/scope, and several can't create a table at all. A single, predictable `create_table` contract (same field name, same default, same schema-inference rules) makes every SQL/warehouse sink usable on a first run and removes per-connector surprises. Ties to the Primary Goal (reliability) and connector ergonomics.\n\n## Current state (audit)\n**Full auto-create (done):**\n- `bigquery` — `create_table` (default `true`), infers schema from first page, handles missing **and** schemaless tables, all write modes (#578).\n\n**Has *some* create/auto-map logic — needs review for consistency (field name, default, schema inference, write-mode coverage):**\n- `postgres`, `mysql`, `sqlite` — `auto_map` column mode (verify it actually CREATEs a missing table vs requires it to exist).\n- `mssql` — `create_table` exists but is **`json_column`-mode only** and defaults **`false`** (inconsistent with the proposed default `true` + full column inference).\n- `snowflake`, `spanner`, `duckdb`, `delta`, `iceberg` — verify current create/evolve behavior and align to the contract (iceberg/delta are table-format sinks; dataset/namespace creation differs).\n═══ #583\nTechnical white-paper program (flagship: Governance in the Movement Path)\n## Summary\n\nStand up a **technical white-paper program** for faucet-stream: a small, curated set of evidence-backed deep-dives on the genuinely novel engineering, published at a dedicated **`/papers`** home (HTML + downloadable PDF each). White papers are for the handful of things that are a real technical *contribution* — not a marketing dump. Everything else stays as docs deep-dives or blog posts.\n\n## Motivation\n\nSerious data teams evaluate infra on credible, specific technical claims — not feature lists. A tight set of papers on the hard, differentiated parts (columnar fast path, in-flight governance, crash-safe CDC) earns trust and gives the sales/positioning story a defensible spine vs Meltano / Airbyte / Fivetran. We already have the raw material: the `rfcs/` tree (0001 capability-traits, **0002 arrow-support**, 0003 plugin-system, 0004 streaming, 0005 async-runtime) and internal design specs (observability, DLQ). Papers = polishing these into public, evidence-backed pieces.\n\n## Scope — the shortlist (curated, not exhaustive)\n\n**Tier A — deserves a standalone paper:**\n\n1. **Zero-cost columnar (Arrow) fast path** — the escape-hatch negotiation: a columnar loop engages only when *both* ends advertise `supports_columnar()` **and** no `Value`-shaped stage exists, and **default (arrow-off) builds are byte-identical** to the row path. End-to-end Arrow across parquet / delta / BigQuery Storage Read API / Snowflake bulk-load. (Basis: RFC 0002.)\n2. **Governance in the movement path** — masking before any sink/DLQ/lineage; contracts enforced on the wire; column-level lineage derived from the transform chain; freshness/volume SLAs. The ordering guarantee is the contribution. **← recommended flagship.**\n═══ #589\nfeat(serve): template hosting + sync (github/s3/gcs/azure; pull-based)\n## Summary\nA **template hosting + sync layer** for a running faucet server: pull pipeline templates from a remote store (**GitHub, S3, GCS, Azure Blob**) into the local template registry, and publish local templates back — with a console button to sync. This is the **server-side counterpart to the Template Hub (#571)**; the two designs should be unified.\n\n## Motivation\nTeams want a shared, versioned source of truth for templates that a deployed `faucet serve` can consume, rather than registering each template by hand. GitOps-style: the remote store is the truth; the server's registry mirrors it.\n\n## Proposed design (v1)\n- Backends: `github`, `s3`, `gcs`, `azure-blob` (reuse the existing object-store/credential plumbing).\n- **Pull** = remote → local registry (the default, single source of truth).\n- **Publish** = an explicit local → remote action (not continuous mirroring).\n- Console button to trigger a sync; CLI equivalent (`faucet template sync`).\n\n## Design decisions (from review — resolve in an RFC before coding)\n- **Avoid true two-way sync.** Bidirectional hosting↔DB mirroring means conflict resolution and ambiguous source-of-truth — a classic trap. Make it **pull-based with a single source of truth**; treat push as an explicit publish, not a background two-way sync.\n═══ #591\nfeat(serve-ui): non-local dataset preview (BigQuery/Snowflake/DB) — RFC-gated\n## Summary\nExtend the dataset preview to **non-local datasets** (BigQuery, Snowflake, Postgres/MySQL, object stores, …): the user picks an **auth type**, enters credentials, and previews a **capped page** of the dataset in the serve console. Reuses the source-backed preview engine introduced for local sinks (#586) — local vs remote differs only by source `type` + whether auth is required. **RFC required before implementation.**\n\n## Motivation\nfaucet already has the read half — source connectors for BigQuery/Snowflake/Postgres/etc. and a capped `faucet preview`. Surfacing that in the UI lets a user **verify a remote sink actually landed correctly** (\"is my BigQuery table right?\") and explore a source before wiring a pipeline — without leaving the console. It's a read-only data browser over connectors faucet already speaks.\n\n## Scope discipline\nRead-only, **capped preview only** — NOT a query/BI tool. Arbitrary SQL / full browse is mission drift for a movement engine; keep it a bounded \"first N rows of a declared dataset.\"\n\n## Proposed design\n- **Reuse the generic preview engine from #586**: `source spec (type + config + auth) → capped page → rendered rows` (same code path as `faucet preview`). Remote support = additional source `type`s + a UI **auth-collection** step. No new preview subsystem.\n- Auth: per-connector auth types (service-account JSON, OAuth, keys, connection URL) collected in the UI, validated, and passed to the source's existing auth config.\n\n## Hard requirements (the RFC must resolve these before any code)\n═══ #604\nFile connectors: uniform CSV/JSON/XML/Excel formats + configurable compression across all sources & sinks\n## Summary\n\nGive **every file/object-storage connector** (S3, GCS, Azure Blob, SFTP — source *and* sink) a **uniform, configurable set of file formats** — CSV, JSON (lines & array), XML, Excel (`.xlsx`) — with **configurable compression** (gzip, zstd, zip, none, auto) on both read and write. The user picks the format and compression explicitly in config; sources decode/decompress it, sinks encode/compress to it.\n\n## Motivation\n\nFile stores hold data in many shapes — CSV exports, JSON dumps, XML feeds, Excel workbooks — often zipped. A data-movement platform (see [Primary Goal] and [Third-Party Connector Friendliness]) should read and write all of them across *every* file connector uniformly, not just a subset per connector. Today the capability is uneven, which forces users into external pre/post-processing.\n\n## Current state (verified against code)\n\n- **Sources**: `S3FileFormat` = `JsonLines | JsonArray | RawText | Parquet` (`crates/source/s3/src/config.rs`), plus `compression` auto-detect + codecs via `faucet_core::compression` (`compression` feature). GCS/Azure/SFTP are similar subsets. **Missing: CSV, XML, Excel** as first-class read formats.\n- **Sinks**: `S3SinkFormat` = `JsonLines | Parquet` only (`crates/sink/s3/src/config.rs`); compression is inferred from `file_extension` (e.g. `.jsonl.gz`), not an explicit knob. **Missing: CSV, XML, Excel output, and an explicit compression selector.**\n- **The parsers already exist**: the REST source's `decode` chain parses `json | csv | xlsx | xml` and does `gunzip`/`unzip` — so the format machinery is in the codebase and should be **reused**, not reinvented.\n\n═══ #608\nserve: first-class read/write/admin token trio + verified RBAC permission matrix\n## Summary\nGive `faucet serve` a simple, first-class way to configure **three tokens — read / write / admin** — and ship a **verified, documented permission matrix** proving the read token can do read-only operations and nothing that mutates state.\n\n## Motivation\nOperators want to hand out a **read token** (dashboards, viewers), a **write token** (trigger runs, register/launch templates), and an **admin token** (everything incl. the audit log) without hand-authoring an RBAC file. We also need a guarantee — enforced by a test — that the read token can never submit/trigger/register/cancel/delete. Ties to the platform's governance story (Primary Goal: reliability + safe-by-construction).\n\n## Current state (there *is* a classification already)\nRBAC exists today (`cli/src/serve/rbac.rs`, #205):\n- **Auth:** `--auth-token` = one implicit `admin`; `--auth-config <file>` = principals `{ name, token, role }`, role ∈ `viewer` / `operator` / `admin`.\n- **Permissions:** `RunRead, RunWrite, SchemaRead, DlqRead, TriggerFire, CatalogRead, TemplateRead, TemplateWrite, AuditRead`.\n- **Role → permission:** `Viewer` = all reads (`RunRead, SchemaRead, DlqRead, CatalogRead, TemplateRead`); `Operator` = viewer **+** `RunWrite, TriggerFire, TemplateWrite`; `Admin` = everything **+** `AuditRead` and any unmapped `/v1` route (fail-closed admin-only).\n- **`required_permission(method, path)`** maps each route to a permission.\n\nSo **\"what can admin do that read can't\"** is already answered: admin can write (submit/cancel/delete runs, backfill, register/delete/launch/promote/deprecate templates, fire triggers) **and** read the audit log; the read (`viewer`) role has none of those — reads only. Operator is the middle tier (writes, but no audit).\n═══ #610\nPer-run concurrency override at trigger time (POST /v1/runs and POST /v1/templates/{id}/runs)\n## Summary\nLet the caller pass a **concurrency override when triggering a run** — on both the ad-hoc path (`POST /v1/runs`, `faucet run`) and the templated path (`POST /v1/templates/{id}/runs`) — so the **same template/config** can drive different source connection/fetch concurrency per invocation, without per-customer copies.\n\n## Motivation\nMulti-tenant reality: triggering one template for two customers, where customer A has beefy read replicas (can take, say, 20 connections) and customer B is small (5). Today the source pool size is baked into the connector config (or must be pre-declared as a `params:` field by the template author). Operators want to say **\"run this one at concurrency N\"** at trigger time. Ties to the Primary Goal (throughput) while respecting each source's capacity.\n\n## Current state\nConcurrency is expressed today only inside the config/template:\n- Source pools: `max_connections` (postgres/mysql/mssql/sqlite/…, default 10), `partition_concurrency` (rest), `concurrency` (s3), `shard_concurrency` (kinesis).\n- Matrix parallelism: top-level `execution.max_concurrent`.\n- Server-wide slots: `faucet serve --max-concurrent`.\nTemplated runs *can* already override a knob only if the template explicitly exposes it as a `${param.*}` — not first-class, and every template has to opt in.\n\n## Proposed design\n═══ #612\nBigQuery sink: per-page jobs.query makes loads job-latency-bound (60k rows ≈ 6 min); use a load job / larger batching\n## Summary\nThe BigQuery sink writes **one `jobs.query` per page**, so load time is dominated by **per-job latency**, not data volume. A trivial 59,358-row postgres→BQ overwrite took **~6m44s** because `batch_size` defaults to 1000 → ~60 sequential BigQuery query jobs, each with multi-second scheduling+poll overhead. Real tenant tables (millions of rows) would issue thousands of jobs — unacceptably slow and undercuts the throughput story.\n\n## Evidence\n- `test-postgres` (default `batch_size=1000`), 59,358 rows → `<dataset>.postgres__de_data_202607231635_entries`: submitted 13:17:21 → finished 13:24:05 (**~404s**). ~60 pages ⇒ ~60 `jobs.query` calls ⇒ ~6–7s each.\n\n## Root cause\n`crates/sink/bigquery/src/sink.rs`:\n- Overwrite: `insert_overwrite_page` runs `INSERT … SELECT FROM UNNEST(JSON_QUERY_ARRAY(@payload))` via `job().query()` + `await_query_job` **per page**.\n- The exactly-once/idempotent path is similarly per-page `jobs.query`.\nEach `jobs.query` is a full BigQuery **query job** (schedule + execute + long-poll), whose fixed latency dwarfs a 1000-row insert. Throughput is therefore `pages × job_latency`, independent of row count.\n\n## Proposed design\n1. **Primary — bulk load path:** for append/overwrite, ingest via a **BigQuery load job** (newline-delimited JSON/Parquet to a temp/staging table) or the **Storage Write API**, which handle millions of rows in a single operation and have no ~10 MB request cap. Overwrite then swaps staging→target as it already does. This makes load time volume-bound, not job-count-bound.\n═══ #614\nBigQuery sink: streaming bounded-memory bucket-free load (resumable NDJSON, then Parquet) — decouple memory from atomicity\n## Summary\n\nThe BigQuery media-load path (#612) buffers the whole page in memory (`Vec<serde_json::Value>` + a full NDJSON `String`) before uploading. For a single-page atomic overwrite this means **O(table) memory** — measured ~346 MB peak for a 59,358-row / 19 MB table (~15–20× the raw data), on par with the Meltano/iPaaS pandas-DataFrame footprint (~293 MB for the same job). Add a **streaming, bounded-memory, bucket-free load writer** so a single atomic load job is fed incrementally from small source pages, dropping peak memory to ~1–2× a page.\n\n## Motivation\n\n- **Primary Goal (efficiency).** Memory is currently the one axis where faucet does **not** beat iPaaS — both materialize the whole table (faucet: `Vec<Value>`; iPaaS: DataFrame). Runtime is I/O + BQ-API bound (the load job has a ~5–7s server-side floor both tools share), so memory is the remaining lever. Streaming makes faucet decisively lighter *and* trims the serialize/upload slice of runtime.\n- **Capability, not just footprint.** Today a *single atomic* full-refresh needs the whole table in one page (`batch_size ≥ rows`). Streaming **decouples memory from atomicity**: the source can page at e.g. 10k rows (O(batch_size) memory) while all pages feed **one** load job — so arbitrarily large tables can be atomically overwritten with bounded RAM. Removes the \"huge table ⇒ multi-page ⇒ lose single-load atomicity or OOM\" tradeoff.\n\n## Structural design\n\n- **BigQuery resumable upload session** held as sink state across pages:\n  - First `write_batch`/`insert_overwrite_page` opens a resumable upload session for one load job (WRITE_TRUNCATE for overwrite, WRITE_APPEND for append).\n  - Each page serializes its rows → gzip → uploads one chunk to the session (rows are dropped after the chunk is sent — no full `Vec<Value>`/NDJSON retained).\n═══ #615\nREST source: streaming bounded-memory CSV decode — decouple peak memory from result-set size (3.96 GB for one 838k-row Salesforce Bulk result)\n## Summary\n\nThe REST source's decode pipeline (#515) materializes the **entire** response body — and, with locator paging (#557), the **entire result set** — in memory before a single record reaches the pipeline. Measured on a live Salesforce Bulk API 2.0 CSV job: **3.96 GB peak RSS for one 838,058-row object** that serializes to 593 MB of JSONL — a **~6.8× blow-up**, for a single row with no matrix concurrency. At the default matrix concurrency (`clamp(cores, 1, 8)` → 8 on a 14-core box) a Salesforce object sync reached ~30 GB and had to be aborted.\n\nMake the `decode: [{ parse: { format: csv } }]` path stream, so peak memory is O(`batch_size`) rather than O(result set).\n\n## Motivation\n\n- **Primary Goal (efficiency + reliability).** This is a hard ceiling, not a slow path: peak memory scales with the *source object's* size, so a large-enough table OOMs the runner regardless of how the sink is configured. It is the source-side twin of #614 (BigQuery sink, O(table) memory) — together they are the one axis where faucet does not currently beat Meltano/iPaaS.\n- **It blocks a real workload.** A 21-object Salesforce tenant (Account 426 fields, Opportunity 332, `Whitespace__c` 838k rows) cannot be synced at default settings on a 14-core/32 GB box. The only present mitigation is `execution.max_concurrent: 2`, which trades throughput for headroom and still leaves a single-object floor of ~4 GB.\n- **Locator paging does not help.** #557 added `Sforce-Locator` result-set paging, which correctly reduces per-*request* size — but the loop accumulates into one `Vec`, so peak memory is unchanged. The feature currently buys wire-level correctness only, not bounded memory.\n- **The parser is already streaming-capable.** `parse_csv` uses `csv_async::AsyncReaderBuilder`, which takes an `AsyncRead`; it is simply handed a `&[u8]` and collects into a `Vec<Value>`. The blocker is the API shape at both ends, not the CSV machinery — so the fix is tractable.\n\n## Where the memory goes","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"45626107-4b1f-4653-a9a2-561f1a7daeaf","timestamp":"2026-09-19T10:26:53.511Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"═══ #580\nfeat(sinks): consistent auto-create-table (schema-inferred) across all table-based sinks\n## Summary\nGeneralize the BigQuery `create_table` behavior (#578) into a **consistent auto-create-table capability across every table-based sink**: when the destination table (and, where applicable, its schema/dataset/database) does not exist, create it from the schema inferred over the first written page — controlled by a uniform `create_table: bool` config field, defaulting to `true` (a first-ever sync can't assume the target exists). Set `false` to require a pre-existing target and fail fast.\n\n## Motivation\nMigrating the 47 Meltano taps surfaced this on BigQuery (#578): a first sync to a fresh destination failed because the sink required the table to pre-exist. Every table-based destination has the same first-sync problem. Today the behavior is **inconsistent** across sinks — some auto-map columns, some have a `create_table` flag with different defaults/scope, and several can't create a table at all. A single, predictable `create_table` contract (same field name, same default, same schema-inference rules) makes every SQL/warehouse sink usable on a first run and removes per-connector surprises. Ties to the Primary Goal (reliability) and connector ergonomics.\n\n## Current state (audit)\n**Full auto-create (done):**\n- `bigquery` — `create_table` (default `true`), infers schema from first page, handles missing **and** schemaless tables, all write modes (#578).\n\n**Has *some* create/auto-map logic — needs review for consistency (field name, default, schema inference, write-mode coverage):**\n- `postgres`, `mysql`, `sqlite` — `auto_map` column mode (verify it actually CREATEs a missing table vs requires it to exist).\n- `mssql` — `create_table` exists but is **`json_column`-mode only** and defaults **`false`** (inconsistent with the proposed default `true` + full column inference).\n- `snowflake`, `spanner`, `duckdb`, `delta`, `iceberg` — verify current create/evolve behavior and align to the contract (iceberg/delta are table-format sinks; dataset/namespace creation differs).\n═══ #583\nTechnical white-paper program (flagship: Governance in the Movement Path)\n## Summary\n\nStand up a **technical white-paper program** for faucet-stream: a small, curated set of evidence-backed deep-dives on the genuinely novel engineering, published at a dedicated **`/papers`** home (HTML + downloadable PDF each). White papers are for the handful of things that are a real technical *contribution* — not a marketing dump. Everything else stays as docs deep-dives or blog posts.\n\n## Motivation\n\nSerious data teams evaluate infra on credible, specific technical claims — not feature lists. A tight set of papers on the hard, differentiated parts (columnar fast path, in-flight governance, crash-safe CDC) earns trust and gives the sales/positioning story a defensible spine vs Meltano / Airbyte / Fivetran. We already have the raw material: the `rfcs/` tree (0001 capability-traits, **0002 arrow-support**, 0003 plugin-system, 0004 streaming, 0005 async-runtime) and internal design specs (observability, DLQ). Papers = polishing these into public, evidence-backed pieces.\n\n## Scope — the shortlist (curated, not exhaustive)\n\n**Tier A — deserves a standalone paper:**\n\n1. **Zero-cost columnar (Arrow) fast path** — the escape-hatch negotiation: a columnar loop engages only when *both* ends advertise `supports_columnar()` **and** no `Value`-shaped stage exists, and **default (arrow-off) builds are byte-identical** to the row path. End-to-end Arrow across parquet / delta / BigQuery Storage Read API / Snowflake bulk-load. (Basis: RFC 0002.)\n2. **Governance in the movement path** — masking before any sink/DLQ/lineage; contracts enforced on the wire; column-level lineage derived from the transform chain; freshness/volume SLAs. The ordering guarantee is the contribution. **← recommended flagship.**\n═══ #589\nfeat(serve): template hosting + sync (github/s3/gcs/azure; pull-based)\n## Summary\nA **template hosting + sync layer** for a running faucet server: pull pipeline templates from a remote store (**GitHub, S3, GCS, Azure Blob**) into the local template registry, and publish local templates back — with a console button to sync. This is the **server-side counterpart to the Template Hub (#571)**; the two designs should be unified.\n\n## Motivation\nTeams want a shared, versioned source of truth for templates that a deployed `faucet serve` can consume, rather than registering each template by hand. GitOps-style: the remote store is the truth; the server's registry mirrors it.\n\n## Proposed design (v1)\n- Backends: `github`, `s3`, `gcs`, `azure-blob` (reuse the existing object-store/credential plumbing).\n- **Pull** = remote → local registry (the default, single source of truth).\n- **Publish** = an explicit local → remote action (not continuous mirroring).\n- Console button to trigger a sync; CLI equivalent (`faucet template sync`).\n\n## Design decisions (from review — resolve in an RFC before coding)\n- **Avoid true two-way sync.** Bidirectional hosting↔DB mirroring means conflict resolution and ambiguous source-of-truth — a classic trap. Make it **pull-based with a single source of truth**; treat push as an explicit publish, not a background two-way sync.\n═══ #591\nfeat(serve-ui): non-local dataset preview (BigQuery/Snowflake/DB) — RFC-gated\n## Summary\nExtend the dataset preview to **non-local datasets** (BigQuery, Snowflake, Postgres/MySQL, object stores, …): the user picks an **auth type**, enters credentials, and previews a **capped page** of the dataset in the serve console. Reuses the source-backed preview engine introduced for local sinks (#586) — local vs remote differs only by source `type` + whether auth is required. **RFC required before implementation.**\n\n## Motivation\nfaucet already has the read half — source connectors for BigQuery/Snowflake/Postgres/etc. and a capped `faucet preview`. Surfacing that in the UI lets a user **verify a remote sink actually landed correctly** (\"is my BigQuery table right?\") and explore a source before wiring a pipeline — without leaving the console. It's a read-only data browser over connectors faucet already speaks.\n\n## Scope discipline\nRead-only, **capped preview only** — NOT a query/BI tool. Arbitrary SQL / full browse is mission drift for a movement engine; keep it a bounded \"first N rows of a declared dataset.\"\n\n## Proposed design\n- **Reuse the generic preview engine from #586**: `source spec (type + config + auth) → capped page → rendered rows` (same code path as `faucet preview`). Remote support = additional source `type`s + a UI **auth-collection** step. No new preview subsystem.\n- Auth: per-connector auth types (service-account JSON, OAuth, keys, connection URL) collected in the UI, validated, and passed to the source's existing auth config.\n\n## Hard requirements (the RFC must resolve these before any code)\n═══ #604\nFile connectors: uniform CSV/JSON/XML/Excel formats + configurable compression across all sources & sinks\n## Summary\n\nGive **every file/object-storage connector** (S3, GCS, Azure Blob, SFTP — source *and* sink) a **uniform, configurable set of file formats** — CSV, JSON (lines & array), XML, Excel (`.xlsx`) — with **configurable compression** (gzip, zstd, zip, none, auto) on both read and write. The user picks the format and compression explicitly in config; sources decode/decompress it, sinks encode/compress to it.\n\n## Motivation\n\nFile stores hold data in many shapes — CSV exports, JSON dumps, XML feeds, Excel workbooks — often zipped. A data-movement platform (see [Primary Goal] and [Third-Party Connector Friendliness]) should read and write all of them across *every* file connector uniformly, not just a subset per connector. Today the capability is uneven, which forces users into external pre/post-processing.\n\n## Current state (verified against code)\n\n- **Sources**: `S3FileFormat` = `JsonLines | JsonArray | RawText | Parquet` (`crates/source/s3/src/config.rs`), plus `compression` auto-detect + codecs via `faucet_core::compression` (`compression` feature). GCS/Azure/SFTP are similar subsets. **Missing: CSV, XML, Excel** as first-class read formats.\n- **Sinks**: `S3SinkFormat` = `JsonLines | Parquet` only (`crates/sink/s3/src/config.rs`); compression is inferred from `file_extension` (e.g. `.jsonl.gz`), not an explicit knob. **Missing: CSV, XML, Excel output, and an explicit compression selector.**\n- **The parsers already exist**: the REST source's `decode` chain parses `json | csv | xlsx | xml` and does `gunzip`/`unzip` — so the format machinery is in the codebase and should be **reused**, not reinvented.\n\n═══ #608\nserve: first-class read/write/admin token trio + verified RBAC permission matrix\n## Summary\nGive `faucet serve` a simple, first-class way to configure **three tokens — read / write / admin** — and ship a **verified, documented permission matrix** proving the read token can do read-only operations and nothing that mutates state.\n\n## Motivation\nOperators want to hand out a **read token** (dashboards, viewers), a **write token** (trigger runs, register/launch templates), and an **admin token** (everything incl. the audit log) without hand-authoring an RBAC file. We also need a guarantee — enforced by a test — that the read token can never submit/trigger/register/cancel/delete. Ties to the platform's governance story (Primary Goal: reliability + safe-by-construction).\n\n## Current state (there *is* a classification already)\nRBAC exists today (`cli/src/serve/rbac.rs`, #205):\n- **Auth:** `--auth-token` = one implicit `admin`; `--auth-config <file>` = principals `{ name, token, role }`, role ∈ `viewer` / `operator` / `admin`.\n- **Permissions:** `RunRead, RunWrite, SchemaRead, DlqRead, TriggerFire, CatalogRead, TemplateRead, TemplateWrite, AuditRead`.\n- **Role → permission:** `Viewer` = all reads (`RunRead, SchemaRead, DlqRead, CatalogRead, TemplateRead`); `Operator` = viewer **+** `RunWrite, TriggerFire, TemplateWrite`; `Admin` = everything **+** `AuditRead` and any unmapped `/v1` route (fail-closed admin-only).\n- **`required_permission(method, path)`** maps each route to a permission.\n\nSo **\"what can admin do that read can't\"** is already answered: admin can write (submit/cancel/delete runs, backfill, register/delete/launch/promote/deprecate templates, fire triggers) **and** read the audit log; the read (`viewer`) role has none of those — reads only. Operator is the middle tier (writes, but no audit).\n═══ #610\nPer-run concurrency override at trigger time (POST /v1/runs and POST /v1/templates/{id}/runs)\n## Summary\nLet the caller pass a **concurrency override when triggering a run** — on both the ad-hoc path (`POST /v1/runs`, `faucet run`) and the templated path (`POST /v1/templates/{id}/runs`) — so the **same template/config** can drive different source connection/fetch concurrency per invocation, without per-customer copies.\n\n## Motivation\nMulti-tenant reality: triggering one template for two customers, where customer A has beefy read replicas (can take, say, 20 connections) and customer B is small (5). Today the source pool size is baked into the connector config (or must be pre-declared as a `params:` field by the template author). Operators want to say **\"run this one at concurrency N\"** at trigger time. Ties to the Primary Goal (throughput) while respecting each source's capacity.\n\n## Current state\nConcurrency is expressed today only inside the config/template:\n- Source pools: `max_connections` (postgres/mysql/mssql/sqlite/…, default 10), `partition_concurrency` (rest), `concurrency` (s3), `shard_concurrency` (kinesis).\n- Matrix parallelism: top-level `execution.max_concurrent`.\n- Server-wide slots: `faucet serve --max-concurrent`.\nTemplated runs *can* already override a knob only if the template explicitly exposes it as a `${param.*}` — not first-class, and every template has to opt in.\n\n## Proposed design\n═══ #612\nBigQuery sink: per-page jobs.query makes loads job-latency-bound (60k rows ≈ 6 min); use a load job / larger batching\n## Summary\nThe BigQuery sink writes **one `jobs.query` per page**, so load time is dominated by **per-job latency**, not data volume. A trivial 59,358-row postgres→BQ overwrite took **~6m44s** because `batch_size` defaults to 1000 → ~60 sequential BigQuery query jobs, each with multi-second scheduling+poll overhead. Real tenant tables (millions of rows) would issue thousands of jobs — unacceptably slow and undercuts the throughput story.\n\n## Evidence\n- `test-postgres` (default `batch_size=1000`), 59,358 rows → `<dataset>.postgres__de_data_202607231635_entries`: submitted 13:17:21 → finished 13:24:05 (**~404s**). ~60 pages ⇒ ~60 `jobs.query` calls ⇒ ~6–7s each.\n\n## Root cause\n`crates/sink/bigquery/src/sink.rs`:\n- Overwrite: `insert_overwrite_page` runs `INSERT … SELECT FROM UNNEST(JSON_QUERY_ARRAY(@payload))` via `job().query()` + `await_query_job` **per page**.\n- The exactly-once/idempotent path is similarly per-page `jobs.query`.\nEach `jobs.query` is a full BigQuery **query job** (schedule + execute + long-poll), whose fixed latency dwarfs a 1000-row insert. Throughput is therefore `pages × job_latency`, independent of row count.\n\n## Proposed design\n1. **Primary — bulk load path:** for append/overwrite, ingest via a **BigQuery load job** (newline-delimited JSON/Parquet to a temp/staging table) or the **Storage Write API**, which handle millions of rows in a single operation and have no ~10 MB request cap. Overwrite then swaps staging→target as it already does. This makes load time volume-bound, not job-count-bound.\n═══ #614\nBigQuery sink: streaming bounded-memory bucket-free load (resumable NDJSON, then Parquet) — decouple memory from atomicity\n## Summary\n\nThe BigQuery media-load path (#612) buffers the whole page in memory (`Vec<serde_json::Value>` + a full NDJSON `String`) before uploading. For a single-page atomic overwrite this means **O(table) memory** — measured ~346 MB peak for a 59,358-row / 19 MB table (~15–20× the raw data), on par with the Meltano/iPaaS pandas-DataFrame footprint (~293 MB for the same job). Add a **streaming, bounded-memory, bucket-free load writer** so a single atomic load job is fed incrementally from small source pages, dropping peak memory to ~1–2× a page.\n\n## Motivation\n\n- **Primary Goal (efficiency).** Memory is currently the one axis where faucet does **not** beat iPaaS — both materialize the whole table (faucet: `Vec<Value>`; iPaaS: DataFrame). Runtime is I/O + BQ-API bound (the load job has a ~5–7s server-side floor both tools share), so memory is the remaining lever. Streaming makes faucet decisively lighter *and* trims the serialize/upload slice of runtime.\n- **Capability, not just footprint.** Today a *single atomic* full-refresh needs the whole table in one page (`batch_size ≥ rows`). Streaming **decouples memory from atomicity**: the source can page at e.g. 10k rows (O(batch_size) memory) while all pages feed **one** load job — so arbitrarily large tables can be atomically overwritten with bounded RAM. Removes the \"huge table ⇒ multi-page ⇒ lose single-load atomicity or OOM\" tradeoff.\n\n## Structural design\n\n- **BigQuery resumable upload session** held as sink state across pages:\n  - First `write_batch`/`insert_overwrite_page` opens a resumable upload session for one load job (WRITE_TRUNCATE for overwrite, WRITE_APPEND for append).\n  - Each page serializes its rows → gzip → uploads one chunk to the session (rows are dropped after the chunk is sent — no full `Vec<Value>`/NDJSON retained).\n═══ #615\nREST source: streaming bounded-memory CSV decode — decouple peak memory from result-set size (3.96 GB for one 838k-row Salesforce Bulk result)\n## Summary\n\nThe REST source's decode pipeline (#515) materializes the **entire** response body — and, with locator paging (#557), the **entire result set** — in memory before a single record reaches the pipeline. Measured on a live Salesforce Bulk API 2.0 CSV job: **3.96 GB peak RSS for one 838,058-row object** that serializes to 593 MB of JSONL — a **~6.8× blow-up**, for a single row with no matrix concurrency. At the default matrix concurrency (`clamp(cores, 1, 8)` → 8 on a 14-core box) a Salesforce object sync reached ~30 GB and had to be aborted.\n\nMake the `decode: [{ parse: { format: csv } }]` path stream, so peak memory is O(`batch_size`) rather than O(result set).\n\n## Motivation\n\n- **Primary Goal (efficiency + reliability).** This is a hard ceiling, not a slow path: peak memory scales with the *source object's* size, so a large-enough table OOMs the runner regardless of how the sink is configured. It is the source-side twin of #614 (BigQuery sink, O(table) memory) — together they are the one axis where faucet does not currently beat Meltano/iPaaS.\n- **It blocks a real workload.** A 21-object Salesforce tenant (Account 426 fields, Opportunity 332, `Whitespace__c` 838k rows) cannot be synced at default settings on a 14-core/32 GB box. The only present mitigation is `execution.max_concurrent: 2`, which trades throughput for headroom and still leaves a single-object floor of ~4 GB.\n- **Locator paging does not help.** #557 added `Sforce-Locator` result-set paging, which correctly reduces per-*request* size — but the loop accumulates into one `Vec`, so peak memory is unchanged. The feature currently buys wire-level correctness only, not bounded memory.\n- **The parser is already streaming-capable.** `parse_csv` uses `csv_async::AsyncReaderBuilder`, which takes an `AsyncRead`; it is simply handed a `&[u8]` and collects into a `Vec<Value>`. The blocker is the API shape at both ends, not the CSV machinery — so the fix is tractable.\n\n## Where the memory goes","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"9763e107-3004-488b-9cbe-bbb98aae26bb","session_id":"55398c64-7c65-40db-9b68-886111a86d7f","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/c-sikawats/Documents/GitHub/Personal/faucet-stream","sessionId":"55398c64-7c65-40db-9b68-886111a86d7f","version":"2.1.267","gitBranch":"fix/inert-config-knobs","slug":"linear-noodling-leaf"}
