leaf"}
{"parentUuid":"17394ff5-eae7-48b4-81e4-058a7214611b","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"hook_non_blocking_error","hookName":"PreToolUse:Bash","toolUseID":"toolu_01LKtcXd6DgDxdaMYvaChNp4","hookEvent":"PreToolUse","stderr":"Failed with non-blocking status code: /bin/sh: rtk: command not found","stdout":"","exitCode":127,"command":"rtk hook claude","durationMs":73},"type":"attachment","uuid":"8e334fa6-588b-4a5c-83a4-36d60c20d54f","timestamp":"2026-09-19T11:02:37.378Z","session_id":"55398c64-7c65-40db-9b68-886111a86d7f","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/c-sikawats/Documents/GitHub/Personal/faucet-stream","sessionId":"55398c64-7c65-40db-9b68-886111a86d7f","version":"2.1.267","gitBranch":"fix/audit-batch-580","slug":"linear-noodling-leaf"}
{"parentUuid":"17394ff5-eae7-48b4-81e4-058a7214611b","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"23ad4779-0c1f-48fc-a10e-1c92c4154f79","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01LKtcXd6DgDxdaMYvaChNp4","type":"tool_result","content":"## Summary\n\nThe CLI executor dispatches matrix roots into the shared `Semaphore(max_concurrent)` pool in **matrix-declaration (BFS) order** (`cli/src/executor.rs::run_expanded`; the ready set is stable-sorted by original row index and each task then `sem.acquire().await`s in that order). With heterogeneous per-object durations, total wall-clock (makespan) therefore depends on *the order the user happens to list objects in*: a large object listed late becomes a long idle tail. Add an optional **cost-aware / Longest-Processing-Time-first (LPT) dispatch order** so the executor starts the biggest jobs first and minimizes makespan without the user hand-tuning row order.\n\n## Motivation\n\nPrimary Goal (every pipeline as fast as possible). Measured on a real 21-object Salesforce → BigQuery run at `max_concurrent: 8`: the two large objects `lead` (605k rows) and `contact` (483k rows) sat late in the matrix and finished **~9 min after the other 19 objects were already done** (07:55 → 08:04), with **6 of the 8 slots idle** — a recoverable ~9 min out of a 32.5 min run. Classic `P || Cmax` scheduling: LPT (longest job first) is provably within 4/3 of optimal makespan, while *largest-last* — which plain declaration order can accidentally produce — is the worst case. This is a free, connector-agnostic runtime win: no code in any connector, just the enqueue order.\n\n## Proposed design\n\nTwo independent, composable pieces, both **opt-in** (default preserves today's exact deterministic order):\n\n1. **Explicit weight hint** — an optional `weight: <number>` per matrix row (and optionally per source template). Higher weight = dispatched earlier. `cli/src/config.rs::MatrixRow` gains `weight: Option<f64>`.\n2. **Auto cost estimate — a *bytes* proxy, not a row proxy.** The scheduling cost of a job is roughly the volume of data moved = `rows × row_width`, and row_width scales with **column count** (and field types), not just row count. A 970k-row × 3-column history table can be *lighter* than a 122k-row × 100-column `account`; ranking on rows alone mis-orders wide-but-short tables. So the default weight is:\n\n   ```\n   weight ≈ estimated_rows × estimated_row_width\n   estimated_row_width ≈ Σ per-column type width   (fallback: column_count × K)\n   ```\n\n   Both inputs are already available cheaply from discovery: `faucet_core::discover::DatasetDescriptor.estimated_rows` **and** its `schema` (column count + types), #211. When schema/width is unknown, fall back to `estimated_rows`, and when that is unknown too, fall back to declared order. The pure cost function `estimate_weight(rows: Option<u64>, schema: Option<&Value>) -> Option<f64>` is unit-testable in isolation.\n\nNew `execution.schedule` knob selecting the policy:\n\n```yaml\nexecution:\n  max_concurrent: 8\n  schedule: lpt          # new; default \"declared\" (current behavior)\nmatrix:\n  - id: contact\n    weight: 483000       # optional explicit hint; else auto (estimated_rows) or none\n    ...\n```\n\nExecutor change (`cli/src/executor.rs`, `run_expanded`): at each level, when `schedule == lpt`, **stable-sort the *ready* roots by weight descending before spawning/acquiring permits** (ties fall back to declaration order, so dispatch stays deterministic). Everything else is unchanged — one shared `Semaphore`, same `JoinSet`, same `on_error` cancellation. Only the order in which ready siblings queue for permits changes.\n\n## Affected crates / files\n\n- `cli/src/config.rs` — `ExecutionSpec.schedule: DispatchOrder` (`Declared` default / `Lpt`), `MatrixRow.weight: Option<f64>`.\n- `cli/src/executor.rs` — pure `order_ready(ready, weights, policy) -> Vec<String>` used to sort the ready set before permit acquisition.\n- `cli/src/expand.rs` — thread `weight` onto `ExpandedNode`; default it from `estimated_rows` when the source supports discover and no explicit weight is given.\n- `docs/book/src/reference/config.md` (`execution.schedule`, per-row `weight`) + a cookbook note on scheduling many objects; `faucet schema` for the execution block.\n\n## Edge cases & failure modes\n\n- Ties / all-missing weights → identical to `declared` (deterministic; dispatch order is never nondeterministic).\n- LPT applies **only to ready sibling roots competing for permits** — children and `depends_on` edges keep their existing completion-gated ordering.\n- Auto-estimate must be **metadata-only and non-blocking**: if discovery is unavailable/slow/errors, silently fall back to declared rather than delaying dispatch.\n- Server-side-queue-dominated sources (e.g. Salesforce Bulk): duration can be dominated by org-side job queue time, so `estimated_rows` is a *proxy*, not exact — LPT still can't hurt, but the doc should say the win depends on duration correlating with weight.\n- `on_error: stop` cooperative-cancel + `STOP_FLUSH_GRACE` semantics unchanged.","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"770a1f25-ef02-47f1-ae37-87d3f04f4d77","timestamp":"2026-09-19T11:02:41.039Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"## Summary\n\nThe CLI executor dispatches matrix roots into the shared `Semaphore(max_concurrent)` pool in **matrix-declaration (BFS) order** (`cli/src/executor.rs::run_expanded`; the ready set is stable-sorted by original row index and each task then `sem.acquire().await`s in that order). With heterogeneous per-object durations, total wall-clock (makespan) therefore depends on *the order the user happens to list objects in*: a large object listed late becomes a long idle tail. Add an optional **cost-aware / Longest-Processing-Time-first (LPT) dispatch order** so the executor starts the biggest jobs first and minimizes makespan without the user hand-tuning row order.\n\n## Motivation\n\nPrimary Goal (every pipeline as fast as possible). Measured on a real 21-object Salesforce → BigQuery run at `max_concurrent: 8`: the two large objects `lead` (605k rows) and `contact` (483k rows) sat late in the matrix and finished **~9 min after the other 19 objects were already done** (07:55 → 08:04), with **6 of the 8 slots idle** — a recoverable ~9 min out of a 32.5 min run. Classic `P || Cmax` scheduling: LPT (longest job first) is provably within 4/3 of optimal makespan, while *largest-last* — which plain declaration order can accidentally produce — is the worst case. This is a free, connector-agnostic runtime win: no code in any connector, just the enqueue order.\n\n## Proposed design\n\nTwo independent, composable pieces, both **opt-in** (default preserves today's exact deterministic order):\n\n1. **Explicit weight hint** — an optional `weight: <number>` per matrix row (and optionally per source template). Higher weight = dispatched earlier. `cli/src/config.rs::MatrixRow` gains `weight: Option<f64>`.\n2. **Auto cost estimate — a *bytes* proxy, not a row proxy.** The scheduling cost of a job is roughly the volume of data moved = `rows × row_width`, and row_width scales with **column count** (and field types), not just row count. A 970k-row × 3-column history table can be *lighter* than a 122k-row × 100-column `account`; ranking on rows alone mis-orders wide-but-short tables. So the default weight is:\n\n   ```\n   weight ≈ estimated_rows × estimated_row_width\n   estimated_row_width ≈ Σ per-column type width   (fallback: column_count × K)\n   ```\n\n   Both inputs are already available cheaply from discovery: `faucet_core::discover::DatasetDescriptor.estimated_rows` **and** its `schema` (column count + types), #211. When schema/width is unknown, fall back to `estimated_rows`, and when that is unknown too, fall back to declared order. The pure cost function `estimate_weight(rows: Option<u64>, schema: Option<&Value>) -> Option<f64>` is unit-testable in isolation.\n\nNew `execution.schedule` knob selecting the policy:\n\n```yaml\nexecution:\n  max_concurrent: 8\n  schedule: lpt          # new; default \"declared\" (current behavior)\nmatrix:\n  - id: contact\n    weight: 483000       # optional explicit hint; else auto (estimated_rows) or none\n    ...\n```\n\nExecutor change (`cli/src/executor.rs`, `run_expanded`): at each level, when `schedule == lpt`, **stable-sort the *ready* roots by weight descending before spawning/acquiring permits** (ties fall back to declaration order, so dispatch stays deterministic). Everything else is unchanged — one shared `Semaphore`, same `JoinSet`, same `on_error` cancellation. Only the order in which ready siblings queue for permits changes.\n\n## Affected crates / files\n\n- `cli/src/config.rs` — `ExecutionSpec.schedule: DispatchOrder` (`Declared` default / `Lpt`), `MatrixRow.weight: Option<f64>`.\n- `cli/src/executor.rs` — pure `order_ready(ready, weights, policy) -> Vec<String>` used to sort the ready set before permit acquisition.\n- `cli/src/expand.rs` — thread `weight` onto `ExpandedNode`; default it from `estimated_rows` when the source supports discover and no explicit weight is given.\n- `docs/book/src/reference/config.md` (`execution.schedule`, per-row `weight`) + a cookbook note on scheduling many objects; `faucet schema` for the execution block.\n\n## Edge cases & failure modes\n\n- Ties / all-missing weights → identical to `declared` (deterministic; dispatch order is never nondeterministic).\n- LPT applies **only to ready sibling roots competing for permits** — children and `depends_on` edges keep their existing completion-gated ordering.\n- Auto-estimate must be **metadata-only and non-blocking**: if discovery is unavailable/slow/errors, silently fall back to declared rather than delaying dispatch.\n- Server-side-queue-dominated sources (e.g. Salesforce Bulk): duration can be dominated by org-side job queue time, so `estimated_rows` is a *proxy*, not exact — LPT still can't hurt, but the doc should say the win depends on duration correlating with weight.\n- `on_error: stop` cooperative-cancel + `STOP_FLUSH_GRACE` semantics unchanged.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"17394ff5-eae7-48b4-81e4-058a7214611b","session_id":"55398c64-7c65-40db-9b68-886111a86d7f","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/c-sikawats/Documents/GitHub/Personal/faucet-stream","sessionId":"55398c64-7c65-40db-9b68-886111a86d7f","version":"2.1.267","gitBranch":"fix/audit-batch-580","slug":"linear-noodling-leaf"}
