{"type":"pr-link","sessionId":"55398c64-7c65-40db-9b68-886111a86d7f","prNumber":665,"prUrl":"https://github.com/faucet-hq/faucet-stream/pull/665","prRepository":"faucet-hq/faucet-stream","timestamp":"2026-09-19T12:02:39.295Z"}
{"parentUuid":"ea45f7e9-8a2c-4bf7-a779-41f9b124ca90","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CfCmHNqdzVHCNhDTAJGr7","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01ADsCczixw2gR5mxHsVSNHT","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /Users/c-sikawats/Documents/GitHub/Personal/faucet-stream\ncat > /tmp/pr_audit.md <<'BODY'\nAn audit of every issue from #580 onwards, plus the fixes for the ones that\nturned out to be real. The audit half is the bigger half: several issues were\nalready implemented and several of my own first answers were wrong, so each\nverdict below is backed by code, tests, or a measured differential.\n\n## Fixed here\n\n### #666 — `database is locked` on concurrent `template_register`\n\nMy own filed diagnosis was wrong twice, and the SQLite error codes corrected it:\n\n1. \"Add a busy timeout\" — WAL and a 5s `busy_timeout` were **already** set.\n2. \"The un-retried `tx.commit()` is the culprit\" — retrying it changed nothing;\n   8/8 runs still failed at 64 writers.\n\nThe codes said why: **517 = `SQLITE_BUSY_SNAPSHOT`**. sqlx's `begin()` issues a\n*deferred* `BEGIN`, so the read-max-then-insert transaction starts as a reader\nand must upgrade. In WAL mode SQLite refuses that upgrade **immediately** when\nanother writer committed in between — it does not consult `busy_timeout`,\nbecause waiting could deadlock. No timeout and no retry budget can fix a\nconflict the engine declines to wait on.\n\n`BEGIN IMMEDIATE` takes the write lock up front, so contention becomes a wait\nthe busy timeout absorbs. `impl_sql_history!` now takes the statement as a\nparameter: SQLite passes `BEGIN IMMEDIATE`, Postgres passes `BEGIN` (unchanged).\n\n**Measured, 64 concurrent registers: 5/5 runs failed before, 0/10 after.**\n\n### #658 — topology runs lost the circuit-breaker cooldown\n\n`NodeReport.error` was a rendered `String`, so a topology node that tripped the\nbreaker got no scheduler cooldown while the identical matrix failure did — a\ncron tick re-fired straight at a destination that had just tripped. Adds a typed\n`NodeErrorKind` classified where the `FaucetError` is still intact, threaded to\n`InvocationErrorKind`; the scheduler needed no change.\n\nThe pre-existing test asserting the kind was *unset* said in its own comment\nthat it should be what changes when this lands, so it now asserts the half that\nsurvives: classification comes from the typed kind, **never** from what the\nmessage says.\n\n### #644 — cost-aware (LPT) dispatch order\n\n`execution.schedule: lpt` dispatches the heaviest ready rows first (`declared`\nstays the default). Ranking is by a per-row `weight` that `faucet discover`\nfills in as **rows × row width** — bytes, not row count, because a 970k-row ×\n3-column table is lighter than a 122k-row × 100-column one, which is exactly\nthe mis-ordering that left 6 of 8 slots idle for ~9 minutes in the reported run.\n\nTies and unweighted rows keep declaration order, so dispatch stays predictable;\na NaN weight is ordered with `total_cmp` rather than leaving the comparator\ninconsistent.\n\n### #634 — JSON structured logging\n\n`--log-format json` / `FAUCET_LOG_FORMAT` at all four subscriber sites. Beyond\nswapping the formatter, two things had to be right:\n\n- **Every stderr line must be one object.** The end-of-run status block, per-row\n  timing table and peak-RSS line are `eprintln!`s *between* log records; under\n  `json` they become structured events carrying the same numbers. The test\n  parses each line rather than grepping for braces, and caught two prose lines I\n  had not anticipated.\n- **`faucet mcp` keeps logs on stderr** under either format — stdout carries\n  JSON-RPC.\n\nAlso corrects a stale rustdoc on BigQuery's `media_load` (it claimed append was\nunaffected; the append path reads it).\n\n## Closed as already implemented (verified, not assumed)\n\n| Issue | Evidence |\n|---|---|\n| **#614** BigQuery streaming bounded-memory load | Resumable session held across pages, `WRITE_TRUNCATE` / `WRITE_APPEND`, finalized in `flush()`; `load_native_streaming_payload_feeds_session_chunk_by_chunk` + 4 more |\n| **#632** `keys_case` collision disambiguation | `KeyCollision::{Error,Suffix}`, default stays fail-loud, wired through the CLI, 5 tests incl. order-stability |\n| **#637** three-tier button affordance | `btn-warn` on Purge/Cancel/Discard/Deprecate, `btn-danger` on every Delete, nav untouched |\n| **#628** adaptive poll backoff | `next_poll_delay` doubling to an `interval_secs` cap, wired at `stream.rs:1494`, unit + wiremock tests |\n| **#615, #625** | Duplicates of **#626** (byte-identical titles, same 3.96 GB evidence) — consolidated |\n\n## Left open, with findings posted\n\n- **#612** (BigQuery load job) — the capability landed but `media_load` defaults\n  to `false`, so the reported symptom still reproduces out of the box. Making it\n  the default is a real behaviour change (load jobs have different quota and\n  latency characteristics from `insertAll`), so that is a call for you, not a\n  silent flip.\n- **#624** — the `partition_concurrency` third is done; CSV/Excel whole-file\n  buffering is not, and overlaps #626.\n\n## Not attempted, and why\n\nI am not going to pretend these fit in one PR: **#583** is a white-paper\n*writing* programme, **#589** and **#591** are explicitly RFC-gated, **#604** is\n4 formats × 8 connectors, **#580** is auto-create across ~10 sinks, and\n**#635**/**#636** are Arrow-native re-architectures. Also untouched: #608, #610,\n#617, #618, #621, #626, #629, #638, #646, #648, #654 (partial from #656).\nHappy to take them in priority order — #626 (tier-1, the 3.96 GB REST CSV\nceiling) is the one I would pick next.\n\nCloses #666\nCloses #658\nCloses #644\nCloses #634\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\nBODY\n/opt/homebrew/bin/gh pr create --repo faucet-hq/faucet-stream --head fix/audit-batch-580 --base main \\\n --title \"fix: SQLite write-lock contention, topology cooldown, LPT dispatch, JSON logs (issue audit #580+)\" \\\n --body-file /tmp/pr_audit.md 2>&1 | tail -2","description":"Open the PR for the audit batch","timeout":300000},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":455,"cache_read_input_tokens":858415,"output_tokens":2411,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":455,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":2411,"cache_read_input_tokens":858415,"cache_creation_input_tokens":455,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":455},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01ADsCczixw2gR5mxHsVSNHT":{"command":"cd /Users/c-sikawats/Documents/GitHub/Personal/faucet-stream\ncat > /tmp/pr_audit.md <<'BODY'\nAn audit of every issue from #580 onwards, plus the fixes for the ones that\nturned out to be real. The audit half is the bigger half: several issues were\nalready implemented and several of my own first answers were wrong, so each\nverdict below is backed by code, tests, or a measured differential.\n\n## Fixed here\n\n### #666 — `database is locked` on concurrent `template_register`\n\nMy own filed diagnosis was wrong twice, and the SQLite error codes corrected it:\n\n1. \"Add a busy timeout\" — WAL and a 5s `busy_timeout` were **already** set.\n2. \"The un-retried `tx.commit()` is the culprit\" — retrying it changed nothing;\n   8/8 runs still failed at 64 writers.\n\nThe codes said why: **517 = `SQLITE_BUSY_SNAPSHOT`**. sqlx's `begin()` issues a\n*deferred* `BEGIN`, so the read-max-then-insert transaction starts as a reader\nand must upgrade. In WAL mode SQLite refuses that upgrade **immediately** when\nanother writer committed in between — it does not consult `busy_timeout`,\nbecause waiting could deadlock. No timeout and no retry budget can fix a\nconflict the engine declines to wait on.\n\n`BEGIN IMMEDIATE` takes the write lock up front, so contention becomes a wait\nthe busy timeout absorbs. `impl_sql_history!` now takes the statement as a\nparameter: SQLite passes `BEGIN IMMEDIATE`, Postgres passes `BEGIN` (unchanged).\n\n**Measured, 64 concurrent registers: 5/5 runs failed before, 0/10 after.**\n\n### #658 — topology runs lost the circuit-breaker cooldown\n\n`NodeReport.error` was a rendered `String`, so a topology node that tripped the\nbreaker got no scheduler cooldown while the identical matrix failure did — a\ncron tick re-fired straight at a destination that had just tripped. Adds a typed\n`NodeErrorKind` classified where the `FaucetError` is still intact, threaded to\n`InvocationErrorKind`; the scheduler needed no change.\n\nThe pre-existing test asserting the kind was *unset* said in its own comment\nthat it should be what changes when this lands, so it now asserts the half that\nsurvives: classification comes from the typed kind, **never** from what the\nmessage says.\n\n### #644 — cost-aware (LPT) dispatch order\n\n`execution.schedule: lpt` dispatches the heaviest ready rows first (`declared`\nstays the default). Ranking is by a per-row `weight` that `faucet discover`\nfills in as **rows × row width** — bytes, not row count, because a 970k-row ×\n3-column table is lighter than a 122k-row × 100-column one, which is exactly\nthe mis-ordering that left 6 of 8 slots idle for ~9 minutes in the reported run.\n\nTies and unweighted rows keep declaration order, so dispatch stays predictable;\na NaN weight is ordered with `total_cmp` rather than leaving the comparator\ninconsistent.\n\n### #634 — JSON structured logging\n\n`--log-format json` / `FAUCET_LOG_FORMAT` at all four subscriber sites. Beyond\nswapping the formatter, two things had to be right:\n\n- **Every stderr line must be one object.** The end-of-run status block, per-row\n  timing table and peak-RSS line are `eprintln!`s *between* log records; under\n  `json` they become structured events carrying the same numbers. The test\n  parses each line rather than grepping for braces, and caught two prose lines I\n  had not anticipated.\n- **`faucet mcp` keeps logs on stderr** under either format — stdout carries\n  JSON-RPC.\n\nAlso corrects a stale rustdoc on BigQuery's `media_load` (it claimed append was\nunaffected; the append path reads it).\n\n## Closed as already implemented (verified, not assumed)\n\n| Issue | Evidence |\n|---|---|\n| **#614** BigQuery streaming bounded-memory load | Resumable session held across pages, `WRITE_TRUNCATE` / `WRITE_APPEND`, finalized in `flush()`; `load_native_streaming_payload_feeds_session_chunk_by_chunk` + 4 more |\n| **#632** `keys_case` collision disambiguation | `KeyCollision::{Error,Suffix}`, default stays fail-loud, wired through the CLI, 5 tests incl. order-stability |\n| **#637** three-tier button affordance | `btn-warn` on Purge/Cancel/Discard/Deprecate, `btn-danger` on every Delete, nav untouched |\n| **#628** adaptive poll backoff | `next_poll_delay` doubling to an `interval_secs` cap, wired at `stream.rs:1494`, unit + wiremock tests |\n| **#615, #625** | Duplicates of **#626** (byte-identical titles, same 3.96 GB evidence) — consolidated |\n\n## Left open, with findings posted\n\n- **#612** (BigQuery load job) — the capability landed but `media_load` defaults\n  to `false`, so the reported symptom still reproduces out of the box. Making it\n  the default is a real behaviour change (load jobs have different quota and\n  latency characteristics from `insertAll`), so that is a call for you, not a\n  silent flip.\n- **#624** — the `partition_concurrency` third is done; CSV/Excel whole-file\n  buffering is not, and overlaps #626.\n\n## Not attempted, and why\n\nI am not going to pretend these fit in one PR: **#583** is a white-paper\n*writing* programme, **#589** and **#591** are explicitly RFC-gated, **#604** is\n4 formats × 8 connectors, **#580** is auto-create across ~10 sinks, and\n**#635**/**#636** are Arrow-native re-architectures. Also untouched: #608, #610,\n#617, #618, #621, #626, #629, #638, #646, #648, #654 (partial from #656).\nHappy to take them in priority order — #626 (tier-1, the 3.96 GB REST CSV\nceiling) is the one I would pick next.\n\nCloses #666\nCloses #658\nCloses #644\nCloses #634\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\nBODY\n/opt/homebrew/bin/gh pr create --repo faucet-hq/faucet-stream --head fix/audit-batch-580 --base main \\\n --title \"fix: SQLite write-lock contention, topology cooldown, LPT dispatch, JSON logs (issue audit #580+)\" \\\n --body-file /tmp/pr_audit.md 2>&1 | tail -2","timeout":300000,"description":"Open the PR for the audit batch"}},"apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011CfCmHMdxHMAn9Y1WWVzc4","type":"assistant","uuid":"341085c5-9bfd-4cdc-b44a-5c10c042d09c","timestamp":"2026-09-19T12:03:09.168Z","effort":"high","perTurnEffort":null,"session_id":"55398c64-7c65-40db-9b68-886111a86d7f","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/c-sikawats/Documents/GitHub/Personal/faucet-stream","sessionId":"55398c64-7c65-40db-9b68-886111a86d7f","version":"2.1.267","gitBranch":"fix/audit-batch-580","slug":"linear-noodling-leaf"}
